window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jepas-2020"] = {"id": "jepas-2020", "title": "JEPAS(PG) 2020", "fullTitle": "JEPAS(PG) 2020, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance, West Bengal (real paper, Set A)", "sub": "100 real questions · answers from a marked copy, every one checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics", "aptitude": "Aptitude, Teaching and Communication"}, "questions": [{"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the", "options": {"a": "somatic nervous system.", "b": "central nervous system.", "c": "autonomic nervous system.", "d": "cerebral cortex."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the body's involuntary organs: heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut, sweat glands, pupils and the adrenal glands. It works without conscious intention and has two branches. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action; Walter Bradford Cannon (1915) called this the fight-or-flight response. Heart rate and blood pressure rise, breathing quickens, pupils widen, digestion stops and the adrenal medulla pours adrenaline into the blood. The parasympathetic branch, working largely through the vagus nerve, does the opposite (rest and digest) and calms the body once the danger has passed. Commands reach the ANS from the hypothalamus, which receives alarm signals from the amygdala. These brain areas are the controllers higher up, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) even argued that the feeling of an emotion is our perception of these autonomic changes. Example: Kabir survives a bus crash. His heart pounds, his palms sweat, his mouth goes dry and his breathing races; weeks later a screeching horn sets off the same surge. Each sign is sympathetic arousal, and the lasting hyperarousal of post-traumatic stress disorder is largely autonomic. Option c is right because the ANS is the direct effector of the visceral, involuntary reactions that make up an emotional response to trauma. Exam trap: \"most directly\" points to the system that carries out the response, not the higher centres. The CNS and the cortex appraise the event and send commands, but they act on the organs through the ANS; the somatic system moves skeletal muscles under voluntary control.", "topic": "Autonomic nervous system and emotion"}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence.", "options": {"a": "fluid; fixed.", "b": "fixed; fluid.", "c": "fluid; crystallized.", "d": "crystallized; fluid."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Bernard Cattell (1963), later with John Leonard Horn (1966), split the general factor g of Charles Spearman (1904) into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on what you already know: seeing patterns, working out rules, holding and juggling information. It is tested by tasks such as Raven's Progressive Matrices. It leans heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then declines steadily. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary, skills and strategies built up through education and experience. It is tested by vocabulary, general information and comprehension items, and it holds steady or keeps rising into the sixties and beyond. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability invested in learning becomes crystallised ability, so the two are related but age differently. The Seattle Longitudinal Study of K. Warner Schaie (begun 1956), which followed the same adults for decades, confirmed that perceptual speed and fluid reasoning fall earliest, while verbal ability is kept well into late adulthood. Example: Meera's grandmother, aged 72, is slow with a new mobile banking app and struggles with timed puzzles, but she beats everyone at word games and gives wise advice on family disputes. Her fluid ability has declined; her crystallised ability has not. Option c is right because it places the decline on fluid intelligence and the stability on crystallised intelligence, which is the classic Horn and Cattell finding. Exam trap: the reversal in d is the commonest slip, and \"fixed\" in a and b is not a type of intelligence at all; it borrows from the fixed mindset of Carol Dweck (2006).", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence in ageing"}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "options": {"a": "Block design.", "b": "mazes.", "c": "non-verbal cues.", "d": "drawing people."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "topic": "Porteus Maze Test"}, {"q": "The function of mass communication of supplying information regarding the processes, issues, events and societal developments is known as", "options": {"a": "Content supply.", "b": "Surveillance.", "c": "Gratification.", "d": "Correlation."}, "correct": "b", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is b: supplying information about the processes, issues, events and developments in society is the surveillance function of mass communication. Mass communication is the sending of messages to large, scattered audiences through media such as newspapers, radio, television and the internet. Harold Lasswell (1948), in his essay on the structure and function of communication in society, named three social functions. Surveillance of the environment means watching the world and reporting what is happening: news, weather warnings, election results, price rises, the outbreak of a disease. Correlation means interpreting that information and linking the parts of society in their response: editorials, analysis and commentary that tell people what an event means and what to do about it. Transmission of the social heritage means passing values and culture on to new members of society, which overlaps with socialisation. Charles Wright (1959) later added a fourth function, entertainment. Example: when Riya reads that a cyclone will reach the Odisha coast tomorrow, the media are performing surveillance; when an editorial argues that the state's evacuation plan is too slow, that is correlation. Option b is right because the stem describes the supply of information about events and developments, which is exactly Lasswell's surveillance function. Exam trap: correlation sounds like \"connecting information\", but it means interpreting the news and shaping society's response, not supplying the facts themselves.", "topic": "Functions of mass communication"}, {"q": "A young child learns that Daddy has two legs, spectacles, and is a bit balding. One day on the street, while passing a balding man, the child says \"Daddy\". This is a case of", "options": {"a": "Using an algorithm.", "b": "Using a semantic logarithm.", "c": "Extending a morpheme.", "d": "Overgeneralizing."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: calling a balding stranger \"Daddy\" is a case of overgeneralising, which in language development is called overextension. When children learn a new word, they do not yet know its full adult meaning. Eve Clark (1973), in her semantic feature hypothesis, proposed that a young child stores a word with only a few striking perceptual features. Here the child's meaning of \"Daddy\" is roughly \"grown-up man, spectacles, bald\". Anyone who shares those features gets the label, so the word is applied too widely. Overextension is common between about 1 and 2½ years, while vocabulary is small: \"dog\" for every four-legged animal, \"moon\" for any round light, \"ball\" for an orange. Its opposite is underextension, using a word too narrowly (\"cat\" only for the family cat). A related grammatical error is overregularisation, applying a rule such as adding \"-ed\" to irregular verbs (\"I goed\", \"two foots\"). Both errors show active learning, not imitation, because adults rarely say them. Example: Arjun, aged 18 months, learns \"Appa\" for his father and then greets the postman, a neighbour and an uncle with \"Appa\"; as words such as \"uncle\" and \"man\" arrive, the label narrows to his father. Option d is right because the child has taken a word learned for one person and stretched it to others who share some of his features, which is overgeneralisation of a word's meaning. Exam trap: \"extending a morpheme\" sounds close, but over-applying a grammatical morpheme is overregularisation (\"goed\"); the Daddy error concerns word meaning, not grammar.", "topic": "Overextension in early language"}, {"q": "In how many different ways can the letters of the word 'LEADING' be arranged in such a way that the vowels always come together?", "options": {"a": "360.", "b": "480.", "c": "720.", "d": "5040."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: the letters of LEADING can be arranged in 720 ways with the vowels always together. Step 1: list the letters. LEADING has 7 letters, all different: L, E, A, D, I, N, G. The vowels are E, A and I (3 vowels); the consonants are L, D, N and G (4 consonants). Step 2: glue the vowels into one block. If the vowels must always come together, treat (EAI) as a single unit. Now there are 4 consonants plus 1 vowel block, which makes 5 units. Step 3: arrange the 5 units. Five different units can be arranged in 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 ways. Step 4: arrange the vowels inside the block. The three vowels can be ordered among themselves in 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 ways (EAI, EIA, AEI, AIE, IEA, IAE). Step 5: multiply, because every order of the units can be combined with every order inside the block: 120 × 6 = 720. Check: LDAIENG is one valid arrangement, because A, I and E sit side by side. Option c is right because 5! × 3! = 720. Exam trap: 5040 is 7!, the count with no restriction at all; forgetting step 4 gives only 120; and halving 720 to 360 happens when a solver wrongly divides as if a letter were repeated, which none is in LEADING.", "topic": "Permutations with letters together"}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as", "options": {"a": "Transposition.", "b": "Transaction.", "c": "Transduction.", "d": "Transition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "topic": "Sensory transduction"}, {"q": "A student is trying to solve a problem without the help of his teacher. The teacher must", "options": {"a": "Advise his student to seek assistance from his friends.", "b": "Be attentive towards his student.", "c": "Rebuke his student for his foolish act.", "d": "Appreciate his personal efforts."}, "correct": "d", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is d: the teacher should appreciate the student's personal efforts. Teaching aptitude items test whether you know what good teaching looks like in practice. When a student tries to solve a problem alone, he is showing initiative, self-reliance and intrinsic motivation, the very qualities education tries to grow. Praising the effort reinforces that behaviour, so it becomes more likely next time; this is the basic principle of positive reinforcement described by B. F. Skinner (1953). Research on mindsets by Carol Dweck (2006) adds that praise for effort and strategy, rather than for being clever, helps students believe that ability grows with work and keeps them going when problems get hard. Appreciation does not mean leaving the student alone. The teacher can stay available and offer a hint if he gets stuck, the kind of support that Lev Vygotsky (1978) placed in the zone of proximal development and that David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) called scaffolding. But the first response to independent effort should be recognition. Example: Kabir, in Class 9, works through a geometry proof without asking for help. His teacher says, \"Good, you tried a new route on your own; show me how you got there,\" and Kabir is keen to attempt the next one. Option d is right because appreciating independent effort encourages autonomy and motivation, which is what teaching aims at. Exam trap: \"be attentive\" (b) sounds caring and safe, but it is passive; in these items the best answer is the one that actively encourages the learner.", "topic": "Encouraging independent learning"}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "options": {"a": "Trait; humanistic.", "b": "Psychoanalytic; behaviorist.", "c": "Psychoanalytic; humanistic.", "d": "Trait; behaviorist."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised, and behaviourist theory minimised, the role of the unconscious. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams; 1915, \"The Unconscious\") put the unconscious at the centre of personality. He pictured the mind like an iceberg: the conscious mind is the tip, the preconscious holds what can easily be recalled, and the vast unconscious holds instincts, wishes and memories pushed out of awareness by repression. These hidden forces were said to drive dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices, which is why his therapy used free association and dream analysis to uncover them. Behaviourism went to the opposite pole. John Broadus Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour and set aside consciousness and the unconscious alike as unscientific. B. F. Skinner (1953) explained behaviour by a person's history of reinforcement; an \"unconscious\" motive was, for him, at most behaviour whose causes the person cannot describe. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also gave the unconscious little weight, stressing conscious experience and choice, but they sit between the two poles. Example: Meera bites her nails before exams. A Freudian looks for an unconscious oral conflict; a behaviourist looks for the relief from anxiety that reinforces the habit, with no reference to hidden motives. Option b is right because it pairs the school that made the unconscious central with the school that set it aside entirely. Exam trap: humanistic theory in c also played down the unconscious, so c looks right; but behaviourism minimised it most, and the blanks ask for the two extremes.", "topic": "The unconscious across theories"}, {"q": "Which is not a clinical subscale of MMPI?", "options": {"a": "Masculinity-femininity.", "b": "Psychopathic deviate.", "c": "Social Introversion.", "d": "Schizotypal."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: schizotypal is not a clinical scale of the MMPI. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) using empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale if it separated a diagnosed patient group from normal people, whatever its content. The result was ten clinical scales, numbered 1 to 0: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). Scales 5 and 0 measure interests and social style rather than illness, but they are still counted among the ten clinical scales. Raw scores become T scores with a mean of 50 and SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 (1989) a T of 65 or more is elevated, and clinicians read the two highest scales as a code type. So options a, b and c are all genuine clinical scales: Masculinity-femininity is scale 5, Psychopathic deviate scale 4 and Social introversion scale 0. Schizotypal is a DSM personality disorder in Cluster A (odd or eccentric), marked by odd beliefs, eccentric behaviour and social anxiety; it is not one of the ten MMPI clinical scales. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 profile shows T 72 on scale 4 and T 68 on scale 9; his clinician reads a 4-9 code type, suggesting impulsive acting out, and there is no \"schizotypal\" score to read. Option d is right because schizotypal is a diagnostic category, not an MMPI clinical scale. Exam trap: scale 8 is Schizophrenia, and the shared prefix makes \"schizotypal\" look familiar. A schizotypal scale does exist, but on the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977), not the MMPI.", "topic": "MMPI clinical scales"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "options": {"a": "CBT and sertraline are not significantly different in terms of efficacy.", "b": "CBT improves outcome in around 60% of cases.", "c": "CBT and sertraline combined produce the best outcome.", "d": "All of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the main findings on treating childhood anxiety, so \"all of the above\" is correct. The strongest evidence comes from the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS; John Walkup and colleagues, 2008, New England Journal of Medicine). It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) alone, the SSRI sertraline alone, the two combined, or a pill placebo. The CBT was 14 sessions adapted from the Coping Cat programme of Philip Kendall (1990): recognising anxious feelings and thoughts, coping self-talk, problem solving and, above all, graded exposure to feared situations with rewards for brave behaviour. Response meant being rated \"much\" or \"very much\" improved by evaluators who did not know the treatment. The response rates were 80.7% for the combination, 59.7% for CBT alone, 54.9% for sertraline alone and 23.7% for placebo. The combination beat each single treatment, and each single treatment beat placebo, but CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other. So statement a (CBT and sertraline similar) is true, statement b (CBT helps about 60%) matches 59.7%, and statement c (combination best) is true. Example: Riya, 10, refuses to sleep away from her mother and gets stomach aches before school; a clinician following CAMS might start CBT with exposure and add sertraline if her symptoms are severe or do not improve. Option d is right because a, b and c are each supported by CAMS. Exam trap: c is the headline result and tempts you to stop reading, but in an \"all of the above\" item every option must be tested. Key note: the marked copy of this paper has c; c is true but so are a and b, so d stands.", "topic": "Childhood anxiety treatment (CAMS)"}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of", "options": {"a": "delinquency as a factor affecting the development of intelligence.", "b": "mental age versus chronological age.", "c": "a z-score.", "d": "verbal versus performance scores."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the notion of a z-score. A z-score tells you how far a raw score lies from the mean of a group, in standard deviation (SD) units: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The deviation IQ applies this idea to intelligence. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, stopped using mental age and instead compared each person with a large, representative sample of people the same age. The raw score is turned into a z-score within the person's age group and then rescaled so that every age group has a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. Because IQs are close to normally distributed, an IQ also gives a percentile. An IQ of 100 (z = 0) is the 50th percentile; 115 (z = +1) is about the 84th; 85 (z = minus 1) is about the 16th; 130 (z = +2) is about the 98th. The Stanford-Binet used an SD of 16 for its deviation IQ until the fifth edition (2003) moved to 15. This solved the problem of the ratio IQ, which divided mental age by chronological age. Mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a ratio IQ makes ordinary adults look impaired. Example: Arjun, aged 35, scores 2 SDs above the mean for adults his age on the WAIS-IV. His z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 15 × 2 = 130. Option c is right because a deviation IQ is simply a z-score rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. Exam trap: \"mental age versus chronological age\" in b is the older ratio IQ of William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), which the deviation IQ replaced.", "topic": "Deviation IQ and z-scores"}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because", "options": {"a": "each involves a crisis or dilemma.", "b": "each signals a new stage of cognitive development.", "c": "each is an expression of biological programming.", "d": "their failure to appear is evidence of psychopathology."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson, each life stage matters because each involves a crisis or dilemma. Erik Homburger Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. At each stage the growing person meets new social demands, and these set up a psychosocial crisis, a turning point between two opposite tendencies: trust versus mistrust in infancy, autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority at school age, identity versus role confusion in adolescence, intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation in middle age, and integrity versus despair in old age. \"Crisis\" here does not mean disaster; it means a decisive period of heightened vulnerability and potential. A favourable balance yields a basic strength or virtue: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care and wisdom. An unfavourable balance leaves a weakness that later experience may still repair. The stages follow the epigenetic principle, a built-in order, but society and relationships decide how each crisis turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician, and argues with his parents over each choice. Erikson would see the crisis of identity versus role confusion; working it through, with some freedom to explore, produces fidelity, a stable commitment to values and goals. Option a is right because the crisis, and the way it is resolved, is the core of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: the epigenetic principle sounds like biological programming (c), but Erikson stressed the psychosocial resolution of each crisis, not maturation as such.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial crises"}, {"q": "Match each non parametric test with the data it is suited for. A. Kruskal-Wallis test, B. Mann Whitney, C. Wilcoxon signed ranks, D. Friedman. i) Non-parametric alternative to the one-way ANOVA with repeated measures, ii) Repeated measures test of dependency, iii) One-way ANOVA, unequal sample size, iv) Compare differences between two independent groups", "options": {"a": "A(iii), B(iv), C(ii), D(i).", "b": "A(iv), B(i), C(iii), D(ii).", "c": "A(ii), B(iii), C(i), D(iv).", "d": "A(i), B(ii), C(iii), D(iv)."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: Kruskal-Wallis matches iii, Mann-Whitney iv, Wilcoxon signed ranks ii and Friedman i. Non-parametric tests are used when data are ranks (ordinal) or when interval data break the assumptions of parametric tests, such as normality, especially in small samples. Most convert scores to ranks and compare rank totals. To match them, ask two questions: how many groups or conditions are there, and are they independent (different people) or related (the same people measured more than once)? The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares two independent groups; it is the non-parametric twin of the independent-samples t-test (iv). The Wilcoxon signed-ranks test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) compares two related measurements, such as before and after scores from the same people; it ranks each pair's difference and keeps the sign, making it the twin of the paired t-test, a repeated-measures test for dependent data (ii). The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) compares three or more independent groups, which may be of unequal size; it is the one-way ANOVA by ranks (iii). The Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937) compares three or more related conditions by ranking within each person; it replaces the one-way repeated-measures ANOVA (i). Example: Meera rates the anxiety of patients in three wards of 12, 15 and 9 people; unequal independent groups with ordinal ratings call for Kruskal-Wallis. If she rated the same 12 patients in three successive weeks, she would use Friedman. Option a is right because it is the only code that pairs each test with its design. Exam trap: Wilcoxon has two tests; the signed-rank test is for related pairs, but the Wilcoxon rank-sum test is equivalent to Mann-Whitney for independent groups.", "topic": "Choosing non-parametric tests"}, {"q": "What do you consider as the main aim of interdisciplinary research?", "options": {"a": "To bring out holistic approach to research.", "b": "To reduce the emphasis of single subject in research domain.", "c": "To over simplify the problem of research.", "d": "To create a new trend in research methodology."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the main aim of interdisciplinary research is to bring a holistic approach to a research problem. Interdisciplinary research takes the concepts, theories, methods and data of two or more disciplines and integrates them into one shared understanding. The US National Academies (2004) described it as research that integrates information, techniques, perspectives and theories from two or more disciplines to advance understanding or to solve problems beyond the reach of a single discipline. It differs from multidisciplinary work, in which experts from different fields work side by side but each keeps to their own framework and the findings are simply added together, and from transdisciplinary work, which goes further and builds a new framework that cuts across disciplines, often with community partners. The reason for integration is that real problems do not respect subject boundaries. Clinical psychology shows this well: the biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) argued that illness can only be understood by combining biological, psychological and social levels, which no single discipline covers. Example: Kabir studies farmer suicides in Vidarbha. Psychology alone might look at depression and hopelessness; working with economists, agricultural scientists and public health researchers, he can see how debt, crop failure, easy access to pesticides and stigma interact, and design a programme that addresses all of them. Option a is right because the purpose of integrating disciplines is to see the whole problem, not one slice of it. Exam trap: b describes a side effect, not the aim; the weight of any single subject does fall when fields combine, but no one does interdisciplinary research in order to weaken a discipline.", "topic": "Interdisciplinary research"}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called", "options": {"a": "morphemes.", "b": "syllables.", "c": "phonemes.", "d": "syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. In English, /p/ and /b/ are separate phonemes because \"pat\" and \"bat\" are different words; so are /k/ and /g/ in \"cot\" and \"got\". English has about 40 to 44 phonemes, depending on accent. Language is built in layers. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: \"cat\" is one free morpheme, and \"cats\" has two, because the bound morpheme \"-s\" adds the plural. Morphemes form words, and syntax is the set of rules for combining words into phrases and sentences. Semantics deals with meaning, and pragmatics with use in social context. Charles Hockett (1960) called this layering duality of patterning: a small set of meaningless sounds builds a vast number of meaningful units. Infants can at first tell apart the phonemes of all languages, but by about 10 to 12 months they lose sensitivity to contrasts their own language does not use, as Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) showed. Example: Riya, learning Hindi, must hear the difference between the unaspirated \"k\" of \"kal\" (yesterday) and the aspirated \"kh\", said with a puff of air, of \"khaal\" (skin). In Hindi these are separate phonemes; in English the same two sounds never distinguish words. Option c is right because phonemes are the basic sound units of speech. Exam trap: phoneme (sound) and morpheme (meaning) are paired in almost every exam; remember that \"phone\" means sound, as in telephone.", "topic": "Phonemes and units of language"}, {"q": "Which of the following types of acceptance is most critical and pervasive to the long-range emotional health of a child?", "options": {"a": "Peer.", "b": "Sibling.", "c": "Self.", "d": "Vocational."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: self-acceptance is the most critical and pervasive form of acceptance for a child's long-range emotional health. Self-acceptance means recognising and accepting one's own qualities, strengths and limitations alike, without harsh self-rejection. It is not complacency or believing oneself perfect. It grows out of acceptance by others. Carl Rogers (1959) argued that when parents give unconditional positive regard, the child develops unconditional positive self-regard; when love depends on meeting conditions of worth, the child learns to accept only parts of the self and to deny experiences that do not fit, which leads to anxiety and maladjustment. Elizabeth Hurlock (1974), whose texts Indian courses follow, treated self-acceptance as essential for good personal and social adjustment: a child who accepts himself relates more easily to others, copes better with failure and is less defensive. Carol Ryff (1989) later listed self-acceptance as one of six dimensions of psychological well-being in adults. Self-acceptance is \"pervasive\" because it goes with the child into every setting. Peer, sibling and later vocational acceptance each apply to one domain and change with the group, but the way the child regards herself colours how she reads all of them. Example: Riya is not chosen for the school dance team. If she accepts herself, she is disappointed but tries something else; if she rejects herself, the same event confirms \"I am useless\" and spreads to her studies and friendships. Option c is right because self-acceptance is the internal, lasting base on which the other kinds of acceptance rest. Exam trap: peer acceptance is studied heavily in childhood research and looks like the answer, but it is tied to particular groups and changes; the words \"most critical and pervasive\" point to the self.", "topic": "Self-acceptance in childhood"}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II and choose the correct answer from the code given below. List-I: A. Historical method, B. Survey method, C. Philosophical method, D. Experimental method. List-II: 1. Past events, 2. Vision, 3. Present events, 4. Exceptional cases, 5. Future action", "options": {"a": "A-1, B-3, C-2, D-5.", "b": "A-1, B-2, C-4, D-5.", "c": "A-1, B-4, C-2, D-5.", "d": "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: historical method goes with past events, survey method with present events, philosophical method with vision, and experimental method with future action. This matching follows John W. Best (1959), Research in Education, later written with James V. Kahn, which sorts research by its relation to time. Historical research describes what was. It studies past events through records, documents and remains, testing sources for genuineness (external criticism) and accuracy (internal criticism), to understand how the present came about. Descriptive research, of which the survey is the classic form, describes what is: current conditions, practices, attitudes and opinions, gathered by questionnaire, interview or observation. Experimental research describes what will be when certain variables are carefully controlled or manipulated. Because it tests cause and effect, it lets us predict and plan future action. Philosophical research, the reflective analysis of aims, values and concepts, gives education its vision, the picture of where it should be going. The fourth item in List-II, exceptional cases, is the distractor; intensive study of unusual individuals belongs to the case study, which is not in List-I. Example: Meera wants to improve counselling services in Kolkata colleges. Archives on how the counselling cells began are historical research; a questionnaire on how many students use them now is a survey; a controlled trial of a peer-support programme tells her what will happen if she introduces it; and reflection on what a caring college should be gives her the vision. Option a is right because it is the only code that pairs each method with its time frame and leaves exceptional cases unused. Exam trap: the survey is present-oriented, not visionary; codes b and d put the survey with vision, and code c pairs it with exceptional cases.", "topic": "Research methods and time frames"}, {"q": "REM sleep behavior is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterized as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "options": {"a": "Atonia.", "b": "Rapid eye movements.", "c": "High frequency, low amplitude EEG.", "d": "Penile erections in males."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "topic": "REM sleep behaviour disorder"}, {"q": "Which of the following neurological disorders is associated with a deficiency of acetylcholine?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's Disease.", "b": "Parkinson's Disease.", "c": "Wernicke's Aphasia.", "d": "Autism."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: Alzheimer's disease is the neurological disorder associated with a deficiency of acetylcholine (ACh). Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906) in his patient Auguste Deter, is the commonest cause of dementia. Its brain pathology includes beta-amyloid plaques outside neurons, neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein inside them, and neuron loss that begins in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus. Among the earliest and worst-hit cells are the cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain, especially the nucleus basalis of Meynert, which send ACh to the cortex and hippocampus. Peter Davies and A. J. F. Maloney (1976) found sharply reduced levels of choline acetyltransferase, the enzyme that makes ACh, in Alzheimer brains. Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982) then proposed the cholinergic hypothesis: much of the memory loss of ageing and Alzheimer's disease reflects this cholinergic failure. Supporting it, anticholinergic drugs such as scopolamine impair memory in healthy people, while drugs that raise ACh (cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil) give modest relief. ACh is central to attention, learning and memory, which fits the early symptoms. Example: Arjun's grandmother, 78, forgets conversations from the same morning and repeats her questions; her neurologist diagnoses Alzheimer's disease and starts donepezil to make the most of the ACh that remains. Option a is right because cholinergic loss is the classic neurotransmitter deficit of Alzheimer's disease. Exam trap: Parkinson's disease (b) is the classic dopamine deficiency; candidates who remember only that both are neurodegenerative diseases swap the two transmitters.", "topic": "Acetylcholine and Alzheimer's disease"}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Dopamine.", "b": "GABA.", "c": "Noradrenaline.", "d": "Acetylcholine."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine (ACh). The logic comes from the cholinergic hypothesis (Raymond Bartus and colleagues, 1982). Alzheimer's disease destroys cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain (the nucleus basalis of Meynert), leaving the cortex and hippocampus short of ACh, which supports attention and memory. The dying neurons cannot be replaced, so the drugs try to make the remaining ACh last longer. Normally, once ACh is released into the synapse, the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks it down within milliseconds. Cholinesterase inhibitors block this enzyme, so ACh stays in the synapse longer and stimulates its receptors more. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. The first, tacrine (1993), was withdrawn because it damaged the liver. These drugs give modest, temporary gains in memory and daily function; they do not stop the disease. The fourth standard drug, memantine, works differently: it blocks NMDA glutamate receptors to protect neurons from excess glutamate and is used in moderate to severe stages. Newer antibody drugs such as lecanemab (approved 2023) clear amyloid plaques rather than changing any transmitter. So three of the four long-standing medicines target ACh. Example: Meera's grandfather starts donepezil; over the next months he is a little more alert and follows conversations better, although his memory problems remain. Option d is right because the main class of Alzheimer medicines, the cholinesterase inhibitors, works by increasing acetylcholine. Exam trap: memantine acts on glutamate, which is not offered here; and do not let dopamine, the Parkinson's transmitter, tempt you just because both are neurodegenerative diseases.", "topic": "Cholinesterase inhibitors for Alzheimer's"}, {"q": "As one moves from external stimulus to motor response, which of the following constitutes an accurate sequential pattern?", "options": {"a": "Afferent nerve-interneuron-efferent nerve.", "b": "Efferent nerve-interneuron-afferent nerve.", "c": "Afferent nerve-efferent nerve-neural modulator.", "d": "Efferent nerve-afferent nerve-neural modulator."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the sequence from external stimulus to motor response runs afferent nerve, interneuron, efferent nerve. Neurons fall into three functional classes. Afferent (sensory) neurons carry signals from receptors toward the central nervous system; the word comes from Latin afferre, to carry toward. Interneurons (association or connector neurons), by far the commonest type, lie wholly inside the brain and spinal cord and link incoming to outgoing signals. Efferent (motor) neurons carry commands away from the CNS to effectors, the muscles and glands; efferre means to carry out. A memory aid: afferent arrives, efferent exits. Together these form the reflex arc, analysed in detail by Charles Scott Sherrington (1906) in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System. In the spinal cord, the Bell-Magendie law (Charles Bell, 1811; Francois Magendie, 1822) holds that sensory fibres enter through the dorsal root and motor fibres leave through the ventral root. Example: Meera touches a hot tawa. Heat receptors in her fingertip fire; afferent neurons carry the signal through the dorsal root into the spinal grey matter; an interneuron passes it on; efferent neurons leave through the ventral root and contract her arm muscles. She pulls away before she even feels the pain, because the arc closes in the spinal cord. This withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic; the knee-jerk is monosynaptic and skips the interneuron. Option a is right because the signal must first arrive (afferent), be relayed (interneuron) and then exit (efferent). Exam trap: options b and d reverse afferent and efferent, and \"neural modulator\" in c and d sounds technical but is a chemical messenger, not a link in the chain.", "topic": "Reflex arc sequence"}, {"q": "______ is a subprocess of ______", "options": {"a": "psychological testing; observation.", "b": "psychological testing; interviewing.", "c": "interviewing; psychological assessment.", "d": "psychological assessment; observation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: psychological testing is a subprocess of observation. Observation is the broadest way of gathering information about behaviour: watching, recording and describing what a person does, in natural or arranged settings. A psychological test is a special, tightly controlled form of observation. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour: everyone meets the same items, instructions, time limits and scoring rules, so the behaviour observed can be compared across people and against norms. Lee J. Cronbach, in later editions of Essentials of Psychological Testing (first published 1949), described a test as a systematic procedure for observing behaviour and describing it with a numerical scale or a category system. On this view, testing is observation made standard, systematic and quantified, and a test score is a record of behaviour observed under fixed conditions. Psychological assessment is wider still: it answers a referral question by combining tests, interviews, observation, case history and records into a reasoned conclusion. Example: Kabir, aged 8, is referred for attention problems. His psychologist watches him in class (naturalistic observation) and then gives a timed cancellation test in the clinic. The test is still observation, but now every child meets the same task under the same rules, so Kabir's speed and errors can be compared with norms. Option a is right because a test is a controlled, standardised sample of observed behaviour. Exam trap: assessment is the widest of these terms and testing the narrowest; any option that makes assessment part of something smaller, as d does, has the hierarchy upside down. Key note: in modern texts (Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik) testing and interviewing are both tools inside psychological assessment, so c has support; the key follows Lee Cronbach's definition of a test as a systematic procedure for observing behaviour, which makes testing a controlled form of observation. The key stands.", "topic": "Testing, observation and assessment"}, {"q": "The quality of teaching is reflected in", "options": {"a": "The attendance of students in the class.", "b": "The pass percentage of the students.", "c": "The quality of the questions asked by the students.", "d": "The period of silence maintained in the class."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: the quality of teaching is reflected in the quality of the questions students ask. A student can ask a good question only after thinking about the material: noticing a gap, linking it to what she already knows, or testing an idea against a new case. So the questions a class asks are a window into how deeply it has understood. In the taxonomy of Benjamin Bloom (1956), recall questions (\"What is the definition?\") sit at the lowest level, knowledge, while questions that apply, analyse or evaluate (\"Would this still hold if the sample were small?\") show higher-order thinking. A teacher who explains clearly, invites curiosity and makes it safe to ask produces students who ask questions of the second kind. Example: after a lesson on the reflex arc, one class asks only \"Will this come in the exam?\", while Meera's class asks why the knee-jerk reflex skips the interneuron and what would happen if a dorsal root were cut. The second set of questions shows that the teaching produced understanding, not just note-taking. Option c is right because thoughtful student questions are direct evidence of engaged, deep learning, which is what good teaching aims at. Exam trap: pass percentage (b) looks like an objective measure, but results can be driven by rote memorisation, coaching and lenient marking, so they reflect many things besides teaching quality.", "topic": "Indicators of teaching quality"}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to", "options": {"a": "Increase the amount of neurotransmitter released into a synapse.", "b": "Decrease the distance of the synaptic cleft.", "c": "Increase the rate of propagation of an action potential.", "d": "Increase the amplitude of an action potential."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "topic": "Myelin and conduction speed"}, {"q": "Grief refers to", "options": {"a": "the period of emotional adjustment that follows the death of a loved one.", "b": "the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend, or relative.", "c": "the period during which a person seems dazed or numb and shows little emotion.", "d": "demoralization and discouragement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often confused. Bereavement is the objective fact of having lost someone close, and in its wider sense the whole period of adjusting to that loss. Grief is the emotional response itself: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and sometimes relief, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep, loss of appetite and tightness in the chest. Mourning is the outward, social expression of grief, the rituals and customs a culture prescribes, such as the rites a Hindu family performs in the days after a death. Grief is usually most intense in the early weeks and months, then eases while the bond with the dead person is kept in memory. John Bowlby (1980) described four phases of mourning: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) replaced fixed stages with a dual process model, in which the bereaved person swings between loss-oriented coping (crying, remembering) and restoration-oriented coping (new roles, practical tasks). Most grief is normal; when intense yearning still disables an adult 12 months after the death, DSM-5-TR (2022) allows a diagnosis of prolonged grief disorder. Example: when Riya's grandmother dies, Riya feels waves of sadness and longing, cannot concentrate and cries at the smell of her grandmother's cooking. That raw emotional state is her grief; the funeral and the rites her family observes are mourning; the whole months-long adjustment is bereavement. Option b is right because it defines grief as an emotional state, intense and triggered by a death. Exam trap: option a sounds scholarly but describes a period of adjustment, which is bereavement or mourning, and option c is only the first, numb phase of mourning, not grief as a whole.", "topic": "Grief, bereavement and mourning"}, {"q": "Mutism, self-destructive behavior, and echolalia are symptoms of", "options": {"a": "childhood autism.", "b": "anorexia nervosa.", "c": "ADHD.", "d": "dyslexia."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: mutism, self-destructive behaviour and echolalia are symptoms of childhood autism. Autism was first described by Leo Kanner (1943) in eleven children who showed extreme aloneness from early life and an anxious insistence on sameness; Hans Asperger (1944) described verbally able boys with similar social difficulties. DSM-5 (2013) now uses one label, autism spectrum disorder, defined by two groups of signs present from early childhood: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction (poor eye contact, little shared enjoyment, difficulty with relationships), and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities (hand flapping, lining up toys, rigid routines, unusual sensory reactions). Language problems are central in classic autism. Some children never develop useful speech and stay mute or minimally verbal; estimates put this at roughly a quarter to a third. Others speak but show echolalia, parroting words they have just heard (immediate) or repeating phrases heard earlier, such as television jingles (delayed), and pronoun reversal, saying 'you' for 'I'. Self-injurious behaviour, such as head banging, hand biting or face slapping, is common, especially when intellectual disability is also present, and is often treated with applied behaviour analysis, which asks what the behaviour gets the child or lets him escape. Example: four-year-old Arjun does not answer to his name, repeats 'Do you want a biscuit?' instead of saying yes, and bangs his head when his routine changes. Option a is right because only autism combines absent or odd speech with repetitive and self-injurious behaviour from early childhood. Exam trap: mutism and echolalia also appear in catatonia, and selective mutism is an anxiety disorder in which the child talks normally at home; but among these four options, autism is the developmental disorder that fits all three signs.", "topic": "Autism: core symptoms"}, {"q": "Unconditional positive regard is part of what therapy?", "options": {"a": "logotherapy.", "b": "psychoanalysis.", "c": "Gestalt therapy.", "d": "client-centered therapy."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: unconditional positive regard is part of client-centred therapy. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, argued that people have an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, which is blocked when love comes with conditions of worth ('I am only good if I top the class'). The person then distorts or denies experiences that clash with the self-concept, a state Rogers called incongruence. Therapy repairs this through a relationship. In 1957 Rogers set out the necessary and sufficient conditions for change; the three core conditions on the therapist's side are congruence (being genuine), accurate empathic understanding, and unconditional positive regard (UPR). UPR means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judging and without conditions. It does not mean approving of every act; the therapist values the person while still having views about behaviour. Rogers credited the phrase to his student Stanley Standal (1954). Feeling accepted without conditions, the client can drop defences, admit feared feelings and move towards congruence. The therapist is non-directive: no interpretation, advice or diagnosis, mainly reflection of feeling. Example: Meera tells her counsellor that she secretly resents her ailing mother and feels like a monster. The counsellor neither scolds nor rushes to reassure, but conveys, 'You are carrying something heavy, and it makes sense to me,' so Meera can explore the feeling safely. Option d is right because UPR is one of Rogers's core conditions and the heart of client-centred (person-centred) therapy. Exam trap: humanistic and existential therapies are often lumped together, so logotherapy and Gestalt therapy tempt; both value the person and the present, but UPR as a named condition of change belongs to Rogers alone.", "topic": "Rogers's core conditions"}, {"q": "In operant conditioning, the reinforcer occurs ______ the response, and in classical conditioning, it occurs ______.", "options": {"a": "after; before.", "b": "after; after.", "c": "before; before.", "d": "before; after."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in operant conditioning the reinforcer occurs after the response, and in classical conditioning it occurs before. In classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus such as a bell is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) such as food, which automatically elicits an unconditioned response, salivation. Pavlov called the food the reinforcement, because it strengthens the bell and food link. The sequence runs bell, food, salivation: the UCS (the reinforcer) is presented first and draws the response out, so it comes before the response. The learner is passive, and the behaviour is elicited. In operant conditioning, set out by Edward Thorndike (1898) with his puzzle-box cats and by B. F. Skinner (1938), the organism first emits a behaviour, and a consequence then follows it. A rat presses a lever, and then a food pellet drops. The reinforcer is contingent on the response, so it must come after it; a reward given before the act could not tell the animal which behaviour paid off. Skinner called the two kinds of behaviour respondent (elicited) and operant (emitted). Example: each time the pressure cooker whistles, lunch is served, and the food in Kabir's mouth makes him salivate; the food, the reinforcer, comes first and pulls out the response, until the whistle alone makes his mouth water. In an operant case, Kabir gets praise only after he finishes his homework, so finishing homework becomes more frequent. Option a is right because the reinforcer follows the emitted response in operant learning but precedes, and elicits, the response in classical learning. Exam trap: in classical conditioning the UCS comes after the conditioned stimulus, so students write 'after' twice (option b); the stem asks about the timing relative to the response, not the stimulus.", "topic": "Reinforcer timing in conditioning"}, {"q": "Othello Syndrome is a syndrome characterized by", "options": {"a": "Pathological drinking.", "b": "Pathological jealousy.", "c": "Over concern with perfectness of bodily features.", "d": "Condition in which family member is thought as an imposter."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Othello syndrome is pathological (morbid) jealousy. It is a delusion, or sometimes an overvalued idea, that one's sexual partner is being unfaithful, held without adequate evidence and not corrected by reassurance. John Todd and Kenneth Dewhurst (1955) named it after Shakespeare's Othello, who kills his innocent wife Desdemona in a fit of jealousy fed by Iago's lies. The person checks phones and clothes for 'proof', questions the partner for hours, follows them, and reads tiny events (a late bus, a new hairstyle) as signs of betrayal. It is more common in men and is classically linked to alcohol dependence, where sexual dysfunction and marital conflict may feed it; it also occurs in delusional disorder (jealous type), schizophrenia, dementia and stimulant misuse. It matters clinically because it carries a real risk of violence, even homicide, against the partner or the imagined rival, so assessment includes asking about threats and sometimes advising separation. Example: Rohan, a heavy drinker, insists his wife Kavya is meeting a colleague; he smells her saree for perfume and demands to see her phone every evening, and no denial convinces him. Option b is right because the eponym refers to the jealousy theme alone. Exam trap: because Othello syndrome is so often seen in people who drink heavily, examiners offer 'pathological drinking' (option a); drinking is a common setting, not the syndrome. Also keep the named delusional syndromes apart: Capgras is the impostor delusion, Fregoli the belief that strangers are a familiar person in disguise, and de Clerambault the erotomanic belief that someone of higher status is secretly in love with one.", "topic": "Othello syndrome"}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1?", "options": {"a": "50 percent.", "b": "16 percent.", "c": "25 percent.", "d": "10 percent."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 16 per cent of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1. A z score tells you how many standard deviations a score lies from the mean: z = (X minus the mean) divided by the SD. The normal curve, derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and later tied to measurement error by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809), is symmetrical, with mean, median and mode at the centre, and its areas are fixed. Working step by step: 1. Half of all scores lie above the mean (z = 0), so the area above z = 0 is 50 per cent. 2. The area between the mean and z = +1 is 34.13 per cent (the familiar 68 per cent within one SD either side is 34.13 on each side). 3. So the area above z = +1 is 50 minus 34.13 = 15.87 per cent, which rounds to 16 per cent. 4. Put another way, z = +1 sits at about the 84th percentile, and 100 minus 84 = 16. Example: IQ tests have a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. Ananya scores 115, so z = (115 minus 100) divided by 15 = +1. About 84 per cent of people score below her and about 16 per cent above. By symmetry, another 16 per cent score below 85 (z = minus 1). Option b is right because 50 minus 34.13 leaves 15.87, roughly 16 per cent, in the upper tail beyond one SD. Exam trap: option a (50 per cent) is the area above the mean, not above z = 1; students who forget to subtract the middle band choose it. Also, the tail beyond two SDs is only about 2.3 per cent, so split the 68, 95 and 99.7 rule into halves before reading off a tail.", "topic": "Normal curve areas and z"}, {"q": "The so-called critical period in prenatal development is the", "options": {"a": "Germinal stage.", "b": "Embryonic stage.", "c": "Zygotic stage.", "d": "Mucotic stage."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the embryonic stage is the critical period of prenatal development. Prenatal life, about 38 weeks from conception, has three periods. The germinal period (also called the period of the zygote) covers about the first two weeks: the fertilised egg divides into a morula and then a hollow blastocyst, which implants in the uterine wall. The embryonic period runs from about week 3 to week 8. It opens with gastrulation, when the inner cell mass forms three germ layers (ectoderm for skin and nervous system, mesoderm for muscle, bone and heart, endoderm for gut and lungs), and by about week 4 the neural tube, the future brain and spinal cord, has formed. Almost every major organ takes shape in these six weeks (organogenesis). The fetal period, week 9 to birth, is mostly growth and refinement. A critical period is a window when a structure is forming and is most open to harm. Because the organs form in the embryonic period, teratogens (harmful agents such as alcohol, rubella or certain drugs) do the most structural damage then. The classic evidence is thalidomide, a sedative given to pregnant women in the late 1950s: babies exposed during the embryonic weeks were born with missing or shortened limbs, while later exposure caused far less harm. Example: Priya learns at 6 weeks that she is pregnant after taking an anti-epileptic drug; her doctor is concerned because the embryo's heart, limbs and other organs are still forming. Option b is right because organogenesis, and so maximum vulnerability, falls in weeks 3 to 8. Exam trap: the fetal period is longer and the fetus bigger, so students think it is more vulnerable; and the germinal period tends to follow an all-or-none rule, in which harm usually ends the pregnancy or leaves no defect.", "topic": "Prenatal periods and teratogens"}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of cortex is responsible for our sense of", "options": {"a": "Taste.", "b": "Sight.", "c": "Hearing.", "d": "Touch."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "topic": "Somatosensory cortex and touch"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important characteristic of research procedure?", "options": {"a": "Communicability.", "b": "Generalizability.", "c": "Replicability.", "d": "Objectivity."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: replicability is the most important characteristic of a research procedure. Science differs from opinion and authority because its findings are public and can be checked. A procedure is replicable when it is described so fully (sample, materials, measures, steps and analysis) that another researcher can repeat it and see whether the same result appears. Replication is how science corrects itself: a chance finding, an artefact or a fraud will not keep turning up when others repeat the work, while a real effect will. That is why journals demand a detailed method section, written so that any trained reader could redo the study. The importance of replication became painfully clear in the replication crisis. The Open Science Collaboration (2015) repeated 100 studies published in leading psychology journals: about 97 per cent of the originals had significant results, but only about 36 per cent of the repetitions did. Example: Riya reports that a five-minute breathing exercise lowers test anxiety in Delhi students. Unless she states exactly how she sampled, which anxiety scale she used and how long the exercise lasted, Arjun in Chennai cannot repeat her study, and her claim can be neither confirmed nor refuted. Option c is right because a procedure that cannot be repeated cannot be verified, so replicability is what makes the other qualities testable. Exam trap: objectivity (option d) is also a hallmark of science and tempts many; but objectivity means freedom from personal bias in observation, and it is itself shown by replication, when different researchers repeating the procedure get the same result. The stem asks about the procedure, and the defining property of a procedure is that it can be repeated.", "topic": "Replicability in research"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important dimension in the reference to the qualitative research?", "options": {"a": "Newness and originality.", "b": "Clarity on the basis of objectivity.", "c": "Technical practicability.", "d": "Contextual relevance."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: contextual relevance is the most important dimension in qualitative research. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, images and observed action, in natural settings, analysed by interpretation rather than statistics. Its central claim is that behaviour makes sense only in its context: the culture, relationships, history and situation in which it occurs. The anthropologist Clifford Geertz (1973) called the goal thick description, recording behaviour with the context that gives it meaning, which alone tells a wink from a twitch of the eyelid. So a qualitative study is judged by how well its account fits and illuminates the setting it studied, and how far it captures the participants' own (emic) meanings. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) replaced the quantitative criteria of validity, reliability and objectivity with trustworthiness: credibility, transferability, dependability and confirmability. Transferability makes no claim of statistical generalisation; it relies on rich contextual description so that a reader can judge whether the findings apply to another setting. Example: Meera interviews women who care for relatives with schizophrenia in rural Bengal. Their talk of 'fate' and 'family duty' is meaningful only against local beliefs about karma and joint-family obligations; strip that context away and the findings become empty. Option d is right because qualitative findings take their value from fidelity to context, not from numbers or controlled conditions. Exam trap: objectivity and technical precision are virtues of quantitative, positivist research, so 'clarity on the basis of objectivity' sounds scientific but belongs to the other paradigm; qualitative researchers instead practise reflexivity, openly accounting for their own influence on the data.", "topic": "Qualitative research and context"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "'Eccentric' behaviour marked by odd patterns of thinking and communication.", "b": "An enduring pattern of instability in personal relationships.", "c": "A lack of a well-defined and stable self-image.", "d": "Regular and unpredictable changes in mood."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is NOT a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder (BPD). Adolph Stern (1938) used 'borderline' for patients thought to sit on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation. DSM-5 (2013) defines BPD as a pervasive pattern of instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood; five of nine criteria are needed. They include frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships that swing between idealisation and devaluation, identity disturbance, self-damaging impulsivity, recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm, affective instability, chronic emptiness, intense anger, and brief stress-related paranoia or dissociation. So options b, c and d are true cardinal features: b is relationship instability, c is identity disturbance, and d is affective instability, with mood shifting within hours. Option a is the odd one out. Odd thinking, odd speech, magical beliefs and eccentric dress define schizotypal personality disorder, introduced in DSM-III (1980) and placed in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, with paranoid and schizoid. BPD sits in Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD around this core of emotional dysregulation. Example: Tanya idealises a new friend in week one, accuses her of betrayal in week three, cuts her wrist after a fight and says she does not know who she really is; that is BPD. Kabir, who dresses strangely, believes he can read minds and speaks vaguely, fits schizotypal instead. Option a is right because eccentricity is the Cluster A hallmark, not a BPD feature. Exam trap: BPD can show brief paranoid ideas under stress, so 'odd thinking' tempts; persistent eccentricity, though, is schizotypal.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder features"}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer from the code given below. List-I (Interviews): A. Structured interviews, B. Unstructured interviews, C. Focused interviews, D. Clinical interviews. List-II (Meaning): 1. Greater flexibility approach, 2. Attention on the questions to be answered, 3. Individual life experience, 4. Pre-determined question, 5. Non-directive", "options": {"a": "A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3", "b": "A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3", "c": "A-5, B-2, C-4, D-1", "d": "A-1, B-3, C-5, D-4"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3. This matching follows C. R. Kothari (2004), Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, a standard Indian research methods text. Interviews differ in how much is fixed in advance. A structured interview uses a set of predetermined questions with fixed wording and order, often read from a schedule; answers are easy to compare and score, but the method is rigid (A-4). An unstructured interview fixes only the aim and lets the interviewer change the order, wording and follow-up of questions, so its mark is greater flexibility, gaining depth at the cost of comparability (B-1). The focused interview, described by Robert Merton and Patricia Kendall (1946), focuses attention on a given experience of the respondent, such as watching a film or living through a disaster, and on its effects; the interviewer decides the manner and sequence of questions while keeping attention on the issues to be answered (C-2). The clinical interview is concerned with broad underlying feelings or motives, or with the course of the individual's life experience, as when a psychologist takes a developmental history (D-3). Kothari also lists the non-directive interview, in which the interviewer simply encourages the respondent to talk with a bare minimum of direct questioning, a style drawn from the counselling of Carl Rogers (1942); it is the extra item 5 that matches nothing. Example: Sneha studies a cyclone's impact on fishermen. She asks all 200 the same 20 questions (structured), chats freely with village elders (unstructured), asks survivors specifically about the night of the storm and how it changed them (focused), and traces one man's whole life story (clinical). Option a is right because it pairs each interview type with its defining feature. Exam trap: 'non-directive' sounds like unstructured, but here it is the spare distractor; flexibility is what defines the unstructured interview.", "topic": "Types of research interview"}, {"q": "What hormone, secreted by the pineal gland, increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes feelings of drowsiness?", "options": {"a": "Testosterone.", "b": "Melatonin.", "c": "Cortisol.", "d": "Epinephrine."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "topic": "Melatonin and the pineal gland"}, {"q": "Out of 7 consonants and 4 vowels, how many words of 3 consonants and 2 vowels can be formed?", "options": {"a": "210.", "b": "1050.", "c": "25200.", "d": "21400."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: 25200 words can be formed. The task has two parts: first choose which letters to use, then arrange them. Choosing is a combination problem, written nCr = n! / (r! (n minus r)!), because the order of picking does not matter. Arranging is a permutation problem, because in a word the order of letters does matter. Step 1: choose 3 consonants from 7: 7C3 = (7 x 6 x 5) / (3 x 2 x 1) = 210 / 6 = 35. Step 2: choose 2 vowels from 4: 4C2 = (4 x 3) / (2 x 1) = 6. Step 3: number of different sets of 5 letters = 35 x 6 = 210 (multiply, because each consonant set can go with each vowel set). Step 4: each set of 5 different letters can be arranged in 5! = 5 x 4 x 3 x 2 x 1 = 120 orders. Step 5: total words = 210 x 120 = 25200. Here a 'word' means any arrangement of the letters, whether or not it means anything, and no letter is repeated. Option c is right because it multiplies the number of letter selections (210) by the number of arrangements of each selection (120). Exam trap: option a (210) catches students who stop after choosing the letters and forget that words must also be arranged; option b (1050) comes from multiplying by 5 instead of by 5 factorial.", "topic": "Permutations and combinations"}, {"q": "During which stage of sleep are EEG recordings most similar to those during alert, awake periods, giving this stage the informal name \"paradoxical sleep\"?", "options": {"a": "Stage 1.", "b": "Stage 2.", "c": "Stage 3.", "d": "REM sleep."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: REM sleep is the stage whose EEG most resembles alert waking, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) noticed bursts of rapid eye movements in sleepers, and William Dement (1957), working with Kleitman, linked these periods to vivid dreaming. Michel Jouvet (1959), working with cats, coined the term paradoxical sleep, because the brain looks awake while the body is deeply asleep. Sleep is scored from the EEG, eye movements and muscle tone, following Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968). Stage 1 is light, drowsy sleep with low-voltage theta waves. Stage 2 adds sleep spindles and K-complexes. Stages 3 and 4 (now merged as N3) show large, slow delta waves: deep, slow-wave sleep. In REM sleep the EEG becomes fast, low-voltage and desynchronised, with sawtooth waves, much like that of an awake, alert person; yet the sleeper is hard to wake, the core muscles are almost paralysed (REM atonia), the eyes dart, and breathing and heart rate become irregular. About 80 per cent of awakenings from REM yield a dream report. REM makes up about 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep, and REM periods lengthen in the later cycles of the night, each cycle lasting about 90 minutes. Example: at 5 a.m. Neha watches her sleeping brother Rahul: his eyes flick under his lids and his body is limp, yet an EEG would show his brain as busy as when he solves maths; woken now, he will probably report a dream. Option d is right because only REM combines a waking-like EEG with deep behavioural sleep. Exam trap: stage 1 also has a low-voltage, mixed EEG close to drowsy waking, so it tempts; but 'paradoxical' refers to the clash between an active brain and a paralysed body, which belongs to REM.", "topic": "REM as paradoxical sleep"}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of", "options": {"a": "negative reinforcement.", "b": "punishment.", "c": "positive reinforcement.", "d": "secondary reinforcement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both examples of punishment. B. F. Skinner (1938) classified consequences by two questions. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. This gives four cells. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet); both increase behaviour. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding), and negative punishment removes something pleasant, as in time-out from reinforcement or response cost (a fine, losing points); both decrease behaviour. The stem describes exactly the two punishment cells. Edward Thorndike (1911), in his law of effect, had already held that responses followed by discomfort are weakened. Punishment can suppress behaviour quickly, but Skinner argued that the suppression is often temporary and can teach fear, aggression and avoidance of the punisher, so it works best when it is immediate, consistent and paired with reinforcement of a better behaviour. Example: when ten-year-old Kabir hits his sister, his mother scolds him (positive punishment) and takes away his cricket bat for the weekend (negative punishment). Both aim to make hitting less frequent. Option b is right because what defines punishment is its effect, a decrease in the behaviour, whether a stimulus is added or removed. Exam trap: the word 'removal' makes many students choose negative reinforcement (option a); but negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant and increases behaviour, whereas removing something pleasant to reduce behaviour is negative punishment.", "topic": "Punishment versus negative reinforcement"}, {"q": "Group therapy can be advantageous when an individual:", "options": {"a": "May need to work out their problems in the presence of others (e.g. in the case of emotional problems relating to relationships, feelings of isolation, loneliness and rejection).", "b": "May need comfort and support from others.", "c": "May acquire therapeutic benefit from observing and watching others.", "d": "All of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe when group therapy is advantageous. Group therapy treats several clients together with one or two therapists. Joseph Pratt (1905) began it in Boston with classes for poor tuberculosis patients and found that their mutual support lifted morale. Irvin Yalom (1970), in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy, named eleven therapeutic factors that explain why groups help, and each option matches some of them. Option a, working out problems in the presence of others, suits problems that are interpersonal in nature. The group becomes a social microcosm in which members soon behave as they do in life, so relationship difficulties, isolation, loneliness and fear of rejection show up in the room and can be worked on through interpersonal learning, the development of socialising techniques and universality, the relief of finding that others share the same struggle. Option b, needing comfort and support, matches group cohesiveness (a sense of belonging), instillation of hope (seeing others improve) and altruism (the boost to self-worth from helping others). Option c, benefiting from watching others, matches imitative behaviour: seeing how another member faces a fear or handles conflict teaches without one having to go first, much as Albert Bandura (1977) described observational learning. Groups also save therapist time and cost, which matters in busy hospital clinics. Example: Sameer, a lonely college student who feels no one understands him, joins a group; he finds others feel the same, receives warm support, and learns to speak up by watching a quieter member practise assertiveness. Option d is right because a, b and c are each recognised advantages that groups offer. Exam trap: each option alone looks like a complete answer, especially a, which reads like a full definition; when every option names a real Yalom factor, choose 'all of the above'.", "topic": "Group therapy: Yalom's factors"}, {"q": "Any obstacle in communication process is called?", "options": {"a": "Encroachment.", "b": "Plumpness.", "c": "Noise.", "d": "Inner process."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: any obstacle in the communication process is called noise. Communication moves a message from a sender to a receiver. The sender encodes an idea into symbols and sends it through a channel (speech, print, a phone); the receiver decodes it and gives feedback. Claude Shannon and Warren Weaver (1949) added noise to this model: anything that interferes with the message between encoding and decoding, so that what is received differs from what was sent. Noise is not only sound. Physical noise is outside interference, such as traffic, a weak network or a smudged page. Physiological noise comes from the body: hunger, poor hearing, fatigue. Psychological noise is inner distraction or bias: worry, prejudice, daydreaming. Semantic noise arises when words mean different things to sender and receiver, as with jargon or a word with two senses. Example: Professor Iyer explains 'reliability' while a drill runs next door (physical noise), Rahul is anxious about his rent (psychological noise), and Pooja takes 'reliability' in its everyday sense rather than the statistical one (semantic noise): three kinds of noise in one lecture. Option c is right because noise is the technical term for every barrier that distorts or blocks a message. Exam trap: 'inner process' sounds like psychological noise, but it simply names thinking, or intrapersonal communication, not an obstacle; noise is the umbrella term for all barriers.", "topic": "Noise in communication"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "options": {"a": "Essential features of the disorder.", "b": "Associated features.", "c": "A list of symptoms that must be present for diagnosis.", "d": "Information on treatment options."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: information on treatment options is not provided in DSM-IV-TR. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, it has used explicit diagnostic criteria and a descriptive approach that is neutral about causes. DSM-IV (1994) was updated as DSM-IV-TR (2000), the text revision, which mainly refreshed the descriptive text for each disorder with newer research while leaving the criteria almost unchanged. For each disorder the manual uses standard headings: diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory and physical findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern, differential diagnosis, and finally the boxed criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present. So options a, b and c are all in the manual: a is the essential features, b the associated features, and c the criteria set. What it leaves out is treatment. The DSM is a tool for classification and diagnosis, not a treatment guide; choosing therapy is left to clinical judgement, outcome research and separate practice guidelines. Example: Dr Kavita looks up obsessive-compulsive disorder in DSM-IV-TR. She finds the essential features, the usual age of onset, the course, the prevalence and the criteria she must check; to choose between exposure and response prevention and an SSRI, she must turn to the treatment literature. Option d is right because DSM-IV-TR describes and defines disorders but deliberately says nothing about how to treat them. Exam trap: the word 'manual' makes students assume it tells clinicians what to do; it tells them what to call a problem, not how to treat it, and the same holds for DSM-5 and ICD-11.", "topic": "What the DSM-IV-TR contains"}, {"q": "Mommy shows her boy five black and three white marbles and asks him \"Are there more marbles or more black marbles?\" He replied \"More black ones!\". According to Piaget, the child is in", "options": {"a": "Pre-operational stage.", "b": "Concrete operational stage.", "c": "Formal operational stage.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the child who says there are 'more black ones' is in Piaget's pre-operational stage. Jean Piaget (1952) described a pre-operational stage, from about 2 to 7 years, when children can use symbols and language but cannot yet perform operations, mental actions that are reversible and logically organised. The marble question tests class inclusion: understanding that a subclass (black marbles) is part of a larger class (marbles), so the whole must be at least as large as any of its parts. Here there are 8 marbles, 5 black and 3 white, so there are more marbles than black marbles. The pre-operational child fails because of centration: he fixes on the most striking contrast, black versus white, and compares the two subclasses instead of comparing a subclass with the whole. Holding the part and the whole in mind at once needs reversibility, which he lacks. Piaget's own version used wooden beads, mostly brown and a few white: asked 'Are there more brown beads or more wooden beads?', young children said 'more brown'. Class inclusion, like conservation and seriation, is mastered in the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11. Example: shown 6 roses and 2 lilies, five-year-old Ishaan says there are more roses than flowers; at 8, his sister Diya laughs, 'Roses are flowers too!' Option a is right because failure of class inclusion is a hallmark of pre-operational thought. Exam trap: James McGarrigle, Robert Grieve and Martin Hughes (1978) showed that rewording the question raises young children's success, so the failure partly reflects language; but in Piaget's scheme this answer still places the child in the pre-operational stage, not 'none of the above'.", "topic": "Piaget: class inclusion"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a mature defense mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Altruism.", "b": "Intellectualisation.", "c": "Repression.", "d": "Rationalisation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: altruism is a mature defence mechanism. Defence mechanisms are mostly unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety by distorting or rechannelling threatening feelings; Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea, and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, used decades of life-history data on Harvard men in the Grant Study to rank defences in four levels of maturity. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation, isolation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation and suppression. Men who relied on mature defences had better marriages, careers, health and happiness decades later. Altruism as a defence means gaining satisfaction from constructive service to others, turning one's own pain into help for people in the same position instead of being swamped by it. It is mature because it faces reality and feelings while also strengthening relationships. Example: after losing her brother to suicide, Nisha volunteers on a suicide prevention helpline; her grief is real, but channelling it into helping callers protects her from despair and helps others too. Option a is right because altruism sits at Vaillant's mature level with humour, anticipation, sublimation and suppression. Exam trap: rationalisation and intellectualisation sound grown-up and thoughtful, so they tempt as mature defences; but both distort reality through thinking, and they sit lower, at the neurotic level or below.", "topic": "Vaillant's mature defences"}, {"q": "Mediated intra-personal communication can be seen in writing:", "options": {"a": "Letter to friends.", "b": "A personal diary.", "c": "A memo to subordinates.", "d": "Reports for internal circulation in an organization."}, "correct": "b", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is b: writing a personal diary is mediated intrapersonal communication. Communication is classified by who takes part. Intrapersonal communication is communication with oneself: thinking, self-talk, planning and reflecting. Interpersonal communication is between two or a few people; group communication happens within a small group; organisational communication runs through the formal channels of an organisation; and mass communication reaches large, scattered audiences through the media. Communication is mediated when a medium, such as pen and paper, a phone or a computer, carries the message instead of direct face-to-face speech. Mediated intrapersonal communication therefore needs both features: the sender and the receiver are the same person, and the message passes through a medium. A personal diary is the classic case. The writer records thoughts and feelings in writing, and the only intended reader is the writer, now or later. Example: every night Arjun writes in his diary about his fear of the entrance exam; reading it a month later, he notices how his worry has eased. He is both sender and receiver, and the diary is the medium. Option b is right because a diary is a message to oneself carried through a medium. Exam trap: all four options involve writing, which makes them all mediated, so the real test is who the receiver is; only in the diary is the receiver the writer.", "topic": "Levels of communication"}, {"q": "Which of the following has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "options": {"a": "Criminal behaviour leading to a prison sentence.", "b": "Psychosis.", "c": "Antisocial personality disorder.", "d": "All of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of behaviour that violates the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate norms: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule-breaking such as truancy. The strongest link is to antisocial personality disorder (ASPD). DSM-5 (2013) diagnoses ASPD only in people aged at least 18 with evidence of conduct disorder beginning before age 15, so adult ASPD is by definition a continuation of childhood CD. The classic evidence on adult crime is Lee Robins (1966), Deviant Children Grown Up, which followed children seen at a St Louis child guidance clinic some 30 years later: the antisocial children became adults with high rates of arrest, imprisonment and sociopathy, and adult sociopathy almost never appeared without antisocial behaviour in childhood. Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated adolescence-limited offending from a life-course-persistent pattern, whose early-onset CD predicts the worst adult outcomes. The link to psychosis comes from the Dunedin cohort in New Zealand, followed from birth: Julia Kim-Cohen and colleagues (2003) found that many adults with schizophreniform disorder had a juvenile history of conduct disorder. Example: Vikram, at 13, bullies classmates, steals and sets fire to a shed; if the pattern persists, he is at high risk of imprisonment and an ASPD diagnosis as an adult, and at some raised risk of later psychotic illness. Option d is right because each of a, b and c has research support. Exam trap: ASPD is the textbook link, so many tick c alone; the stem asks what has been associated with CD, and all three have. Key note: psychosis is the least direct of the three links, resting on cohort data rather than on diagnostic definition, but d stands.", "topic": "Conduct disorder outcomes"}, {"q": "Which of the following is false? Social anxiety:", "options": {"a": "is linked to genetic factor.", "b": "is linked to over protective parenting.", "c": "is more likely to manifest in adulthood.", "d": "may result from early experience of shame."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: it is false that social anxiety is more likely to manifest in adulthood. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is a marked fear of situations in which one may be scrutinised by others, such as speaking, eating or writing in public, because one fears acting in a way that will be humiliating or embarrassing; the situations are avoided or endured with intense anxiety, typically for 6 months or more. Its onset is early. DSM-5 (2013) gives a median age of onset in the United States of 13 years, with about 75 per cent of cases beginning between 8 and 15; first onset in adulthood is relatively rare and often follows a stressful or humiliating event or a new social role. So c is the false statement and the answer. The other three are true. Option a: social anxiety runs in families, and twin data (Kenneth Kendler and colleagues, 1992) suggest a moderate heritability of about 30 per cent, partly through the behavioural inhibition that Jerome Kagan (1988) found in shy, high-reactive infants. Option b: overprotective, controlling parenting is linked to child anxiety (reviewed by Ronald Rapee, 1997), because it signals that the world is dangerous and denies the child practice at coping. Option d: early experiences of shame, humiliation, teasing or bullying are common; Ann Hackmann, David Clark and Freda McManus (2000) found that the negative self-images in social phobia often trace back to such memories. Example: Ritu was mocked when she stammered during a Class 6 recitation; by 14 she avoids answering in class and eats lunch alone, a typical adolescent onset. Option c is right because social anxiety usually begins in childhood or early adolescence, not adulthood. Exam trap: many adults first seek help in their twenties or later, so students mistake late help-seeking for late onset.", "topic": "Social anxiety: onset and causes"}, {"q": "Of the following individuals, the one who is credited as being one of the founders of social psychology is", "options": {"a": "Norman Triplett.", "b": "Kenneth Gergen", "c": "David Sears", "d": "Harry Triandis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Norman Triplett is credited as one of the founders of social psychology. Norman Triplett (1898), working at Indiana University, studied cycling records and noticed that riders were faster when paced by another cyclist or racing rivals than when riding alone against the clock. He then had about 40 children wind fishing reels as fast as possible, alone or beside another child doing the same task. Many wound faster in company, though some did worse. Published in the American Journal of Psychology as 'The dynamogenic factors in pacemaking and competition', it is often called the first experiment in social psychology, and it began research on social facilitation, later explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, which helps on easy or well-learnt tasks and hurts on hard ones. Other founding figures are William McDougall and Edward Ross, who both published textbooks titled Social Psychology in 1908, and Floyd Allport (1924), who made the field experimental. Example: Aarav runs his best 400-metre time at the inter-school meet with rivals beside him, not in solo practice; that is the effect Triplett measured. Option a is right because Triplett's 1898 study is the landmark taken to mark the start of experimental social psychology. Exam trap: the other three are real, well-known social psychologists, so their names feel familiar; but they did their main work in the 1970s to 1990s, long after the field was founded. Some historians, such as Wolfgang Stroebe (2012), argue that Triplett's data gave weaker support than textbooks suggest, but he is still the standard answer to this question.", "topic": "Founders of social psychology"}, {"q": "The auditory nerve, which transmits information from the ear to the brain, is an example of an", "options": {"a": "Afferent nerve.", "b": "Efferent nerve.", "c": "Autonomic.", "d": "Analgesic."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the auditory nerve is an afferent nerve, because it carries sensory messages from the ear inward to the brain. The peripheral nervous system has two directions of traffic. Afferent (sensory) fibres carry signals from receptors toward the central nervous system; efferent (motor) fibres carry commands away from it to muscles and glands. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent; afferent fibres arrive, efferent fibres exit. The split was first shown at the spinal cord by Charles Bell (1811) and Francois Magendie (1822): the dorsal roots carry sensory input in, and the ventral roots carry motor output out (the Bell-Magendie law). Interneurons inside the brain and spinal cord link the two, as in the reflex arc. The auditory nerve is the cochlear branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve, cranial nerve VIII. Hair cells in the cochlea transduce sound vibrations into neural impulses, and the nerve carries these to the cochlear nuclei of the brainstem, then on through the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. Because its job is to bring information in, cranial nerve VIII is classed as a sensory nerve. Example: when Meera hears her name called in a crowded market, the message travels along her afferent auditory nerve to her brain; the turn of her head that follows uses efferent motor nerves. Option a is right because afferent means conducting toward the central nervous system, which is exactly what a nerve that transmits information from the ear to the brain does. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, offering efferent for a sensory nerve, and they add labels such as autonomic that name a division of the nervous system rather than the direction in which a nerve conducts.", "topic": "Afferent and efferent nerves"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": {"a": "In research, objectives can be worded in question form.", "b": "In research, objectives can be worded in statement form.", "c": "Objectives are to be stated in chapter I of the thesis.", "d": "All of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements are correct, since research objectives may be phrased as questions or as statements and are presented in the first chapter of a thesis. An objective states what a study sets out to find or do. It grows out of the research problem: the researcher narrows a broad area to a specific problem, raises questions about it, and turns them into objectives, which then guide the hypotheses, design, sample and analysis. Objectives can take two grammatical forms. In statement form they usually begin with an action verb: 'To study the relationship between screen time and sleep quality among college students.' In question form the same aim reads: 'Is there a relationship between screen time and sleep quality among college students?' Both are accepted; the question form is often called a research question. Good objectives are specific, clear, measurable and achievable within the limits of the study. In a standard thesis, Chapter I (Introduction) presents the background, the statement of the problem, its significance, the objectives, the hypotheses and the operational definitions of the main terms. Chapter II reviews the literature, Chapter III describes the method, and the results, discussion and summary follow. Example: Kabir, an M.Phil scholar, writes in Chapter I: 'Objective 1: To assess the level of exam anxiety in Class 12 students. Objective 2: Do boys and girls differ in exam anxiety?' Both lines are legitimate objectives, and both sit in the introductory chapter. Option d is right because a, b and c are each true, and only d includes all of them. Exam trap: students pick one form, thinking objectives must be statements and that questions belong only to hypotheses; the examiner wants you to see that both forms are allowed and that objectives belong in the introduction, not in the method or results chapter.", "topic": "Wording research objectives"}, {"q": "According to Gould, a \"crisis of questions,\" where an individual ponders the meaning of life, occurs during", "options": {"a": "adolescence.", "b": "early adulthood.", "c": "middle adulthood.", "d": "old age."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Roger Gould placed the questioning of life's meaning in middle adulthood, in what he called the mid-life decade, roughly 35 to 45. Roger Gould (1978), an American psychiatrist, set out his theory in Transformations: Growth and Change in Adult Life. He argued that adults grow by shedding false assumptions carried over from childhood, beliefs that once gave a feeling of safety but later block maturity. Each phase of adulthood challenges one of them. From about 16 to 22 people question 'I will always belong to my parents and believe in their world.' From about 22 to 28 they test 'Doing things my parents' way, with will power and perseverance, will bring results, and they will rescue me if I cannot cope.' From about 28 to 34 they face 'Life is simple and controllable.' In the mid-life decade, roughly 35 to 45, comes the deepest challenge: 'There is no evil or death in the world.' Awareness of mortality, the sense that time is running out, children growing up and limits at work push people to ask what their life means and what they truly want. After about 45, those who work through this reach a more settled, self-accepting adult consciousness. Gould's account sits beside Daniel Levinson's (1978) midlife transition at 40 to 45 and Elliott Jaques's (1965) coinage of the term midlife crisis. Example: Arjun, 42, a successful bank manager, suddenly asks whether his work means anything, thinks about his father's recent death and wonders what he wants from the years left to him. That is Gould's mid-life questioning. Option c is right because Gould's questioning of meaning, driven by a new sense of mortality, falls in the mid-life decade, which belongs to middle adulthood. Exam trap: the word crisis makes students jump to adolescence and Erik Erikson's (1950) identity crisis; Gould's crisis is about meaning and mortality, and it peaks at midlife, not in the teens.", "topic": "Gould's adult transformations"}, {"q": "Which one of the following options are the main tasks of research in modern society? i. To keep pace with the advancement in knowledge, ii. To discover new things, iii. To write a critique on the earlier writings, iv. To systematically examine and critically analyze the investigations/sources with objectivity", "options": {"a": "iv, ii, i.", "b": "i, ii, iii.", "c": "i, iii.", "d": "ii, iii, iv."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: statements i, ii and iv (keeping pace with knowledge, discovering new things, and objective critical examination of sources) are the main tasks of research in modern society, while writing a critique of earlier writings is not. Research is a careful, systematic search for new knowledge. In a modern society that runs on science, technology and evidence-based policy, it serves three linked tasks. First, it keeps pace with the advancement of knowledge: findings age quickly, so fields such as medicine, psychology and education must keep updating what they know. Second, it discovers new things: new facts, relations, theories, tools and solutions to practical problems, from a vaccine to a better school curriculum. Third, it examines investigations and sources systematically and critically, with objectivity, so that conclusions rest on evidence rather than on opinion, authority or tradition. Charles Sanders Peirce (1877) called science the only way of fixing belief that corrects its own mistakes, and these tasks are what keep that self-correction going. Writing a critique of earlier writings is a scholarly or literary exercise. It happens inside research as part of the literature review, but on its own it creates no new knowledge and is not one of research's main tasks in society. Example: Meera, a clinical psychologist in Pune, wants to know whether an online CBT app reduces anxiety in college students. She reads recent trials to stay current (i), runs her own trial to find out something new (ii), and analyses her data and her sources objectively (iv). A book review she writes for a journal is a separate activity. Option a is right because it is the only choice that contains i, ii and iv and leaves out iii. Exam trap: option a lists the numbers in an odd order (iv, ii, i), which makes students distrust it; the order does not matter, only which statements are included.", "topic": "Tasks of research in society"}, {"q": "A person who has an extreme lack of self-confidence and who allows others to run his or her life is said to have a(n) ______ personality.", "options": {"a": "antisocial.", "b": "narcissistic.", "c": "paranoid.", "d": "dependent."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: an extreme lack of self-confidence, together with letting others run one's life, is the core picture of dependent personality disorder. Dependent personality disorder belongs to DSM-5 Cluster C, the anxious-fearful cluster, along with avoidant and obsessive-compulsive personality disorders. Its essence is a pervasive and excessive need to be taken care of, which leads to submissive, clinging behaviour and fear of separation, beginning by early adulthood. DSM-5 requires five of eight features: difficulty making everyday decisions without excessive advice and reassurance; needing others to take responsibility for major areas of life; difficulty disagreeing for fear of losing support; difficulty starting projects alone because of a lack of self-confidence rather than of motivation; going to great lengths to obtain care, even volunteering for unpleasant tasks; feeling helpless when alone; urgently seeking a new relationship when one ends; and unrealistic fear of being left to care for oneself. Theodore Millon (1981) described this as the passive-dependent pattern: the person sees himself as weak and others as strong and protective, and trades autonomy for security. Example: Riya, 30, lets her mother choose her clothes, her course and her husband, cannot decide what to cook without phoning someone, and agrees with everyone so that no one will leave her. Option d is right because both parts of the stem, extreme lack of self-confidence and allowing others to run one's life, are defining criteria of dependent personality disorder and of no other option. Exam trap: avoidant personality disorder is the near miss. The avoidant person also feels inadequate, but withdraws from relationships for fear of rejection, whereas the dependent person clings to relationships and hands over control. Both are Cluster C, so tell them apart by direction: avoidant moves away from people, dependent moves toward them.", "topic": "Dependent personality disorder"}, {"q": "Delusions shared between two closely connected persons is also known as", "options": {"a": "Derealization.", "b": "Deja vu.", "c": "Deja entendu", "d": "Folie a deux."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a delusion shared by two closely connected people is called folie à deux, French for 'madness of two'. Charles Lasegue and Jules Falret (1877) described it. Typically a dominant primary person has a genuine psychotic disorder with a fixed delusion, and a more passive secondary partner, closely bound to the primary and often socially isolated, comes to share the same belief. The pair are usually relatives, such as spouses, siblings or parent and child, living together and cut off from outside contact. The main clinical point is that separation often lets the secondary partner's belief fade, while the primary's persists. Variants include folie imposée (the delusion is imposed on a healthy partner), folie simultanée (two predisposed people fall ill together) and folie à trois or folie à famille when more people share it. ICD-10 coded it as induced delusional disorder (F24) and DSM-IV as shared psychotic disorder. DSM-5 dropped the separate category: the partner is diagnosed with delusional disorder if the criteria are met, otherwise with other specified schizophrenia spectrum disorder (delusional symptoms in the partner of an individual with delusional disorder). Example: Meera's elderly mother believes the neighbours are poisoning their water. Meera, who lives alone with her and rarely goes out, begins boiling every drop and accusing the neighbours too. When Meera moves to a hostel for her course, her belief slowly weakens; her mother's does not. Option d is right because folie à deux is the specific term for a delusion shared by two people in a close relationship. Exam trap: the distractors are other French terms from descriptive psychopathology. Déjà vu and déjà entendu are disturbances of recognition memory, and derealisation is a change in how the world feels; none of them is a delusion, and none is shared.", "topic": "Folie à deux"}, {"q": "Effectiveness of teaching depends on", "options": {"a": "Qualification of teacher.", "b": "Personality of teacher.", "c": "Handwriting of teacher.", "d": "Subject understanding of teacher."}, "correct": "d", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is d: the effectiveness of teaching depends above all on the teacher's understanding of the subject. A teacher can only help students understand what he himself understands. Deep command of the subject lets a teacher explain an idea in more than one way, choose good examples, foresee the usual mistakes, answer unexpected questions and connect the topic to students' lives. Lee Shulman (1986) called the most useful form of this pedagogical content knowledge: knowing a subject in the special way needed to teach it, such as which analogy makes fractions clear to a ten-year-old. Personality matters too (warmth, enthusiasm, fairness), but a pleasant teacher who does not understand the subject cannot teach it well, while a clear, knowledgeable teacher can be effective with many kinds of temperament. Formal qualification shows that a course was passed, not that the subject was understood. Example: Kabir and Ananya both hold an M.Sc. in physics. Ananya truly understands why objects float, so she turns a bucket of water into a lively lesson; Kabir recites a memorised definition of buoyancy and freezes when a student asks how a steel ship floats. Same qualification, very different teaching. Option d is right because understanding of the subject is the foundation on which explanation, questioning and every other teaching skill rests. Exam trap: qualification sounds official and personality sounds modern, but teaching aptitude questions reward the factor that most directly produces learning, which is the teacher's grasp of the subject.", "topic": "Effective teaching"}, {"q": "Which one of the following measures is to make research more ethical?", "options": {"a": "Informed consent.", "b": "Pilot study.", "c": "Government regulation.", "d": "All the above."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: informed consent is the measure that makes research more ethical. Informed consent means that a person agrees to take part in research freely, after being told what the study involves, and while able to understand that information. It has four parts: information (the purpose, procedures, duration, risks, benefits and the use of data), comprehension (explained in language the person understands), voluntariness (no coercion or undue inducement) and competence (the capacity to decide; for children, their assent plus a parent's permission). Participants must also know that they can withdraw at any time without penalty. The principle grew out of abuse. After Nazi doctors experimented on prisoners, the Nuremberg Code (1947) made the voluntary consent of the human subject its first and absolutely essential rule. The Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), with its principle of respect for persons, built on it. In India the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) require informed consent, usually in writing, and the APA Ethics Code (2002, Standard 8.02) requires it for psychological research. Example: before Arjun interviews flood survivors in Assam about trauma, he explains the study in Assamese, warns that some questions may be upsetting, gives a counsellor's number, promises confidentiality and makes clear they can stop at any point. Only then does each person sign. Option a is right because informed consent directly protects participants' autonomy and is the central ethical safeguard in research with people. Exam trap: 'all the above' tempts students because the other options sound responsible, but a pilot study is a methodological trial run and government regulation is an outside control, not a measure the researcher takes to make a study ethical.", "topic": "Informed consent in research"}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Humanistic principles", "c": "Operant conditioning", "d": "Psychodynamic principles"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: functional analysis rests on operant conditioning, the principle that behaviour is maintained by its consequences. Operant conditioning was developed by B. F. Skinner (1938): behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour that is not reinforced, or is punished, becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this to a problem behaviour by asking what the behaviour does for the person. The clinician defines the target behaviour precisely and records its ABC: the Antecedent (what happens just before), the Behaviour, and the Consequence (what follows). Problem behaviours usually serve one of four functions: gaining attention, escaping demands, getting tangible items, or automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Brian Iwata and colleagues (1982) turned this into an experimental method for self-injury, deliberately varying conditions (attention, academic demand, alone, play) to see which one kept the behaviour going. Strictly, functional analysis is an assessment rather than a full therapy, but it drives treatment in behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis: remove the reinforcer that maintains the problem and reinforce an alternative behaviour that serves the same function. Example: Kabir, 7, throws his workbook whenever he is given maths sums, and the teacher sends him out of class. The ABC record shows that the consequence is escape from maths, so the behaviour is negatively reinforced. The plan keeps him in class, breaks tasks into small steps and teaches him to ask for a short break instead. Option c is right because the whole logic of functional analysis, consequences maintaining behaviour, comes from operant conditioning. Exam trap: because functional analysis is used in behaviour therapy, students choose classical conditioning, which underlies systematic desensitisation and exposure; but classical conditioning is about pairings of stimuli, not about consequences.", "topic": "Functional analysis and operant learning"}, {"q": "Which of the following changes is not caused by activity of the sympathetic nervous system?", "options": {"a": "Dilation of pupil.", "b": "Decrease in digestive secretions.", "c": "Acceleration of heart action.", "d": "All of the above are caused by activity of the sympathetic nervous system."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: pupil dilation, decreased digestive secretions and a faster heart are all caused by sympathetic activity, so none of a, b or c is the exception. The autonomic nervous system controls involuntary organs and has two main divisions that usually act in opposition. The sympathetic division prepares the body for emergency action, what Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, most of its effects use noradrenaline, and adrenaline from the adrenal medulla backs them up. The parasympathetic division, from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the opposite, 'rest and digest', using acetylcholine. Check each option. (a) Sympathetic fibres contract the radial dilator muscle of the iris, so the pupil widens and lets in more light; parasympathetic fibres in cranial nerve III constrict it. (b) Sympathetic activity reduces salivation and digestive secretions and slows the gut, since digestion can wait in an emergency; this is why the mouth goes dry before an interview. (c) It speeds and strengthens the heartbeat, sending more blood to the muscles. It also widens the airways, releases glucose from the liver and causes sweating. Example: Arjun is walking home at night when a dog leaps at him. His pupils widen, his mouth goes dry, his heart pounds and his breathing quickens, all within seconds and all sympathetic. Once he is safe at home and eating dinner, parasympathetic activity slows his heart and restarts digestion. Option d is right because all three listed changes are classic sympathetic effects, so there is no change among a to c that the sympathetic system fails to cause. Exam trap: students mix up the divisions on digestion and the pupil; remember that sympathetic means dilated pupils, a dry mouth and halted digestion, and parasympathetic reverses each one.", "topic": "Sympathetic nervous system effects"}, {"q": "In south and east Asia, a man may experience Koro, which is", "options": {"a": "anxiety about his penis receding into his body.", "b": "insanity.", "c": "depression.", "d": "schizophrenia."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: koro is an acute anxiety that the penis is shrinking or retracting into the body, often with the fear that full retraction will cause death. Koro is a classic culture-bound syndrome, now called a cultural concept of distress: a pattern of suffering shaped by, and mainly found in, particular cultures. It is reported in southern China, where it is called suo yang, in Southeast Asia, and in north-east India. The person, usually a young man, suddenly becomes convinced that his genitals are retracting. He panics, may tie or clamp the penis or have relatives hold it, and seeks urgent help. In women the fear may concern the breasts or vulva. Koro can spread in epidemics carried by rumour, as in Singapore in 1967 and in outbreaks in north-east India and West Bengal in the 1980s. Local beliefs about sexual energy, semen loss and spirit attack give the symptom its meaning. Pow Meng Yap (1965) described koro in detail and helped build the idea of culture-bound syndromes. DSM-5 lists koro as an example of other specified obsessive-compulsive and related disorder, beside other body-focused worries. Example: during a koro scare in his village, Arjun, 19, wakes at night sure that his penis is shrinking, shouts for his brothers to hold it and is rushed to the health centre. Examination is normal, and reassurance with an explanation of anxiety brings relief. Option a is right because koro is defined by anxiety about the penis receding into the body. Exam trap: koro is often confused with dhat syndrome, the South Asian anxiety about losing semen in the urine (named by N. N. Wig, 1960); both involve genital worries, but dhat is about semen loss and weakness, while koro is about retraction.", "topic": "Koro, a culture-bound syndrome"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia makes people violent.", "b": "If you hear voices or hold delusional beliefs you have schizophrenia.", "c": "Siblings of people who are diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to suffer mental health problems in their lifetime.", "d": "People with a diagnosis of schizophrenia are more likely to commit murder than their non-schizophrenic peer group."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: siblings of people with schizophrenia have a higher lifetime risk of mental health problems, including schizophrenia itself. Schizophrenia runs in families. Pooling European family and twin studies, Irving Gottesman (1991) estimated a lifetime risk of about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for the child of one affected parent, 17% for a fraternal twin and about 48% for an identical twin. Risk rises with the share of genes, but environment matters too, since even an identical co-twin falls ill only about half the time. Siblings also show raised rates of related problems such as schizotypal traits, depression and substance misuse, through shared genes and shared stress. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent, and they are more often victims than perpetrators of violence. Hearing voices or holding unusual beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: such experiences occur in bereavement, sleep deprivation, drug use, severe depression, bipolar disorder and PTSD, and in a minority of healthy people. Example: Riya's elder brother was diagnosed with schizophrenia at 22. Riya is not doomed, but her risk is higher than her classmates', so her doctor advises her to avoid cannabis and to seek help early if problems appear. Option c is right because it is the only statement firmly supported by family studies; a, b and d overstate, overgeneralise or stigmatise. Exam trap: d sounds factual because the media link schizophrenia with murder, but the great majority of homicides are committed by people without schizophrenia. Key note: large registry studies (Seena Fazel and colleagues, 2009) do find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, much of it linked to substance misuse, although the absolute risk stays very small; d is written as the popular myth and c is the clearly true statement, so the key used here stands.", "topic": "Schizophrenia myths and family risk"}, {"q": "Identify the TRUE statement:", "options": {"a": "The main purpose of training is to equip candidate with specific skill.", "b": "Teaching can be done without indoctrination but indoctrination is not possible without teaching.", "c": "Instruction does not involve an interaction between the teacher and the learner.", "d": "All the above."}, "correct": "a", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is a: the main purpose of training is to equip a candidate with a specific skill. Teaching aptitude papers separate several related ideas. Training develops particular skills or habits through practice, usually for a definite job: typing, driving, giving an IQ test. Its aim is competent performance of a defined task. Teaching is broader: it aims at understanding, reasoning and the growth of the whole learner, and it respects the learner's right to ask why. Instruction is the organised presentation of knowledge or directions so that someone learns, and it is interactive: the teacher explains, the learner responds, the teacher gives feedback. Indoctrination is getting someone to accept beliefs without regard to evidence, so that they are not questioned. Thomas F. Green (1971), in The Activities of Teaching, placed training and instructing close to the centre of teaching and indoctrinating at its edge, near propaganda. Now test the statements. Statement a is true by definition. Statement b fails in its second half: indoctrination does not need teaching, since propaganda, constant repetition, ritual, peer pressure and the media can indoctrinate without any lesson. Statement c is false because instruction always involves interaction between teacher and learner. So d, 'all the above', fails too. Example: Arjun's driving school trains him to parallel-park, which is training for a specific skill. His physics teacher explaining why a car skids on a wet road is teaching for understanding. Option a is right because equipping a person with a specific skill is exactly what training is for, and it is the only fully true statement. Exam trap: b sounds balanced and philosophical, so students accept it; read both halves, and notice that indoctrination can happen with no teaching at all.", "topic": "Training, teaching and indoctrination"}, {"q": "The MMPI measures which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Mood.", "b": "Physical concerns.", "c": "Social attitudes.", "d": "All of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the MMPI measures mood, physical concerns and social attitudes, along with much else. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. It is a true-false self-report inventory (567 items in the MMPI-2 of James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) made by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. Multiphasic means that it samples many areas of personality and psychopathology at once. Its original item pool was sorted into some two dozen content areas, including general health, bodily symptoms, family, work, sexual, religious, political and social attitudes, depressed and manic mood, phobias and morale. The ten clinical scales reflect this breadth. Mood: scale 2 (Depression) and scale 9 (Hypomania), with scale 7 (Psychasthenia) for anxiety. Physical concerns: scale 1 (Hypochondriasis) and scale 3 (Hysteria), the somatic side of the neurotic triad. Social attitudes: scale 4 (Psychopathic Deviate) taps conflict with authority and social rules, and scale 0 (Social Introversion) taps comfort with people; content scales such as Cynicism and Antisocial Practices add more. Validity scales (L, F, K) check how the person approached the test. Example: Arjun, 35, complains of headaches and stomach pain with no medical cause. His MMPI-2 shows high scores on scales 1, 2 and 3, a pattern that points the psychologist toward somatic and depressive concerns rather than physical illness. Option d is right because mood, physical concerns and social attitudes are each measured by MMPI items and scales, so all three are correct. Exam trap: students think the MMPI measures only clinical syndromes; it is a broad multiphasic inventory, and social attitudes were one of its original item categories.", "topic": "What the MMPI measures"}, {"q": "In which of the following ways a theory is not different from a belief?", "options": {"a": "Antecedent-consequent.", "b": "Acceptability.", "c": "Verifiability.", "d": "Demonstratability."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: acceptability is the one respect in which a theory is not different from a belief, because both are things people accept as true. A belief is a proposition a person accepts, often on the strength of tradition, authority or feeling, without asking for evidence. A scientific theory is a set of interrelated concepts, definitions and propositions that explains and predicts phenomena by specifying relations among variables; Fred Kerlinger (1964) gave this classic definition in Foundations of Behavioral Research. Both a belief and a theory are accepted by those who hold them, so acceptability does not separate them. What separates them is how each stands to evidence. A theory states antecedent-consequent relations: if certain conditions are present, certain outcomes follow, so it explains causes and predicts effects. A theory is verifiable: its predictions can be tested and could turn out false, which Karl Popper (1934) made the mark of science. And a theory is demonstrable: its support can be shown publicly to others, who can repeat the observations. A belief needs none of these. Example: Meera's grandmother believes that exams go well if you eat curd and sugar before leaving home. Meera, studying psychology, learns the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908), which predicts that performance rises with arousal up to a point and then falls, and which can be tested in experiments. Both ideas are accepted by the people who hold them; only the second states testable cause-and-effect relations that can be shown to others. Option b is right because acceptance is common to both, while antecedent-consequent structure, verifiability and demonstrability belong to theory alone. Exam trap: the word not in the stem reverses the usual question; you are looking for the shared feature, not the differences.", "topic": "Theory versus belief"}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her", "options": {"a": "occipital lobe.", "b": "parietal lobe.", "c": "temporal lobes.", "d": "reticular formation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe, which houses the primary visual cortex. Visual information leaves the retina along the optic nerves. At the optic chiasm the fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross, so each optic tract carries the opposite half of the visual field. The tracts reach the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and the optic radiations carry the signal to the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, Korbinian Brodmann's (1909) area 17) around the calcarine sulcus at the back of the occipital lobe. V1 is mapped point by point onto the retina, so damage to part of it removes part of the visual field. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which supplies the occipital lobe, typically causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia: loss of the same half of the visual field in both eyes, on the side opposite the lesion. Central vision is often spared (macular sparing), because the occipital pole also receives blood from the middle cerebral artery. Damage to both occipital lobes causes cortical blindness. Example: after a stroke in her right occipital lobe, Kamala, 70, cannot see cars coming from her left unless she turns her head, although her eyes themselves are healthy. She knows the left half of her view is missing and learns to scan toward it: a left homonymous hemianopia. Option a is right because the occipital lobe contains the primary visual cortex, and losing part of it produces partial blindness. Exam trap: the parietal lobe is the near miss, since a right parietal stroke causes hemispatial neglect, in which the patient ignores the left side; but neglect is a failure of attention, and the person is not blind in that field.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision"}, {"q": "Some proverbs/idioms are given below together with their meanings. Choose the correct meaning of proverb/idiom. To keep one's temper", "options": {"a": "To become hungry.", "b": "To be in good mood.", "c": "To preserve one's energy.", "d": "To be aloof from."}, "correct": "b", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'to keep one's temper' means to stay calm and not become angry, and of the four options 'to be in good mood' is the closest. Temper here means a person's state of mind, especially control over anger. To keep your temper is to stay calm and in control even when provoked; to lose your temper is to become suddenly angry. Example: when the bus conductor shouted at her for not having change, Riya kept her temper, smiled and paid by phone; her friend Kabir lost his temper and argued for ten minutes. Option b is right because a person who keeps his temper stays even-tempered and pleasant, which is nearer to being in a good mood than to hunger, saving energy or aloofness. Exam trap: keep tempts students toward 'to preserve one's energy', and temper sounds like temperament, but the idiom is specifically about not getting angry. Key note: none of the options says 'stay calm' exactly, and being in a good mood is not quite the same as controlling anger; b stands as the closest meaning.", "topic": "Idiom: keep one's temper"}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from", "options": {"a": "Freud's idea that all behaviour was caused by unconscious motivational effects.", "b": "Jung's theory of psychological types.", "c": "the attempt to develop tests that did not rely on language.", "d": "accidentally spilling ink on a test booklet."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: projective tests grew out of Sigmund Freud's idea that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives that show themselves indirectly. Sigmund Freud (1896) described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his own unacceptable impulses to someone else. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious wishes, fears and conflicts shape everything we do, from dreams to slips of the tongue, and that direct questions cannot reach them because the person does not know them. Testing borrowed this logic. Give someone an ambiguous stimulus, such as an inkblot, a vague picture or an unfinished sentence, and he must impose his own meaning on it; in doing so he reveals his inner needs and conflicts. This is the projective hypothesis. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods, comparing them to an X-ray of personality. The best-known examples are Hermann Rorschach's inkblot test (1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935). Projective tests share an ambiguous stimulus, open responses, a disguised purpose and global interpretation. Example: shown a TAT card of a boy staring at a violin, Kabir tells a story about a son forced to practise by a strict father who later runs away from home; the psychologist notes a possible theme of conflict with authority. Option a is right because the core assumption of projective testing, that hidden unconscious motives leak into how we interpret ambiguity, comes directly from Freud's psychoanalytic view of unconscious motivation. Exam trap: Carl Jung did contribute a projective forerunner, the word association test (1910), so students pick b; but his theory of psychological types became the basis of an objective inventory, the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator.", "topic": "Origins of projective tests"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of research?", "options": {"a": "Research is systematic.", "b": "Research is not a process.", "c": "Research is problem-oriented.", "d": "Research is not inactive."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'research is not a process' is false, because research is precisely a systematic process, so b is the statement that is not a characteristic of research. Research is a careful, organised search for new facts or for answers to a problem. Its characteristics are usually listed as follows. It is systematic: it follows a planned sequence of steps, from identifying a problem, reviewing the literature and framing hypotheses to designing the study, collecting and analysing data and reporting conclusions. It is a process: each step leads to the next, and the findings raise new questions, so research is cyclical rather than a one-off act. It is problem-oriented: it starts from a question or a gap in knowledge and aims to answer it. It is active and dynamic: the researcher goes out and gathers evidence rather than waiting for it. It is also empirical, objective, controlled, replicable and critical. Fred Kerlinger (1964) summed this up as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation. Example: Arjun notices that many hostel students sleep badly before exams (problem), reads earlier studies, predicts that night-time phone use worsens sleep (hypothesis), surveys 200 students, analyses the data and reports. His results prompt a new question about screen light, which starts the cycle again. Option b is right because it denies a defining feature: research is always a process, so 'research is not a process' cannot describe it. Exam trap: option d is a double negative. 'Research is not inactive' means research is active, which is true, so d is a characteristic; students who skim see the word not and wrongly pick it.", "topic": "Characteristics of research"}, {"q": "Pia sees Arsi make a strong speech against legalized gambling. She learns that Arsi had free choice to make a speech on any issue she chose, and Pia knows that most of the students in Arsi's class are in favor of legalized gambling. Pia infers that Arsi truly is opposed to legalized gambling. This is most consistent with", "options": {"a": "the self-fulfilling prophecy", "b": "the correspondent inference theory.", "c": "the fundamental attribution error", "d": "the false-consensus effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Pia's confident inference that Arsi truly opposes legalised gambling fits correspondent inference theory. Attribution theory asks how we explain other people's behaviour. Correspondent inference theory, from Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965), asks when we can infer that an act corresponds to a stable inner disposition, that is, that the person really is the way she acted. They proposed three main cues. Choice: freely chosen behaviour tells us more than forced behaviour. Non-common effects: if the chosen action has a distinct consequence that other options lacked, that consequence points to the motive. Low social desirability: behaviour that goes against what is expected or popular reveals more than behaviour anyone would show. Apply the cues. Arsi chose her topic freely, and she argued against the view held by most of her classmates, so her speech was unpopular in that setting. Both cues point to a correspondent inference: she really is against gambling. Harold Kelley's (1972) augmenting principle says the same: behaviour that occurs despite pressure against it is attributed more strongly to the person. Example: if Kabir praises the canteen food while the owner is listening, you learn little; if he criticises it to the owner's face, you infer that he truly dislikes it. Option b is right because free choice plus unpopular behaviour are the exact conditions under which the theory predicts a confident dispositional inference. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is the near miss. It means inferring a disposition even when the situation forced the behaviour, as in the assigned essays of Edward Jones and Victor Harris (1967); Arsi had free choice, so Pia's inference is justified, not an error.", "topic": "Correspondent inference theory"}, {"q": "Counterconditioning is an exposure therapy technique which involves:", "options": {"a": "Learning that one negative event may be linked to another", "b": "Exposing the client to the feared stimulus", "c": "Learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening", "d": "Helping the client to see that their behaviour is counterproductive"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: counterconditioning teaches the client that a feared event or situation is no longer threatening, by linking it with a new response that is incompatible with fear. Counterconditioning comes from classical conditioning. A fear is a conditioned response: a once-neutral stimulus, such as a dog or a lift, has been paired with something frightening and now triggers anxiety. In counterconditioning the feared stimulus is paired instead with a pleasant or calming response that cannot coexist with fear, such as relaxation or eating. As the new association grows, the stimulus stops signalling danger and comes to evoke calm. Mary Cover Jones (1924) gave the classic demonstration: she brought a rabbit gradually closer to Peter, a boy of nearly three, while he ate food he liked, until he could play with it. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built the idea into systematic desensitisation and called the principle reciprocal inhibition: deep muscle relaxation is paired with each step of a graded fear hierarchy, from least to most frightening. Exposure is part of the method, but the extra ingredient is the competing response. Example: Riya panics in lifts. Her therapist teaches her progressive muscle relaxation and then, while she stays relaxed, has her imagine looking at a lift, then standing in one, then riding one floor, before practising in real lifts. Over sessions the lift becomes linked with calm, and Riya learns that it is no longer a threat. Option c is right because the end point of counterconditioning is new learning that the previously feared event is safe. Exam trap: b, exposing the client to the feared stimulus, is true of every exposure therapy, including flooding, so it does not capture what is special about counterconditioning, which is the replacement of fear by an incompatible response.", "topic": "Counterconditioning"}, {"q": "One of the essential characteristics of research is", "options": {"a": "Replicability.", "b": "Generalizability.", "c": "Usability.", "d": "Objectivity."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: replicability, the ability of a study to be repeated with the same results, is taken here as the essential characteristic of research. Replicability means that another researcher, following the same method with a similar sample, can repeat the study and should obtain similar findings. It requires that procedures, measures and analyses are described clearly enough to be copied. It matters because a single result can be a fluke of chance, of a particular sample or of the researcher's own expectations; only a finding that survives repetition becomes accepted knowledge. Replication is science's main tool of self-correction and the practical check on objectivity: if a result depends on who ran the study, it will not replicate. Fred Kerlinger (1964) defined research as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation, and replicability follows from all four. The Open Science Collaboration (2015), led by Brian Nosek, repeated 100 published psychology studies: about 97% of the originals had significant results, but only about 36% of the repetitions did, which showed how seriously the field must take replication. Example: Meera reports that a brief gratitude diary lifted mood in 60 college students in Chennai. Her finding becomes credible only when Kabir, following her published procedure with students in Kolkata, finds a similar effect. Option a is right because replicability turns one investigator's result into public, verifiable knowledge, and it separates research from one-off observation. Exam trap: generalisability is the near miss; it concerns whether findings extend to other people and settings (external validity), which is desirable but not essential, since a deliberately limited study can still be good research. Key note: objectivity (d) is also listed as a basic characteristic of research in most methods texts, so d has support; replicability is preferred because it is the public test of objectivity itself, and the key used here stands.", "topic": "Replicability in research"}, {"q": "Life time prevalence rate of OCD is", "options": {"a": "2.5%.", "b": "1%.", "c": "4.5%.", "d": "less than 1%."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is about 2.5%, the figure given in DSM-IV-TR (2000). Prevalence is the proportion of a population that has a disorder: point prevalence on a given day, period prevalence (such as 12-month) over a stated period, and lifetime prevalence at any time in life. Incidence, by contrast, counts only new cases in a period. Lifetime figures are always higher than 12-month figures, because they include people who have recovered. OCD is marked by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety, such as fear of contamination) and compulsions (repeated acts or mental rituals, such as washing or checking, done to reduce that anxiety), which take more than an hour a day or cause distress or impairment. DSM-IV-TR gave a lifetime prevalence of 2.5% and a 1-year prevalence of 0.5% to 2.1% in adults. The US National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) found 2.3% lifetime and 1.2% over 12 months. DSM-5 (2013) reports a 12-month prevalence of 1.2% in the United States and 1.1% to 1.8% internationally. Onset is usually in adolescence or early adulthood (mean about 19.5 years), earlier in boys. Example: in a town of 10,000 adults, about 250 people would have had OCD at some point in their lives, but only about 120 in the past year; Meera, a psychologist planning a clinic there, should use the 12-month figure for expected caseload. Option a is right because 2.5% is the standard lifetime estimate, and it agrees closely with the 2.3% found in the largest US survey. Exam trap: students confuse lifetime with 12-month prevalence and pick 1% or less; the roughly 1% figure is the 12-month rate. Key note: the marked copy of this paper highlights d (less than 1%), which is wrong for lifetime prevalence, so a is used here.", "topic": "Prevalence of OCD"}, {"q": "Faulty learning involves:", "options": {"a": "Acquiring psychological disorders through poor school attendance.", "b": "Acquiring psychological disorders through low self-esteem.", "c": "Acquiring psychological disorders by exposure to aversive stimuli.", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: faulty learning, the behavioural account of how disorders are acquired, is read broadly here, so all three routes count. The behavioural model holds that most abnormal behaviour is learned in the same way as normal behaviour, through classical conditioning, operant conditioning and observation; disorder reflects faulty (maladaptive) learning rather than disease or unconscious conflict. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) showed the classical route: Little Albert learned to fear a white rat after it was paired with a frightening noise, an exposure to an aversive stimulus. Operant learning adds the reinforcement of unhelpful behaviour, such as avoidance that relieves anxiety, and Albert Bandura (1977) added learning by watching others. Faulty learning also covers failing to learn. A child who misses school misses chances to acquire academic and social skills and may learn that avoidance pays, as in school refusal. A child who is repeatedly criticised learns a negative view of himself, low self-esteem, which then becomes a vulnerability to depression and anxiety. Example: Arjun was bitten by a dog at six and has avoided dogs ever since (aversive conditioning); he skipped school for months after being bullied, so he never learned to handle his peers; and constant comparison with his cousin taught him that he was worthless. Each is part of his learning history. Option d is right because, on the broad behavioural reading, all three are ways in which disorders can be acquired through experience and learning. Exam trap: students treat faulty learning as classical conditioning alone and choose c, overlooking avoidance learning and missed learning. Key note: c is the most direct example of faulty learning, since a and b name circumstances or traits rather than learning processes, so c has real support; the key used here takes the broad reading, and d stands.", "topic": "Faulty learning: behavioural model"}, {"q": "Which of the following bodily patterns does not typically accompany dreaming activity?", "options": {"a": "A distinctive EEG pattern.", "b": "Rapid eye movements.", "c": "High levels of cerebral blood level.", "d": "Higher levels of general muscle activity."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: higher general muscle activity does not accompany dreaming; during REM sleep, when most vivid dreams occur, the major muscles are almost paralysed. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and found that people woken from it usually reported vivid dreams. REM is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is limp. Its features are: (a) a distinctive EEG of fast, low-amplitude, desynchronised waves like waking, with sawtooth waves; (b) rapid, jerky eye movements under closed lids; (c) high brain activity, with cerebral blood flow and metabolism at or above waking levels in limbic and visual areas; plus irregular heart rate and breathing and genital arousal. The remaining feature is atonia: circuits in the pons inhibit the spinal motor neurons, so muscle tone (EMG) in the postural muscles is at its lowest of the night, apart from small twitches. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with small lesions in the pons entered REM without atonia and acted out apparent dreams, stalking and pouncing. In humans this failure is REM sleep behaviour disorder, described by Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986). Example: in a sleep lab, Kabir's recording at 4 a.m. shows waking-like EEG, darting eyes and a flat chin EMG; woken, he describes a vivid dream of running for a train, yet his legs never moved. Option d is right because dreaming in REM is accompanied by low, not high, muscle activity. Exam trap: option c is garbled ('cerebral blood level' for cerebral blood flow), which makes students pick it as the odd one out; but blood flow to the brain really is high in REM, so c does accompany dreaming.", "topic": "REM sleep and muscle atonia"}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "options": {"a": "Negative beliefs about self, world and future.", "b": "Negative beliefs about self, world and others.", "c": "Negative beliefs about self, others and future.", "d": "Negative beliefs about present, past, and future."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Aaron Temkin Beck (1967), a psychiatrist working with depressed patients, noticed that their thinking was coloured by a steady stream of automatic thoughts with the same three themes. The self is seen as defective or worthless (\"I am a failure\"). The world, or current experience, is seen as unfair, demanding and full of obstacles (\"nothing goes right for me; people let me down\"). The future is seen as hopeless (\"it will always be like this\"). Beck argued that these views come from deeper schemas formed in childhood, and that cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising and selective abstraction keep them going. He built cognitive therapy around testing these thoughts against evidence, set out in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (Beck and colleagues, 1979). Hopelessness, the future corner, is the part most linked to suicide risk, which is why Beck also built the Beck Hopelessness Scale (1974). Example: after failing one exam, Meera thinks \"I am stupid\" (self), \"the teachers have always been against me\" (world) and \"I will never get into any college\" (future); her therapist would ask her to list the evidence for and against each thought. Option a is right because it lists exactly Beck's three domains: self, world and future. Exam trap: the distractors swap one corner for a plausible word. \"Others\" is not a separate corner, because other people fall inside \"the world\", and \"past, present, future\" is a time line, not the triad. Do not confuse the triad with the three dimensions of attributional style (internal, stable, global) from Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978), or with the CBT triangle of thoughts, feelings and behaviours.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad"}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language", "options": {"a": "Linguistics.", "b": "Encoding.", "c": "Semantics.", "d": "Syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be studied at several levels. Phonology deals with sounds, the smallest unit being the phoneme (/p/ versus /b/ in pat and bat). Morphology deals with the morpheme, the smallest unit of meaning (\"cats\" has two: cat and -s). Syntax deals with the rules for combining words into sentences. Semantics deals with meaning: what words refer to, how word meanings relate (synonyms, opposites, categories) and how the meaning of a sentence is built from the meanings of its parts. Pragmatics, the last level, deals with how language is used in social context (\"Can you pass the salt?\" is a request, not a question about ability). The French linguist Michel Breal (1897) set out the field in his Essai de sémantique. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed how separate syntax and semantics are with his sentence \"Colourless green ideas sleep furiously\": the grammar is perfect, but the meaning is nonsense. In memory research, semantic memory, named by Endel Tulving (1972), is our store of general knowledge and word meanings, which is why the same root appears there. Example: when Kabir hears \"Riya sat on the bank\" and has to decide whether she sat by a river or at a building where money is kept, he is solving a semantic problem, since the words and grammar are clear and only the meaning is in doubt. Option c is right because semantics is, by definition, the branch of linguistics concerned with meaning. Exam trap: linguistics is the whole science of language, so it is the parent field, not the specific study of meaning; and syntax is about word order and grammar, not meaning. Anchor each term to its level: phonology is sound, syntax is form, semantics is meaning, pragmatics is use.", "topic": "Semantics and levels of language"}, {"q": "Type A and Type B Personality are associated with which personality inventory?", "options": {"a": "Self-Directed Search", "b": "Jenkins Activity Survey", "c": "Myers-Briggs", "d": "MMPI-2"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey is the inventory built to classify people as Type A or Type B. The story starts with two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were impatient, competitive and always racing the clock. They called this the Type A behaviour pattern: hard-driving competitiveness, time urgency and hostility. The relaxed, easy-going opposite was Type B. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 employed men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later work found hostility to be the most harmful part. Friedman and Rosenman first measured the pattern with a Structured Interview, in which the interviewer deliberately provokes impatience and watches speech and gestures. Because interviews are slow and need trained raters, C. David Jenkins, working with Rosenman and Friedman (1967), developed a self-report questionnaire, the Jenkins Activity Survey (JAS). Its standard form (Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Rosenman, 1979) has about 50 multiple-choice items and gives a Type A score plus three factors: speed and impatience, job involvement, and hard-driving competitiveness. Example: Arjun, a bank manager who eats lunch at his desk, finishes other people's sentences and hates queues, would score high on speed and impatience and be classed as Type A. Option b is right because the JAS was designed specifically to measure the Type A and Type B pattern. Exam trap: the word \"type\" pulls students toward the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, but its types come from Carl Jung's (1921) psychological types, not coronary-prone behaviour. Also do not confuse Type A with Type D (distressed: negative affectivity plus social inhibition) from Johan Denollet (1996).", "topic": "Type A: Jenkins Activity Survey"}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by", "options": {"a": "Binet and Simon.", "b": "Spearman.", "c": "Stanford and Binet.", "d": "Terman and Wechsler."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon developed the first practical intelligence test, the Binet-Simon Scale of 1905. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify schoolchildren who would not benefit from ordinary teaching and needed special classes. Earlier \"mental tests\" by Francis Galton (1884) in London and James McKeen Cattell (1890) in America had measured reaction time, grip strength and sensory sharpness, and these turned out to have little to do with school success. Alfred Binet (1905) and his collaborator Theodore Simon took a different route: they tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most normal children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911. William Stern (1912) then proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University multiplied this by 100 to give the ratio IQ in the Stanford-Binet. Example: if eight-year-old Riya passes the items that typical ten-year-olds pass, her mental age is 10 and her ratio IQ is 10 / 8 × 100 = 125. Option a is right because the Binet-Simon Scale (1905) is universally credited as the first intelligence test in the modern sense. Exam trap: \"Stanford and Binet\" turns a university into a person; the Stanford-Binet is Terman's later American revision, not the first test. Also separate the first mental test (Cattell's term, 1890, for sensory and reaction-time measures) from the first intelligence test (Binet and Simon, 1905).", "topic": "First intelligence test"}, {"q": "Find the odd man out: 3, 5, 11, 14, 17, 21", "options": {"a": "21.", "b": "17.", "c": "14.", "d": "3."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: 14 is the odd man out because it is the only even number in the series. Test each possible rule and count how many numbers it leaves out. Step 1, odd or even: 3, 5, 11, 17 and 21 are all odd; 14 is even (14 = 2 × 7). This rule leaves out exactly one number. Step 2, primes: 3, 5, 11 and 17 are prime, but 14 (2 × 7) and 21 (3 × 7) are both not prime, so this rule leaves out two numbers and cannot give a single answer. Step 3, differences: 2, 6, 3, 3 and 4 show no regular pattern, so no series rule applies. Option c is right because 14 is the single number that breaks the rule all the others follow. Exam trap: noticing that 21 is not prime tempts you towards option a, but a rule that excludes two numbers cannot define a unique odd one out; always check that your rule picks out exactly one term.", "topic": "Odd one out: number series"}, {"q": "Pick out the most effective word(s) from the given words to fill in the blank to make the sentence meaningfully complete. Fate smiles ______ those who untiringly grapple with stark realities of life.", "options": {"a": "with", "b": "over.", "c": "on.", "d": "round."}, "correct": "c", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is c: the correct sentence is \"Fate smiles on those who untiringly grapple with stark realities of life\", because \"smile on\" (or \"smile upon\") is the fixed English expression meaning to look on someone with favour or to bring them good luck. \"Smile at\" someone is the literal act of smiling towards a person. \"Smile on\" or \"smile upon\" someone is figurative and is usually used with luck, fate, fortune or the gods as the subject: \"Fortune smiled on Arjun when his last-minute application was accepted.\" The stem says that luck favours people who keep struggling with hard facts, so this sense of favour is needed. Option c is right because \"smile on\" is the only one of the four that expresses being favoured by fate. Exam trap: \"smile with\" sounds natural because people smile with joy or with their eyes, but it describes the manner of smiling, not favour shown to someone; \"over\" and \"round\" do not form this idiom at all.", "topic": "Prepositional idiom: smile on"}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of", "options": {"a": "verbal fluency.", "b": "visual perception.", "c": "memory.", "d": "attention."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is used mainly as a test of attention, in particular selective attention and the inhibition of an automatic response. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people to name the colour of the ink in which colour words were printed. When the word and the ink conflict (the word RED printed in green), naming the ink colour is much slower and more error-prone than naming plain colour patches. The reason is that reading is automatic and well practised, while naming ink colours is controlled and effortful; the automatic reading response pops up and must be suppressed. This slowing is the Stroop interference effect. The clinical version by Charles Golden (1978) has three pages (reading colour words printed in black, naming colour patches, and naming the ink of conflicting colour words), each timed for 45 seconds; the interference score compares the third page with the first two. Neuropsychologists use it to measure selective attention, response inhibition and executive control, linked to the anterior cingulate cortex and the frontal lobes, and it is sensitive to ADHD, frontal injury and schizophrenia. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads colour words quickly but stumbles and corrects himself again and again on the conflicting page, a sign that he cannot hold back the automatic reading response. Option d is right because the task demands attending to one feature (ink colour) while ignoring another (the word), which is the core of selective attention. Exam trap: the test uses words and colours, so verbal fluency or visual perception look plausible, but a person with perfect reading and colour vision can still fail it. It is often called a test of executive function; in a list without that term, choose attention.", "topic": "Stroop test and attention"}, {"q": "The correct sequence of stages of research is", "options": {"a": "Problem selection, data collection, statement of hypothesis.", "b": "Data collection, problem selection, statement of hypothesis, data analysis.", "c": "Problem selection, hypothesis statement, data collection, data analysis.", "d": "Hypothesis statement, problem selection, data collection, data analysis."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: research moves from selecting a problem to stating a hypothesis, then collecting data and finally analysing them. This is the logic of the scientific (hypothetico-deductive) method. You cannot form a hypothesis until you know what problem you are studying, and you cannot collect meaningful data until the hypothesis tells you what to measure and in whom. Analysis comes last, because there is nothing to analyse until data exist. C. R. Kothari (1985), in Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, lays out the full flow: formulating the research problem, an extensive literature survey, developing the hypotheses, preparing the research design, deciding the sample design, collecting data, executing the project, analysing data, testing hypotheses, generalisation and interpretation, and preparing the report. Example: Arjun notices that his hostel mates seem anxious before exams (problem selection); after reading the literature he predicts that students who sleep less than six hours will report higher test anxiety (hypothesis); he gives a sleep diary and an anxiety scale to 200 students (data collection); then he runs a t-test comparing short and normal sleepers (data analysis). Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps all four steps in their logical order. Exam trap: the distractors use the same words but shuffle their order, so read every position; option a looks short and tidy but puts the hypothesis after the data and leaves out analysis altogether. In qualitative, theory-building work such as grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967), ideas may emerge from the data, but the standard quantitative sequence is the one being tested here.", "topic": "Steps of the research process"}, {"q": "Newborns", "options": {"a": "Prefer facial images to mixed, facial elements.", "b": "Habituate almost instantly to novel stimuli.", "c": "Have 20/40 eyesight.", "d": "Have depth perception."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: newborns prefer to look at face-like images over images that contain the same facial features in a scrambled arrangement. Robert Fantz (1961) introduced the preferential looking method: two pictures are shown side by side and an observer times how long the baby looks at each. Infants from a few days to 6 months old looked longest at a face, less at patterns such as newsprint and a bull's-eye, and least at plain discs. Carolyn Goren, Merrill Sarty and Paul Wu (1975) went further with babies only minutes old: when a schematic face was moved slowly across their view, newborns turned their eyes and heads further to follow it than to follow a scrambled face made of the same eyes, nose and mouth, or a blank head shape. Mark Johnson and John Morton (1991) replicated this and proposed an inborn mechanism (CONSPEC) that orients newborns to the basic layout of two eyes above a mouth, later joined by learned face expertise (CONLERN). So it is the arrangement, not just the parts, that attracts the newborn. Example: when baby Meera, two days old, is shown a card with a simple face and another with the same features jumbled, she follows the face card further with her eyes. Option a is right because the preference for organised faces over scrambled facial elements is present from birth. Exam trap: the other options sound like early abilities but each is overstated. Newborn acuity is about 20/200 to 20/600, not 20/40; newborns do habituate, but slowly; and depth perception is demonstrated on the visual cliff only by crawling age.", "topic": "Newborn face preference"}, {"q": "Separation anxiety occurs when the newborn is", "options": {"a": "1 to 2 months old.", "b": "3 to 4 months old", "c": "8 to 15 months old.", "d": "24 to 30 months old."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: separation anxiety, distress when the main caregiver leaves, appears at about 8 months and peaks at about 13 to 15 months, so 8 to 15 months is the best answer. It needs two developments. First, the infant must have formed a specific attachment to one or a few people; in the Glasgow study of H. Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), specific attachments appeared at about 7 to 9 months. Second, the infant's memory must be good enough to notice that someone is absent and to expect them, which links to object permanence in the theory of Jean Piaget (1954). John Bowlby (1969) placed this in his phase of clear-cut attachment, from about 6 to 8 months to 18 to 24 months, when the baby protests the departure of the attachment figure and uses her as a secure base. Jerome Kagan and colleagues (1978) found the same curve in several very different cultures: protest rises from about 8 months, peaks around 13 to 15 months, then declines as toddlers learn that the caregiver comes back. Stranger anxiety, which Rene Spitz (1965) called eight-month anxiety, emerges a little earlier, at about 6 to 8 months. Example: at 11 months, Kabir plays happily while his mother is in the room, but cries the moment she walks out to the kitchen and calms once she returns. Option c is right because 8 to 15 months covers both the onset and the peak of separation protest. Exam trap: 1 to 2 months is the age of the social smile and 3 to 4 months of laughter, when babies still accept any caring adult; distress that stays excessive well beyond the toddler years is separation anxiety disorder, a DSM-5 diagnosis, not the normal milestone.", "topic": "Separation anxiety: age of onset"}, {"q": "To teach a child to eat spaghetti, you would reinforce initial responses such as holding the fork and then increasingly closer approximations to the final response. The procedure is known as:", "options": {"a": "Counter conditioning.", "b": "Successive approximations.", "c": "Shaping.", "d": "Chaining."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: reinforcing first rough attempts and then responses that come closer and closer to the final behaviour is called shaping. Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner (1953) used shaping to build behaviours an animal would never emit on its own. To teach a rat to press a lever, the trainer first reinforces facing the lever, then approaching it, then touching it, and finally only pressing it. At each step the requirement for reinforcement is raised, so responses that were reinforced before are no longer enough: this is differential reinforcement. Shaping is an operant technique because it works through consequences, not through pairing stimuli. In therapy it is used to build speech in children with autism (first eye contact, then any sound, then a syllable, then the word), and it is part of everyday teaching. Example: Riya teaches her four-year-old nephew Kabir to eat spaghetti. At first she praises him just for holding the fork; once he does that easily, she praises him only for putting the fork into the spaghetti; later only for twirling it; and finally only for getting a mouthful in without help. Option c is right because shaping is the name of the whole procedure described in the stem. Exam trap: \"successive approximations\" (option b) describes how shaping works, the steps that get reinforced, so it looks correct; but the stem asks for the name of the procedure, and that name is shaping. Do not confuse shaping with chaining, which links several different, already learned responses into a sequence.", "topic": "Shaping by successive approximations"}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "options": {"a": "Voluntary movement, intuitive thought, object permanence.", "b": "Concrete operations, mental representation, information processing.", "c": "Mental representation, symbolic thought, intuitive thought.", "d": "Conservation, object permanence, intuitive thought."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "topic": "Piaget: sequence of cognitive development"}, {"q": "If two presynaptic neurons coincidentally stimulate a postsynaptic neuron, their combined effects on that neuron are described by a process called", "options": {"a": "Soma summation.", "b": "Temporal summation.", "c": "Cholinergic summation.", "d": "Spatial summation."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: when inputs from two different presynaptic neurons reach a postsynaptic neuron at the same time and add together, the process is spatial summation. A neuron receives thousands of synapses on its dendrites and cell body. Each input produces a small, graded change called a postsynaptic potential: an excitatory one (EPSP) slightly depolarises the membrane and an inhibitory one (IPSP) slightly hyperpolarises it. One EPSP alone is usually too small to fire the neuron; an action potential follows only if the combined potentials at the axon hillock reach threshold. The adding up happens in two ways. In spatial summation, potentials from different locations (different synapses, usually from different neurons) arrive together and combine; an EPSP and an IPSP arriving together can cancel out. In temporal summation, potentials from the same synapse arrive in quick succession and pile up before the first has faded. Charles Sherrington (1906) inferred both from dog reflexes in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System: one weak pinch on the foot produced no withdrawal, but two weak pinches at nearby points at the same moment did (spatial), and so did repeated weak pinches at one point in quick succession (temporal). John Eccles (1952) later recorded these potentials directly with electrodes inside motor neurons. Example: neither neuron A nor neuron B alone can make Meera's motor neuron fire, but when both fire together their EPSPs add up and cross threshold. Option d is right because the inputs come from two neurons at once, which is summation across space. Exam trap: \"coincidentally\" means \"at the same time\", and the idea of time pulls students towards temporal summation; but temporal summation needs repeated input from one source.", "topic": "Spatial versus temporal summation"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the highest level of cognitive ability?", "options": {"a": "Evaluating", "b": "Knowing", "c": "Understanding", "d": "Analysing"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: of the four options, evaluating is the highest level of cognitive ability in Bloom's taxonomy. Benjamin Bloom (1956) and his colleagues wrote the Taxonomy of Educational Objectives to give teachers a shared ladder of thinking skills. Its cognitive domain has six levels, from simplest to most complex: knowledge (recalling facts), comprehension (understanding and explaining), application (using knowledge in a new situation), analysis (breaking material into parts and seeing how they relate), synthesis (combining parts into a new whole) and evaluation (judging the value of ideas or work against criteria). Each level assumes the ones below it: you cannot judge an argument you cannot understand. Teachers use these levels to write learning objectives and to build a test blueprint, a grid of content against levels, so that a paper does not test memory alone. Lorin Anderson and David Krathwohl (2001) revised the taxonomy using verbs: remembering, understanding, applying, analysing, evaluating and creating. Example: Kabir reading a research article first recalls what a p-value is (remembering), explains it in his own words (understanding), computes one for his own data (applying), separates the study's design from its conclusions (analysing), and finally judges whether the conclusions are justified by the evidence (evaluating). Option a is right because evaluating sits above knowing, understanding and analysing in both versions of the taxonomy. Exam trap: in the 2001 revision creating sits above evaluating, so evaluating is not the very top there; but creating is not offered here, and in Bloom's original list evaluation is the summit. Knowing is the lowest level, so do not be misled by its sounding fundamental.", "topic": "Bloom's taxonomy: highest level"}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called", "options": {"a": "proactive memories.", "b": "reactive memories.", "c": "explicit memories.", "d": "implicit memories."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: memories that influence behaviour without conscious awareness are implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) introduced the pair of terms. Explicit memory is conscious, intentional recollection, tested by recall and recognition (\"What did you have for breakfast?\"). Implicit memory shows itself only in performance: past experience changes how a person acts, feels or thinks, even though the person does not remember the experience or is unaware that it is having an effect. Its main forms are procedural memory (skills such as riding a bicycle or typing), priming (a word seen earlier is completed or recognised faster later) and simple classical conditioning. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) drew a similar line between declarative knowing that and procedural knowing how. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Elizabeth Warrington and Lawrence Weiskrantz (1970) found that amnesic patients who could not recall a list of words still completed word fragments with those words about as well as healthy people. Henry Molaison (H.M.), studied by Brenda Milner (1962), improved at mirror drawing over three days while denying he had ever done the task. Example: Riya cannot tell you the order of the keys on her phone keypad, yet her thumbs type her friend's number in a second; the knowledge is there, but outside awareness. Option d is right because implicit memory is defined by the absence of conscious recollection. Exam trap: explicit memory is the exact opposite; \"proactive\" is borrowed from proactive interference (old learning disrupting new) or confused with prospective memory (remembering to do something in future); and \"reactive memory\" is not a standard memory system at all.", "topic": "Implicit versus explicit memory"}, {"q": "White matter is primarily composed of", "options": {"a": "Myelinated axons.", "b": "Myelinated dendrites.", "c": "Unmyelinated axons.", "d": "Unmyelinated dendrites."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: white matter is made up mainly of myelinated axons. When fresh brain or spinal cord is sliced, two tissues are visible. Grey matter consists mostly of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, synapses and glial cells; it is where information is processed. White matter consists mostly of axons wrapped in myelin, a fatty insulating sheath made by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system. The fat in myelin is what makes the tissue look white. Myelin allows saltatory conduction, in which the impulse jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next, so myelinated axons carry signals far faster than bare ones. White matter is therefore the brain's wiring: bundles of axons called tracts connect one area to another, the largest being the corpus callosum that joins the two hemispheres. The arrangement differs by region: in the cerebrum grey matter forms the outer cortex with white matter beneath, while in the spinal cord the butterfly-shaped grey matter lies inside and white matter surrounds it. Disease shows why white matter matters: in multiple sclerosis the immune system attacks myelin in the brain and spinal cord, slowing or blocking conduction. Example: Meera's MRI shows bright patches in the white matter near her ventricles; her neurologist explains that these are areas of damaged myelin, which is why signals to her legs are slowed. Option a is right because myelinated axons are the main component of white matter. Exam trap: dendrites are never myelinated, so any option with \"myelinated dendrites\" is impossible; and unmyelinated axons belong to grey matter, so options a and c differ by one word, which decides the answer.", "topic": "White and grey matter"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements about the typical pattern of sleep cycle is false?", "options": {"a": "The first stage of sleep is Stage 1.", "b": "Infants spend more time in REM sleep than do adults.", "c": "We most often naturally awaken during Stage 4 sleep.", "d": "Near the end of a good night's sleep, a person spends most of his or her time in Stages 1, 2 and REM."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the false statement is that we most often wake naturally from Stage 4 sleep; Stage 4 is the deepest sleep, the hardest to wake from, and it is mostly over by the second half of the night. The modern study of sleep began when Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, and William Dement and Kleitman (1957) described the cycle. Sleep begins with Stage 1, a light, drowsy transition with theta waves, then Stage 2 with sleep spindles and K-complexes, then Stages 3 and 4, slow-wave sleep with large delta waves (merged as N3 in the 2007 scoring rules). The sleeper then climbs back up and enters REM, the stage of vivid dreams and muscle atonia. Each cycle lasts about 90 minutes, giving four to six cycles a night. Slow-wave sleep dominates the early cycles and fades later, while REM periods lengthen towards morning. So the true statements are: a, sleep starts in Stage 1; b, infants spend more time in REM than adults (about half of a newborn's sleep against 20 to 25% in adults); and d, near the end of the night most time is spent in Stages 1, 2 and REM. We usually wake from REM or light sleep. Example: if Arjun's alarm rings an hour after he falls asleep, it drags him out of deep Stage 4 and he feels groggy and confused (sleep inertia); when he wakes by himself at dawn, it is usually from REM or Stage 2, and he often remembers a dream. Option c is right because it is the only false statement. Exam trap: in a \"false\" question the true statements look like plain facts, and the false one hides a single wrong stage name; always check where in the night each stage dominates.", "topic": "Sleep stages and cycles"}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to", "options": {"a": "reduce inappropriate behaviour.", "b": "punish inappropriate behaviour.", "c": "reinforce appropriate behaviour.", "d": "guide behaviour."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "topic": "Definition of ethics"}, {"q": "Which one of the following belongs to the category of homogenous data?", "options": {"a": "Multi-storeyed houses in a colony.", "b": "Trees in a garden.", "c": "Vehicular traffic on a highway.", "d": "Student population in a class."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a student population in a class is the best example of homogeneous data, because its members are alike on the main characteristics that matter. Data or a population are homogeneous when the units are similar to one another on the characteristics being studied, and heterogeneous when the units vary widely. Students in one class are usually of nearly the same age, have reached the same educational level, follow the same syllabus and are taught by the same teachers, so they form a fairly uniform group. By contrast, the houses in a colony differ in number of storeys, size, design and age even if all are multi-storeyed; the trees in a garden belong to different species and differ in height, age and shape; and highway traffic mixes cycles, two-wheelers, cars, buses and trucks moving at different speeds. Homogeneity matters for sampling. The more homogeneous a population, the smaller the sample needed to represent it, since any member is much like any other. With a heterogeneous population a larger sample is needed, or the population is divided into homogeneous subgroups (strata) and each is sampled separately: stratified random sampling, whose most precise allocation was worked out by Jerzy Neyman (1934). Example: if Kabir wants the average reading speed of the Class 8 students in one school, a small random sample of that class will do; to study reading speed across all ages in Kolkata, he would need a much larger, stratified sample. Option d is right because, of the four, only the students in a class share age, level and setting. Exam trap: \"multi-storeyed\" makes option a sound like a group defined by a common feature, but sharing one label does not make units homogeneous when they still differ widely on everything else.", "topic": "Homogeneous versus heterogeneous data"}, {"q": "A failure to develop a consistent identity results in", "options": {"a": "isolation.", "b": "inferiority.", "c": "role confusion.", "d": "stagnation."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Erik Erikson's theory, failing to build a consistent identity in adolescence results in role confusion. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole life span. Each stage is a crisis, a turning point between a healthy and an unhealthy outcome, set by the demands society makes at that age. Stage 5, at about 12 to 18 years, is identity versus role confusion (also called identity diffusion). Faced with a changing body and new demands, the adolescent must pull childhood identifications, abilities, values and goals together into one continuous sense of who he or she is and where life is going. Success brings the virtue of fidelity, the ability to stay loyal to chosen values and people. Failure leaves a person unsure of who they are, drifting between roles and unable to commit to an occupation, beliefs or relationships. Erikson (1968) developed the idea in Identity: Youth and Crisis, describing a psychosocial moratorium, a socially allowed delay for exploring. James Marcia (1966) later turned it into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Example: at 17, Kabir switches from wanting to be an engineer to a musician to a cricketer within a month, and changes his views to match whichever friends he is with; he has no stable sense of self yet, the picture of role confusion. Option c is right because role confusion is Erikson's named negative pole of the identity stage. Exam trap: the other options are the negative outcomes of neighbouring stages, so all are real Erikson terms. Isolation is especially tempting because Erikson held that true intimacy needs a firm identity first, but isolation is the failure of the next stage, not of identity itself.", "topic": "Erikson: identity versus role confusion"}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Implicit learning", "c": "Operant conditioning", "d": "Client centred therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the token economy is based on operant conditioning, in which behaviour is changed by its consequences. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely. A token economy applies this to a whole ward, classroom or home. Clearly defined target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework) earn tokens such as points, stars or plastic chips. The tokens are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers: they have no value in themselves but can be exchanged later for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because tokens can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken away for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) built the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois: long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work nearly stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Gordon Paul and Robert Lentz (1977) later found that a social learning programme built on tokens helped long-stay patients with schizophrenia more than milieu therapy did. Example: in a children's hostel, Arjun earns a star for each morning he is ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option c is right because the method rests on reinforcement contingencies, the core of operant conditioning. Exam trap: Skinner supplied the principles, but the token economy as a ward programme belongs to Ayllon and Azrin; and although tokens gain value by being paired with backup rewards, the technique as a whole is operant, not classical.", "topic": "Token economy and operant conditioning"}, {"q": "Incidence of dementia in 65 years old is approx.", "options": {"a": "0.7%.", "b": "17%.", "c": "37%.", "d": "27%."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the yearly incidence of dementia at about age 65 is roughly 0.7% to 1%, so 0.7% is the best answer. Two measures are easily mixed up. Incidence counts new cases arising during a period, usually one year, among people who did not have the disorder at the start. Prevalence counts all existing cases, old and new, at a point or over a period. For a long-lasting condition like dementia, prevalence is always much larger than yearly incidence, because cases build up over the years (prevalence is roughly incidence multiplied by average duration). Dementia is strongly tied to age. Pooled studies such as the meta-analysis by Anthony Jorm and Daniel Jolley (1998) show that incidence is below 1% a year at 65 to 69 and roughly doubles with every 5 to 6 years of age, reaching several per cent a year in the late 80s. Prevalence follows the same steep curve: about 5% of all people aged 65 and over have dementia, with Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), the commonest cause. Example: in a town with 10,000 people aged 65 to 69 who are free of dementia this year, about 70 to 100 will develop it over the next 12 months; that is incidence. If you counted everyone over 65 who already has dementia today, you would find a much larger share, about one in twenty; that is prevalence. Option a is right because 0.7% matches the yearly rate of new cases at around 65. Exam trap: the large figures (17%, 27%, 37%) are prevalence-sized numbers seen only in the very old, people in their late 80s and 90s, and even then they count existing cases, not new ones; check whether the stem says incidence or prevalence before choosing.", "topic": "Dementia incidence versus prevalence"}, {"q": "Type I error occurs when", "options": {"a": "When a false negative occurs.", "b": "When a false positive occurs.", "c": "Both the above.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a Type I error is a false positive, concluding that an effect exists when in reality it does not. Hypothesis testing starts from the null hypothesis (H0), the claim of no effect or no difference. After collecting data, the researcher either rejects H0 or fails to reject it. Since the truth is unknown, two kinds of wrong decision are possible, as Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) set out. A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis: declaring a difference that is not really there, a false positive. Its probability is alpha, the significance level fixed before the study, usually .05, a 5% risk of a false alarm when H0 is true. A Type II error is failing to reject a false null hypothesis: missing a real effect, a false negative. Its probability is beta, and power (1 minus beta, conventionally at least .80) is the chance of detecting a real effect. The two errors trade off: a stricter alpha (say .01) lowers Type I errors but raises Type II errors unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Meera tests whether a yoga programme reduces anxiety. Her t-test gives p = .03, so she rejects H0 and reports that yoga works. If in truth the programme has no effect and her result came from chance, she has made a Type I error. If instead yoga really works but her small sample gives p = .20 and she concludes it does not, that is a Type II error. Option b is right because a false positive is the definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: think of the boy who cried wolf: the villagers who first believed him made a Type I error (a false alarm), and those who later ignored him when the wolf really came made a Type II error (a miss).", "topic": "Type I and Type II errors"}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that", "options": {"a": "Moral children always adhere to generally recognized moral standards.", "b": "Children seem to apply situational morality, acting differently in separate situations involving the same moral principle.", "c": "Moral children never cheat.", "d": "Boys generally have stronger consciences than girls."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the Character Education Inquiry, Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928 to 1930) found that children's honesty was largely situation-specific: the same child might cheat in one setting and be honest in another, even though both involved the same moral principle. Working at Teachers College, Columbia University, they asked whether children have a general trait of honesty. They gave more than 10,000 schoolchildren cleverly disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in many settings: in class, at home, at parties and in athletic contests. For instance, children could secretly change answers on a test they marked themselves, or keep coins from a box. Almost every child cheated in some situations and not in others, and cheating in one situation predicted cheating in another only weakly. Knowing moral rules, or attending character education and Sunday school, barely predicted honest conduct. Hartshorne and May called this the doctrine of specificity: behaviour depends heavily on the particular situation, such as the risk of being caught and the effort needed. The study became a cornerstone of the critique of trait consistency by Walter Mischel (1968), which started the person-situation debate. Later, Roger Burton (1963) reanalysed the data and found a small general factor of honesty, and Seymour Epstein (1979) showed that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more consistent. Example: Arjun copies answers in a class test when the teacher steps out, yet returns the extra fifty rupees a shopkeeper gives him by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situational morality. Exam trap: \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c state the opposite of what the study found; moral behaviour was inconsistent across situations, not fixed.", "topic": "Hartshorne and May: honesty studies"}, {"q": "The Whitehall study indicated an association between coronary heart disease and which of the following:", "options": {"a": "Lack of training for the job.", "b": "Low job control.", "c": "Anger management issues.", "d": "Depression."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Whitehall studies of British civil servants linked coronary heart disease to low job control. The first Whitehall study, begun in 1967 with about 17,500 male civil servants in London, found a steep social gradient: men in the lowest employment grades, such as messengers and doorkeepers, were several times more likely to die of coronary heart disease than men in the top administrative grades, even though all had secure office jobs and free medical care. Smoking, blood pressure, cholesterol and obesity explained only part of this gap. Michael Marmot and colleagues then began Whitehall II in 1985 with about 10,000 men and women. In their 1997 paper in The Lancet, low job control (little say over how and when one's work is done, also called decision latitude) predicted new coronary heart disease independently of grade and the usual risk factors, and it accounted for a large part of the grade difference. High job demands did not show the same link. The idea draws on the job demand-control model of Robert Karasek (1979), which holds that the most stressful jobs combine high demands with low control. Example: Kabir, a junior clerk who must process files in the order and at the pace set by others, has lower job control than his section officer, who decides her own priorities; the study suggests that Kabir carries the higher heart risk. Option b is right because low job control was the named psychosocial predictor of heart disease in Whitehall. Exam trap: hostility and anger (the toxic part of the Type A pattern of Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, 1959) and depression are real heart-disease risk factors in other research, which makes c and d tempting; but the Whitehall finding concerns control at work.", "topic": "Whitehall study and job control"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jepas-2020": [{"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the", "concept": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the body's involuntary organs: heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut, sweat glands, pupils and the adrenal glands. It works without conscious intention and has two branches. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action; Walter Bradford Cannon (1915) called this the fight-or-flight response. Heart rate and blood pressure rise, breathing quickens, pupils widen, digestion stops and the adrenal medulla pours adrenaline into the blood. The parasympathetic branch, working largely through the vagus nerve, does the opposite (rest and digest) and calms the body once the danger has passed. Commands reach the ANS from the hypothalamus, which receives alarm signals from the amygdala. These brain areas are the controllers higher up, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) even argued that the feeling of an emotion is our perception of these autonomic changes. Example: Kabir survives a bus crash. His heart pounds, his palms sweat, his mouth goes dry and his breathing races; weeks later a screeching horn sets off the same surge. Each sign is sympathetic arousal, and the lasting hyperarousal of post-traumatic stress disorder is largely autonomic. Option c is right because the ANS is the direct effector of the visceral, involuntary reactions that make up an emotional response to trauma. Exam trap: \"most directly\" points to the system that carries out the response, not the higher centres. The CNS and the cortex appraise the event and send commands, but they act on the organs through the ANS; the somatic system moves skeletal muscles under voluntary control.", "traps": {"a": "The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and sends voluntary commands to skeletal muscles. Running away uses it, but the pounding heart, sweating and dry mouth of an emotional response are involuntary, autonomic events.", "b": "The central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) contains the controllers, the amygdala and hypothalamus, but it is too broad an answer; it acts on the body through the autonomic system, which is the direct controller.", "d": "The cerebral cortex appraises the meaning of the event and can partly regulate emotion (the prefrontal cortex can damp the amygdala), but it does not directly drive heart rate, sweating or the adrenal glands."}}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence.", "concept": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Bernard Cattell (1963), later with John Leonard Horn (1966), split the general factor g of Charles Spearman (1904) into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on what you already know: seeing patterns, working out rules, holding and juggling information. It is tested by tasks such as Raven's Progressive Matrices. It leans heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then declines steadily. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary, skills and strategies built up through education and experience. It is tested by vocabulary, general information and comprehension items, and it holds steady or keeps rising into the sixties and beyond. In Cattell's investment theory, fluid ability invested in learning becomes crystallised ability, so the two are related but age differently. The Seattle Longitudinal Study of K. Warner Schaie (begun 1956), which followed the same adults for decades, confirmed that perceptual speed and fluid reasoning fall earliest, while verbal ability is kept well into late adulthood. Example: Meera's grandmother, aged 72, is slow with a new mobile banking app and struggles with timed puzzles, but she beats everyone at word games and gives wise advice on family disputes. Her fluid ability has declined; her crystallised ability has not. Option c is right because it places the decline on fluid intelligence and the stability on crystallised intelligence, which is the classic Horn and Cattell finding. Exam trap: the reversal in d is the commonest slip, and \"fixed\" in a and b is not a type of intelligence at all; it borrows from the fixed mindset of Carol Dweck (2006).", "traps": {"a": "Fluid intelligence does decline, but \"fixed intelligence\" is not a recognised type; the word echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, which is a belief about ability, not an ability.", "b": "This reverses the direction and again uses the made-up \"fixed\" intelligence; fluid ability is the one that declines with age, so it cannot be the one that is spared.", "d": "This is the exact reversal: crystallised intelligence (vocabulary, knowledge) is the one that holds or rises with age, while fluid reasoning falls."}}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "traps": {"a": "Block design belongs to Samuel C. Kohs (1920), whose coloured cubes are arranged to copy printed patterns; David Wechsler later borrowed it as a subtest. It is a performance test, but not the one Porteus built.", "c": "Non-verbal cues are body language and facial signals studied in communication research; no classic performance test is built on them, and the phrase only sounds like \"non-verbal test\".", "d": "Drawing a person is Florence Goodenough's Draw-a-Man test (1926), revised by Dale Harris (1963); it estimates intellectual maturity from the details in a drawing, not from maze tracing."}}, {"q": "The function of mass communication of supplying information regarding the processes, issues, events and societal developments is known as", "concept": "The answer is b: supplying information about the processes, issues, events and developments in society is the surveillance function of mass communication. Mass communication is the sending of messages to large, scattered audiences through media such as newspapers, radio, television and the internet. Harold Lasswell (1948), in his essay on the structure and function of communication in society, named three social functions. Surveillance of the environment means watching the world and reporting what is happening: news, weather warnings, election results, price rises, the outbreak of a disease. Correlation means interpreting that information and linking the parts of society in their response: editorials, analysis and commentary that tell people what an event means and what to do about it. Transmission of the social heritage means passing values and culture on to new members of society, which overlaps with socialisation. Charles Wright (1959) later added a fourth function, entertainment. Example: when Riya reads that a cyclone will reach the Odisha coast tomorrow, the media are performing surveillance; when an editorial argues that the state's evacuation plan is too slow, that is correlation. Option b is right because the stem describes the supply of information about events and developments, which is exactly Lasswell's surveillance function. Exam trap: correlation sounds like \"connecting information\", but it means interpreting the news and shaping society's response, not supplying the facts themselves.", "traps": {"a": "Content supply is not a function in Harold Lasswell's (1948) or Charles Wright's (1959) lists; it is a plausible-sounding phrase built from the stem's word \"supplying\".", "c": "Gratification comes from the uses and gratifications approach (Elihu Katz, Jay Blumler and Michael Gurevitch, 1974), which asks what needs audiences satisfy through media; it concerns the audience's motives, not the supply of news.", "d": "Correlation is Harold Lasswell's second function: interpreting events and coordinating society's response through editorials and commentary. It works on information that surveillance has already supplied."}}, {"q": "A young child learns that Daddy has two legs, spectacles, and is a bit balding. One day on the street, while passing a balding man, the child says \"Daddy\". This is a case of", "concept": "The answer is d: calling a balding stranger \"Daddy\" is a case of overgeneralising, which in language development is called overextension. When children learn a new word, they do not yet know its full adult meaning. Eve Clark (1973), in her semantic feature hypothesis, proposed that a young child stores a word with only a few striking perceptual features. Here the child's meaning of \"Daddy\" is roughly \"grown-up man, spectacles, bald\". Anyone who shares those features gets the label, so the word is applied too widely. Overextension is common between about 1 and 2½ years, while vocabulary is small: \"dog\" for every four-legged animal, \"moon\" for any round light, \"ball\" for an orange. Its opposite is underextension, using a word too narrowly (\"cat\" only for the family cat). A related grammatical error is overregularisation, applying a rule such as adding \"-ed\" to irregular verbs (\"I goed\", \"two foots\"). Both errors show active learning, not imitation, because adults rarely say them. Example: Arjun, aged 18 months, learns \"Appa\" for his father and then greets the postman, a neighbour and an uncle with \"Appa\"; as words such as \"uncle\" and \"man\" arrive, the label narrows to his father. Option d is right because the child has taken a word learned for one person and stretched it to others who share some of his features, which is overgeneralisation of a word's meaning. Exam trap: \"extending a morpheme\" sounds close, but over-applying a grammatical morpheme is overregularisation (\"goed\"); the Daddy error concerns word meaning, not grammar.", "traps": {"a": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution, as in long division; it belongs to problem solving, and a toddler naming people is not following one.", "b": "\"Semantic logarithm\" is not a psychological term at all; it joins semantics (meaning) to a mathematical word so that it sounds technical.", "c": "A morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning, and over-applying a grammatical morpheme such as \"-ed\" is overregularisation. The Daddy error stretches a word's meaning, not a grammatical ending."}}, {"q": "In how many different ways can the letters of the word 'LEADING' be arranged in such a way that the vowels always come together?", "concept": "The answer is c: the letters of LEADING can be arranged in 720 ways with the vowels always together. Step 1: list the letters. LEADING has 7 letters, all different: L, E, A, D, I, N, G. The vowels are E, A and I (3 vowels); the consonants are L, D, N and G (4 consonants). Step 2: glue the vowels into one block. If the vowels must always come together, treat (EAI) as a single unit. Now there are 4 consonants plus 1 vowel block, which makes 5 units. Step 3: arrange the 5 units. Five different units can be arranged in 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 ways. Step 4: arrange the vowels inside the block. The three vowels can be ordered among themselves in 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 ways (EAI, EIA, AEI, AIE, IEA, IAE). Step 5: multiply, because every order of the units can be combined with every order inside the block: 120 × 6 = 720. Check: LDAIENG is one valid arrangement, because A, I and E sit side by side. Option c is right because 5! × 3! = 720. Exam trap: 5040 is 7!, the count with no restriction at all; forgetting step 4 gives only 120; and halving 720 to 360 happens when a solver wrongly divides as if a letter were repeated, which none is in LEADING.", "traps": {"a": "360 is half of 720; it comes from wrongly dividing by 2!, as if LEADING had a repeated letter, but all seven letters are different.", "b": "480 does not follow from any correct method; it is 120 × 4, the slip of multiplying by the number of consonants instead of by 3! for the vowel order.", "d": "5040 is 7! = 7 × 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1, the number of arrangements of LEADING with no condition; it ignores the rule that the vowels stay together."}}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as", "concept": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Transposition is a Gestalt idea: a melody keeps its identity when every note is shifted (Christian von Ehrenfels, 1890), and the animals of Wolfgang Kohler (1918) learned relations such as \"the lighter one\" rather than absolute stimuli. It is not sensory coding.", "b": "Transaction appears in the transactional analysis of Eric Berne (1961) and the transactional model of stress of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984); it means an exchange between people, or between person and environment, not energy conversion.", "d": "Transition is an everyday word for any change from one state to another, as in life transitions; it is not the name for how receptors code a stimulus."}}, {"q": "A student is trying to solve a problem without the help of his teacher. The teacher must", "concept": "The answer is d: the teacher should appreciate the student's personal efforts. Teaching aptitude items test whether you know what good teaching looks like in practice. When a student tries to solve a problem alone, he is showing initiative, self-reliance and intrinsic motivation, the very qualities education tries to grow. Praising the effort reinforces that behaviour, so it becomes more likely next time; this is the basic principle of positive reinforcement described by B. F. Skinner (1953). Research on mindsets by Carol Dweck (2006) adds that praise for effort and strategy, rather than for being clever, helps students believe that ability grows with work and keeps them going when problems get hard. Appreciation does not mean leaving the student alone. The teacher can stay available and offer a hint if he gets stuck, the kind of support that Lev Vygotsky (1978) placed in the zone of proximal development and that David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) called scaffolding. But the first response to independent effort should be recognition. Example: Kabir, in Class 9, works through a geometry proof without asking for help. His teacher says, \"Good, you tried a new route on your own; show me how you got there,\" and Kabir is keen to attempt the next one. Option d is right because appreciating independent effort encourages autonomy and motivation, which is what teaching aims at. Exam trap: \"be attentive\" (b) sounds caring and safe, but it is passive; in these items the best answer is the one that actively encourages the learner.", "traps": {"a": "Sending the student to his friends undermines the independence he is showing and passes the teacher's responsibility to others; peer help has its place, but not as the response to independent effort.", "b": "Being attentive is good general practice, but it is passive; it neither rewards nor strengthens the student's initiative, so it is weaker than appreciation.", "c": "Rebuking independent problem solving punishes the very behaviour education aims to build and would discourage future initiative; it reflects an authoritarian, not a learner-centred, approach."}}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised, and behaviourist theory minimised, the role of the unconscious. Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams; 1915, \"The Unconscious\") put the unconscious at the centre of personality. He pictured the mind like an iceberg: the conscious mind is the tip, the preconscious holds what can easily be recalled, and the vast unconscious holds instincts, wishes and memories pushed out of awareness by repression. These hidden forces were said to drive dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices, which is why his therapy used free association and dream analysis to uncover them. Behaviourism went to the opposite pole. John Broadus Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour and set aside consciousness and the unconscious alike as unscientific. B. F. Skinner (1953) explained behaviour by a person's history of reinforcement; an \"unconscious\" motive was, for him, at most behaviour whose causes the person cannot describe. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also gave the unconscious little weight, stressing conscious experience and choice, but they sit between the two poles. Example: Meera bites her nails before exams. A Freudian looks for an unconscious oral conflict; a behaviourist looks for the relief from anxiety that reinforces the habit, with no reference to hidden motives. Option b is right because it pairs the school that made the unconscious central with the school that set it aside entirely. Exam trap: humanistic theory in c also played down the unconscious, so c looks right; but behaviourism minimised it most, and the blanks ask for the two extremes.", "traps": {"a": "Trait theory (Gordon Allport, 1937; Raymond Cattell, 1946; Hans Eysenck, 1947) measures stable, largely conscious dispositions and never maximised the unconscious; humanistic theory did not maximise it either.", "c": "Psychoanalytic fits the first blank, and humanistic theory (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) did play down the unconscious, but Rogers still allowed experiences to be denied to awareness; behaviourism rejected the unconscious outright, so it is the better fit.", "d": "Behaviourist fits the second blank, but trait theory did not maximise the unconscious; Gordon Allport even felt that psychoanalysis dug too deep and neglected conscious motives."}}, {"q": "Which is not a clinical subscale of MMPI?", "concept": "The answer is d: schizotypal is not a clinical scale of the MMPI. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) using empirical criterion keying: an item was kept for a scale if it separated a diagnosed patient group from normal people, whatever its content. The result was ten clinical scales, numbered 1 to 0: 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). Scales 5 and 0 measure interests and social style rather than illness, but they are still counted among the ten clinical scales. Raw scores become T scores with a mean of 50 and SD of 10; on the MMPI-2 (1989) a T of 65 or more is elevated, and clinicians read the two highest scales as a code type. So options a, b and c are all genuine clinical scales: Masculinity-femininity is scale 5, Psychopathic deviate scale 4 and Social introversion scale 0. Schizotypal is a DSM personality disorder in Cluster A (odd or eccentric), marked by odd beliefs, eccentric behaviour and social anxiety; it is not one of the ten MMPI clinical scales. Example: Arjun's MMPI-2 profile shows T 72 on scale 4 and T 68 on scale 9; his clinician reads a 4-9 code type, suggesting impulsive acting out, and there is no \"schizotypal\" score to read. Option d is right because schizotypal is a diagnostic category, not an MMPI clinical scale. Exam trap: scale 8 is Schizophrenia, and the shared prefix makes \"schizotypal\" look familiar. A schizotypal scale does exist, but on the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977), not the MMPI.", "traps": {"a": "Masculinity-femininity is genuine clinical scale 5 (Mf), measuring interests typical of one sex or the other; it is not a pathology scale, but it is one of the ten clinical scales, so it cannot be the answer.", "b": "Psychopathic deviate is clinical scale 4 (Pd), built against young people referred for delinquency and conflict with family and authority; it is a true clinical scale.", "c": "Social introversion is clinical scale 0 (Si), measuring shyness and social discomfort; it was the last of the ten to be added but is still a clinical scale."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the main findings on treating childhood anxiety, so \"all of the above\" is correct. The strongest evidence comes from the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS; John Walkup and colleagues, 2008, New England Journal of Medicine). It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) alone, the SSRI sertraline alone, the two combined, or a pill placebo. The CBT was 14 sessions adapted from the Coping Cat programme of Philip Kendall (1990): recognising anxious feelings and thoughts, coping self-talk, problem solving and, above all, graded exposure to feared situations with rewards for brave behaviour. Response meant being rated \"much\" or \"very much\" improved by evaluators who did not know the treatment. The response rates were 80.7% for the combination, 59.7% for CBT alone, 54.9% for sertraline alone and 23.7% for placebo. The combination beat each single treatment, and each single treatment beat placebo, but CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other. So statement a (CBT and sertraline similar) is true, statement b (CBT helps about 60%) matches 59.7%, and statement c (combination best) is true. Example: Riya, 10, refuses to sleep away from her mother and gets stomach aches before school; a clinician following CAMS might start CBT with exposure and add sertraline if her symptoms are severe or do not improve. Option d is right because a, b and c are each supported by CAMS. Exam trap: c is the headline result and tempts you to stop reading, but in an \"all of the above\" item every option must be tested. Key note: the marked copy of this paper has c; c is true but so are a and b, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is true: in CAMS, CBT alone (59.7%) and sertraline alone (54.9%) did not differ significantly; but choosing it alone ignores that b and c are true as well.", "b": "This is true: 59.7% of children responded to CBT alone in CAMS, which is about 60%; but a and c are also correct, so b alone is incomplete.", "c": "This is true and is the headline result (80.7% response to CBT plus sertraline); but because a and b are true too, the complete answer is d."}}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of", "concept": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the notion of a z-score. A z-score tells you how far a raw score lies from the mean of a group, in standard deviation (SD) units: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The deviation IQ applies this idea to intelligence. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, stopped using mental age and instead compared each person with a large, representative sample of people the same age. The raw score is turned into a z-score within the person's age group and then rescaled so that every age group has a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. Because IQs are close to normally distributed, an IQ also gives a percentile. An IQ of 100 (z = 0) is the 50th percentile; 115 (z = +1) is about the 84th; 85 (z = minus 1) is about the 16th; 130 (z = +2) is about the 98th. The Stanford-Binet used an SD of 16 for its deviation IQ until the fifth edition (2003) moved to 15. This solved the problem of the ratio IQ, which divided mental age by chronological age. Mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a ratio IQ makes ordinary adults look impaired. Example: Arjun, aged 35, scores 2 SDs above the mean for adults his age on the WAIS-IV. His z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 15 × 2 = 130. Option c is right because a deviation IQ is simply a z-score rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. Exam trap: \"mental age versus chronological age\" in b is the older ratio IQ of William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), which the deviation IQ replaced.", "traps": {"a": "\"Deviation\" here means statistical distance from the mean, not deviance or delinquency; delinquency plays no part in how the score is calculated.", "b": "Mental age divided by chronological age is the ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912; multiplied by 100 by Lewis Terman, 1916). The deviation IQ was introduced precisely to replace it.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores are parts of David Wechsler's older scale structure; a large verbal-performance gap is a clinical sign, but it is not the basis of the deviation IQ."}}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because", "concept": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson, each life stage matters because each involves a crisis or dilemma. Erik Homburger Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. At each stage the growing person meets new social demands, and these set up a psychosocial crisis, a turning point between two opposite tendencies: trust versus mistrust in infancy, autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority at school age, identity versus role confusion in adolescence, intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation in middle age, and integrity versus despair in old age. \"Crisis\" here does not mean disaster; it means a decisive period of heightened vulnerability and potential. A favourable balance yields a basic strength or virtue: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care and wisdom. An unfavourable balance leaves a weakness that later experience may still repair. The stages follow the epigenetic principle, a built-in order, but society and relationships decide how each crisis turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician, and argues with his parents over each choice. Erikson would see the crisis of identity versus role confusion; working it through, with some freedom to explore, produces fidelity, a stable commitment to values and goals. Option a is right because the crisis, and the way it is resolved, is the core of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: the epigenetic principle sounds like biological programming (c), but Erikson stressed the psychosocial resolution of each crisis, not maturation as such.", "traps": {"b": "New stages of cognitive development belong to Jean Piaget (1952), whose stages track changes in thinking; Erikson's stages track social and emotional challenges.", "c": "Biological programming fits the maturational theory of Arnold Gesell (1925); Erikson's epigenetic principle sets the order of stages, but he stressed social forces in how each turns out, so biology is not why the stages matter.", "d": "Every stage arrives with age whether or not the person is ready; in Erikson's view psychopathology comes from an unfavourable resolution of a crisis, not from a stage failing to appear."}}, {"q": "Match each non parametric test with the data it is suited for. A. Kruskal-Wallis test, B. Mann Whitney, C. Wilcoxon signed ranks, D. Friedman. i) Non-parametric alternative to the one-way ANOVA with repeated measures, ii) Repeated measures test of dependency, iii) One-way ANOVA, unequal sample size, iv) Compare differences between two independent groups", "concept": "The answer is a: Kruskal-Wallis matches iii, Mann-Whitney iv, Wilcoxon signed ranks ii and Friedman i. Non-parametric tests are used when data are ranks (ordinal) or when interval data break the assumptions of parametric tests, such as normality, especially in small samples. Most convert scores to ranks and compare rank totals. To match them, ask two questions: how many groups or conditions are there, and are they independent (different people) or related (the same people measured more than once)? The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) compares two independent groups; it is the non-parametric twin of the independent-samples t-test (iv). The Wilcoxon signed-ranks test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) compares two related measurements, such as before and after scores from the same people; it ranks each pair's difference and keeps the sign, making it the twin of the paired t-test, a repeated-measures test for dependent data (ii). The Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952) compares three or more independent groups, which may be of unequal size; it is the one-way ANOVA by ranks (iii). The Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937) compares three or more related conditions by ranking within each person; it replaces the one-way repeated-measures ANOVA (i). Example: Meera rates the anxiety of patients in three wards of 12, 15 and 9 people; unequal independent groups with ordinal ratings call for Kruskal-Wallis. If she rated the same 12 patients in three successive weeks, she would use Friedman. Option a is right because it is the only code that pairs each test with its design. Exam trap: Wilcoxon has two tests; the signed-rank test is for related pairs, but the Wilcoxon rank-sum test is equivalent to Mann-Whitney for independent groups.", "traps": {"b": "This code gives Kruskal-Wallis the two-group comparison that belongs to Mann-Whitney, and gives Mann-Whitney the repeated-measures ANOVA role that belongs to Friedman.", "c": "This code calls Kruskal-Wallis a repeated-measures test and Friedman a two-independent-group test; both are for three or more groups, Kruskal-Wallis independent and Friedman related.", "d": "This code makes Kruskal-Wallis the repeated-measures ANOVA alternative and Wilcoxon signed ranks a one-way ANOVA; Kruskal-Wallis is for independent groups, and Wilcoxon compares only two related measures."}}, {"q": "What do you consider as the main aim of interdisciplinary research?", "concept": "The answer is a: the main aim of interdisciplinary research is to bring a holistic approach to a research problem. Interdisciplinary research takes the concepts, theories, methods and data of two or more disciplines and integrates them into one shared understanding. The US National Academies (2004) described it as research that integrates information, techniques, perspectives and theories from two or more disciplines to advance understanding or to solve problems beyond the reach of a single discipline. It differs from multidisciplinary work, in which experts from different fields work side by side but each keeps to their own framework and the findings are simply added together, and from transdisciplinary work, which goes further and builds a new framework that cuts across disciplines, often with community partners. The reason for integration is that real problems do not respect subject boundaries. Clinical psychology shows this well: the biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) argued that illness can only be understood by combining biological, psychological and social levels, which no single discipline covers. Example: Kabir studies farmer suicides in Vidarbha. Psychology alone might look at depression and hopelessness; working with economists, agricultural scientists and public health researchers, he can see how debt, crop failure, easy access to pesticides and stigma interact, and design a programme that addresses all of them. Option a is right because the purpose of integrating disciplines is to see the whole problem, not one slice of it. Exam trap: b describes a side effect, not the aim; the weight of any single subject does fall when fields combine, but no one does interdisciplinary research in order to weaken a discipline.", "traps": {"b": "Reducing the emphasis on a single subject is a by-product of combining fields, not the purpose; interdisciplinary work still depends on strong single disciplines feeding into it.", "c": "Oversimplifying is the opposite of the aim: interdisciplinary research exists because complex problems need several levels of explanation, as in George Engel's (1977) biopsychosocial model.", "d": "A new methodological trend may follow, but it is not the aim; the aim is to understand a problem fully, and methods are borrowed or combined to serve that aim."}}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called", "concept": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. In English, /p/ and /b/ are separate phonemes because \"pat\" and \"bat\" are different words; so are /k/ and /g/ in \"cot\" and \"got\". English has about 40 to 44 phonemes, depending on accent. Language is built in layers. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: \"cat\" is one free morpheme, and \"cats\" has two, because the bound morpheme \"-s\" adds the plural. Morphemes form words, and syntax is the set of rules for combining words into phrases and sentences. Semantics deals with meaning, and pragmatics with use in social context. Charles Hockett (1960) called this layering duality of patterning: a small set of meaningless sounds builds a vast number of meaningful units. Infants can at first tell apart the phonemes of all languages, but by about 10 to 12 months they lose sensitivity to contrasts their own language does not use, as Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) showed. Example: Riya, learning Hindi, must hear the difference between the unaspirated \"k\" of \"kal\" (yesterday) and the aspirated \"kh\", said with a puff of air, of \"khaal\" (skin). In Hindi these are separate phonemes; in English the same two sounds never distinguish words. Option c is right because phonemes are the basic sound units of speech. Exam trap: phoneme (sound) and morpheme (meaning) are paired in almost every exam; remember that \"phone\" means sound, as in telephone.", "traps": {"a": "Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning (\"cat\", \"-s\"), built out of phonemes; they sit one level above sounds.", "b": "A syllable is a unit of pronunciation built around a vowel (\"ba-na-na\" has three); it usually contains several phonemes, so it is not the basic sound unit.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences, the grammar studied by Noam Chomsky (1957); it concerns structure, not sound."}}, {"q": "Which of the following types of acceptance is most critical and pervasive to the long-range emotional health of a child?", "concept": "The answer is c: self-acceptance is the most critical and pervasive form of acceptance for a child's long-range emotional health. Self-acceptance means recognising and accepting one's own qualities, strengths and limitations alike, without harsh self-rejection. It is not complacency or believing oneself perfect. It grows out of acceptance by others. Carl Rogers (1959) argued that when parents give unconditional positive regard, the child develops unconditional positive self-regard; when love depends on meeting conditions of worth, the child learns to accept only parts of the self and to deny experiences that do not fit, which leads to anxiety and maladjustment. Elizabeth Hurlock (1974), whose texts Indian courses follow, treated self-acceptance as essential for good personal and social adjustment: a child who accepts himself relates more easily to others, copes better with failure and is less defensive. Carol Ryff (1989) later listed self-acceptance as one of six dimensions of psychological well-being in adults. Self-acceptance is \"pervasive\" because it goes with the child into every setting. Peer, sibling and later vocational acceptance each apply to one domain and change with the group, but the way the child regards herself colours how she reads all of them. Example: Riya is not chosen for the school dance team. If she accepts herself, she is disappointed but tries something else; if she rejects herself, the same event confirms \"I am useless\" and spreads to her studies and friendships. Option c is right because self-acceptance is the internal, lasting base on which the other kinds of acceptance rest. Exam trap: peer acceptance is studied heavily in childhood research and looks like the answer, but it is tied to particular groups and changes; the words \"most critical and pervasive\" point to the self.", "traps": {"a": "Peer acceptance matters greatly in middle childhood and adolescence (sociometric status), but it is tied to particular groups and can change; it does not reach every area of life as self-acceptance does.", "b": "Sibling acceptance shapes rivalry and family relationships, but it is limited to the home and is one influence on, not the core of, a child's emotional health.", "d": "Vocational acceptance concerns being accepted in a job or career, an adult issue in the career theory of Donald Super (1953); it is not yet relevant to a child's emotional health."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II and choose the correct answer from the code given below. List-I: A. Historical method, B. Survey method, C. Philosophical method, D. Experimental method. List-II: 1. Past events, 2. Vision, 3. Present events, 4. Exceptional cases, 5. Future action", "concept": "The answer is a: historical method goes with past events, survey method with present events, philosophical method with vision, and experimental method with future action. This matching follows John W. Best (1959), Research in Education, later written with James V. Kahn, which sorts research by its relation to time. Historical research describes what was. It studies past events through records, documents and remains, testing sources for genuineness (external criticism) and accuracy (internal criticism), to understand how the present came about. Descriptive research, of which the survey is the classic form, describes what is: current conditions, practices, attitudes and opinions, gathered by questionnaire, interview or observation. Experimental research describes what will be when certain variables are carefully controlled or manipulated. Because it tests cause and effect, it lets us predict and plan future action. Philosophical research, the reflective analysis of aims, values and concepts, gives education its vision, the picture of where it should be going. The fourth item in List-II, exceptional cases, is the distractor; intensive study of unusual individuals belongs to the case study, which is not in List-I. Example: Meera wants to improve counselling services in Kolkata colleges. Archives on how the counselling cells began are historical research; a questionnaire on how many students use them now is a survey; a controlled trial of a peer-support programme tells her what will happen if she introduces it; and reflection on what a caring college should be gives her the vision. Option a is right because it is the only code that pairs each method with its time frame and leaves exceptional cases unused. Exam trap: the survey is present-oriented, not visionary; codes b and d put the survey with vision, and code c pairs it with exceptional cases.", "traps": {"b": "This code gives the survey method \"vision\" and philosophy \"exceptional cases\"; surveys describe present conditions, and exceptional cases belong to the case study, not philosophy.", "c": "This code gives the survey \"exceptional cases\"; a survey samples many ordinary respondents to describe what is now, whereas exceptional cases are the material of the case study.", "d": "This code makes philosophy describe present events and experiments study exceptional cases; experiments control variables to predict future outcomes, and philosophy supplies vision."}}, {"q": "REM sleep behavior is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterized as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "traps": {"b": "Rapid eye movements continue in RBD; they are how the stage is recognised on the sleep study, so they are not the missing feature.", "c": "The desynchronised, high-frequency, low-amplitude EEG of REM is still present in RBD; the brain is in genuine REM, and only the muscle paralysis fails.", "d": "Penile erections are a normal feature of REM sleep in males and are not what is lost in RBD; their presence during sleep is used instead in nocturnal penile tumescence testing for erectile dysfunction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following neurological disorders is associated with a deficiency of acetylcholine?", "concept": "The answer is a: Alzheimer's disease is the neurological disorder associated with a deficiency of acetylcholine (ACh). Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer (1906) in his patient Auguste Deter, is the commonest cause of dementia. Its brain pathology includes beta-amyloid plaques outside neurons, neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein inside them, and neuron loss that begins in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus. Among the earliest and worst-hit cells are the cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain, especially the nucleus basalis of Meynert, which send ACh to the cortex and hippocampus. Peter Davies and A. J. F. Maloney (1976) found sharply reduced levels of choline acetyltransferase, the enzyme that makes ACh, in Alzheimer brains. Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982) then proposed the cholinergic hypothesis: much of the memory loss of ageing and Alzheimer's disease reflects this cholinergic failure. Supporting it, anticholinergic drugs such as scopolamine impair memory in healthy people, while drugs that raise ACh (cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil) give modest relief. ACh is central to attention, learning and memory, which fits the early symptoms. Example: Arjun's grandmother, 78, forgets conversations from the same morning and repeats her questions; her neurologist diagnoses Alzheimer's disease and starts donepezil to make the most of the ACh that remains. Option a is right because cholinergic loss is the classic neurotransmitter deficit of Alzheimer's disease. Exam trap: Parkinson's disease (b) is the classic dopamine deficiency; candidates who remember only that both are neurodegenerative diseases swap the two transmitters.", "traps": {"b": "Parkinson's disease, described by James Parkinson (1817), is a loss of dopamine from the substantia nigra; cholinergic activity is relatively high there, which is why anticholinergic drugs can ease the tremor.", "c": "Wernicke's aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), is fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension after damage, usually a stroke, to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus; it is a lesion syndrome, not a transmitter deficiency.", "d": "Autism spectrum disorder, first described by Leo Kanner (1943), is a neurodevelopmental condition; its best-known transmitter finding is raised blood serotonin in some children, not an acetylcholine deficiency."}}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine (ACh). The logic comes from the cholinergic hypothesis (Raymond Bartus and colleagues, 1982). Alzheimer's disease destroys cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain (the nucleus basalis of Meynert), leaving the cortex and hippocampus short of ACh, which supports attention and memory. The dying neurons cannot be replaced, so the drugs try to make the remaining ACh last longer. Normally, once ACh is released into the synapse, the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks it down within milliseconds. Cholinesterase inhibitors block this enzyme, so ACh stays in the synapse longer and stimulates its receptors more. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. The first, tacrine (1993), was withdrawn because it damaged the liver. These drugs give modest, temporary gains in memory and daily function; they do not stop the disease. The fourth standard drug, memantine, works differently: it blocks NMDA glutamate receptors to protect neurons from excess glutamate and is used in moderate to severe stages. Newer antibody drugs such as lecanemab (approved 2023) clear amyloid plaques rather than changing any transmitter. So three of the four long-standing medicines target ACh. Example: Meera's grandfather starts donepezil; over the next months he is a little more alert and follows conversations better, although his memory problems remain. Option d is right because the main class of Alzheimer medicines, the cholinesterase inhibitors, works by increasing acetylcholine. Exam trap: memantine acts on glutamate, which is not offered here; and do not let dopamine, the Parkinson's transmitter, tempt you just because both are neurodegenerative diseases.", "traps": {"a": "Dopamine is the transmitter replaced in Parkinson's disease with L-dopa (George Cotzias, 1967); dopamine drugs do not treat the memory loss of Alzheimer's disease.", "b": "GABA is the main inhibitory transmitter; drugs that boost it, such as benzodiazepines, treat anxiety and can worsen confusion in people with dementia.", "c": "Noradrenaline is a target of some antidepressants and ADHD drugs; noradrenergic cells of the locus coeruleus are also lost in Alzheimer's, but no standard Alzheimer drug works by raising it."}}, {"q": "As one moves from external stimulus to motor response, which of the following constitutes an accurate sequential pattern?", "concept": "The answer is a: the sequence from external stimulus to motor response runs afferent nerve, interneuron, efferent nerve. Neurons fall into three functional classes. Afferent (sensory) neurons carry signals from receptors toward the central nervous system; the word comes from Latin afferre, to carry toward. Interneurons (association or connector neurons), by far the commonest type, lie wholly inside the brain and spinal cord and link incoming to outgoing signals. Efferent (motor) neurons carry commands away from the CNS to effectors, the muscles and glands; efferre means to carry out. A memory aid: afferent arrives, efferent exits. Together these form the reflex arc, analysed in detail by Charles Scott Sherrington (1906) in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System. In the spinal cord, the Bell-Magendie law (Charles Bell, 1811; Francois Magendie, 1822) holds that sensory fibres enter through the dorsal root and motor fibres leave through the ventral root. Example: Meera touches a hot tawa. Heat receptors in her fingertip fire; afferent neurons carry the signal through the dorsal root into the spinal grey matter; an interneuron passes it on; efferent neurons leave through the ventral root and contract her arm muscles. She pulls away before she even feels the pain, because the arc closes in the spinal cord. This withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic; the knee-jerk is monosynaptic and skips the interneuron. Option a is right because the signal must first arrive (afferent), be relayed (interneuron) and then exit (efferent). Exam trap: options b and d reverse afferent and efferent, and \"neural modulator\" in c and d sounds technical but is a chemical messenger, not a link in the chain.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the order: efferent (motor) neurons carry commands out to the muscles, so they come at the end of the chain, not the beginning.", "c": "Afferent does come first, but the arc does not jump straight to the efferent neuron and end in a \"neural modulator\"; neuromodulators are chemicals that adjust synaptic transmission, not a stage of the arc.", "d": "This starts with the outgoing efferent neuron and ends with a neuromodulator, so both the order and the final element are wrong; the response ends at an effector muscle or gland."}}, {"q": "______ is a subprocess of ______", "concept": "The answer is a: psychological testing is a subprocess of observation. Observation is the broadest way of gathering information about behaviour: watching, recording and describing what a person does, in natural or arranged settings. A psychological test is a special, tightly controlled form of observation. Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour: everyone meets the same items, instructions, time limits and scoring rules, so the behaviour observed can be compared across people and against norms. Lee J. Cronbach, in later editions of Essentials of Psychological Testing (first published 1949), described a test as a systematic procedure for observing behaviour and describing it with a numerical scale or a category system. On this view, testing is observation made standard, systematic and quantified, and a test score is a record of behaviour observed under fixed conditions. Psychological assessment is wider still: it answers a referral question by combining tests, interviews, observation, case history and records into a reasoned conclusion. Example: Kabir, aged 8, is referred for attention problems. His psychologist watches him in class (naturalistic observation) and then gives a timed cancellation test in the clinic. The test is still observation, but now every child meets the same task under the same rules, so Kabir's speed and errors can be compared with norms. Option a is right because a test is a controlled, standardised sample of observed behaviour. Exam trap: assessment is the widest of these terms and testing the narrowest; any option that makes assessment part of something smaller, as d does, has the hierarchy upside down. Key note: in modern texts (Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik) testing and interviewing are both tools inside psychological assessment, so c has support; the key follows Lee Cronbach's definition of a test as a systematic procedure for observing behaviour, which makes testing a controlled form of observation. The key stands.", "traps": {"b": "Testing and interviewing are parallel tools; a test is not a part of an interview, and an interview is not a standardised measure of a behaviour sample in Anne Anastasi's (1954) sense.", "c": "Modern texts such as that of Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik do treat interviewing as one tool within assessment, so this is defensible; but the item follows Lee Cronbach's view of a test as systematic observation, which makes a the better fit.", "d": "This turns the hierarchy upside down: psychological assessment is the widest process and includes observation, so it cannot be a subprocess of observation."}}, {"q": "The quality of teaching is reflected in", "concept": "The answer is c: the quality of teaching is reflected in the quality of the questions students ask. A student can ask a good question only after thinking about the material: noticing a gap, linking it to what she already knows, or testing an idea against a new case. So the questions a class asks are a window into how deeply it has understood. In the taxonomy of Benjamin Bloom (1956), recall questions (\"What is the definition?\") sit at the lowest level, knowledge, while questions that apply, analyse or evaluate (\"Would this still hold if the sample were small?\") show higher-order thinking. A teacher who explains clearly, invites curiosity and makes it safe to ask produces students who ask questions of the second kind. Example: after a lesson on the reflex arc, one class asks only \"Will this come in the exam?\", while Meera's class asks why the knee-jerk reflex skips the interneuron and what would happen if a dorsal root were cut. The second set of questions shows that the teaching produced understanding, not just note-taking. Option c is right because thoughtful student questions are direct evidence of engaged, deep learning, which is what good teaching aims at. Exam trap: pass percentage (b) looks like an objective measure, but results can be driven by rote memorisation, coaching and lenient marking, so they reflect many things besides teaching quality.", "traps": {"a": "Attendance can be compulsory or driven by rules and marks, so a full classroom shows presence, not learning or teaching quality.", "b": "Pass percentages depend on rote learning, coaching, the difficulty of the paper and marking standards, so they are an indirect and easily distorted index of teaching.", "d": "A silent class may be passive, bored or afraid to speak; good teaching usually produces active discussion and questions, not long silences."}}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to", "concept": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "traps": {"a": "The amount of transmitter released depends on calcium entry and firing rate at the axon terminal, not on myelin; myelin covers the axon, not the synapse.", "b": "The synaptic cleft is a fixed gap of about 20 to 40 nanometres between neurons, and myelin neither reaches nor changes it.", "d": "Action potentials are all-or-none: their size is fixed for a given neuron, so myelin speeds conduction without making the spike any larger."}}, {"q": "Grief refers to", "concept": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often confused. Bereavement is the objective fact of having lost someone close, and in its wider sense the whole period of adjusting to that loss. Grief is the emotional response itself: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and sometimes relief, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep, loss of appetite and tightness in the chest. Mourning is the outward, social expression of grief, the rituals and customs a culture prescribes, such as the rites a Hindu family performs in the days after a death. Grief is usually most intense in the early weeks and months, then eases while the bond with the dead person is kept in memory. John Bowlby (1980) described four phases of mourning: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) replaced fixed stages with a dual process model, in which the bereaved person swings between loss-oriented coping (crying, remembering) and restoration-oriented coping (new roles, practical tasks). Most grief is normal; when intense yearning still disables an adult 12 months after the death, DSM-5-TR (2022) allows a diagnosis of prolonged grief disorder. Example: when Riya's grandmother dies, Riya feels waves of sadness and longing, cannot concentrate and cries at the smell of her grandmother's cooking. That raw emotional state is her grief; the funeral and the rites her family observes are mourning; the whole months-long adjustment is bereavement. Option b is right because it defines grief as an emotional state, intense and triggered by a death. Exam trap: option a sounds scholarly but describes a period of adjustment, which is bereavement or mourning, and option c is only the first, numb phase of mourning, not grief as a whole.", "traps": {"a": "This describes bereavement in its wider sense, or mourning: the months-long process of adjusting to a loss. Grief is the emotional state within that process, not the period itself.", "c": "This is the numbness phase, the first of John Bowlby's (1980) four phases of mourning; a short dazed stage is one part of the grieving process, not the definition of grief.", "d": "Demoralisation is a general state of hopelessness and discouragement, which Jerome Frank (1961) saw as what brings people to therapy; it can follow any setback, not specifically a death."}}, {"q": "Mutism, self-destructive behavior, and echolalia are symptoms of", "concept": "The answer is a: mutism, self-destructive behaviour and echolalia are symptoms of childhood autism. Autism was first described by Leo Kanner (1943) in eleven children who showed extreme aloneness from early life and an anxious insistence on sameness; Hans Asperger (1944) described verbally able boys with similar social difficulties. DSM-5 (2013) now uses one label, autism spectrum disorder, defined by two groups of signs present from early childhood: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction (poor eye contact, little shared enjoyment, difficulty with relationships), and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities (hand flapping, lining up toys, rigid routines, unusual sensory reactions). Language problems are central in classic autism. Some children never develop useful speech and stay mute or minimally verbal; estimates put this at roughly a quarter to a third. Others speak but show echolalia, parroting words they have just heard (immediate) or repeating phrases heard earlier, such as television jingles (delayed), and pronoun reversal, saying 'you' for 'I'. Self-injurious behaviour, such as head banging, hand biting or face slapping, is common, especially when intellectual disability is also present, and is often treated with applied behaviour analysis, which asks what the behaviour gets the child or lets him escape. Example: four-year-old Arjun does not answer to his name, repeats 'Do you want a biscuit?' instead of saying yes, and bangs his head when his routine changes. Option a is right because only autism combines absent or odd speech with repetitive and self-injurious behaviour from early childhood. Exam trap: mutism and echolalia also appear in catatonia, and selective mutism is an anxiety disorder in which the child talks normally at home; but among these four options, autism is the developmental disorder that fits all three signs.", "traps": {"b": "Anorexia nervosa, named by William Gull (1873), is an eating disorder of self-starvation with fear of weight gain and a distorted body image; speech and imitation are normal.", "c": "ADHD, first described by George Still (1902), is inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity; these children often talk too much rather than too little, and they do not parrot speech.", "d": "Dyslexia, described as 'congenital word blindness' by W. Pringle Morgan (1896), is a specific learning disorder in reading with normal speech and social behaviour."}}, {"q": "Unconditional positive regard is part of what therapy?", "concept": "The answer is d: unconditional positive regard is part of client-centred therapy. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, argued that people have an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, which is blocked when love comes with conditions of worth ('I am only good if I top the class'). The person then distorts or denies experiences that clash with the self-concept, a state Rogers called incongruence. Therapy repairs this through a relationship. In 1957 Rogers set out the necessary and sufficient conditions for change; the three core conditions on the therapist's side are congruence (being genuine), accurate empathic understanding, and unconditional positive regard (UPR). UPR means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judging and without conditions. It does not mean approving of every act; the therapist values the person while still having views about behaviour. Rogers credited the phrase to his student Stanley Standal (1954). Feeling accepted without conditions, the client can drop defences, admit feared feelings and move towards congruence. The therapist is non-directive: no interpretation, advice or diagnosis, mainly reflection of feeling. Example: Meera tells her counsellor that she secretly resents her ailing mother and feels like a monster. The counsellor neither scolds nor rushes to reassure, but conveys, 'You are carrying something heavy, and it makes sense to me,' so Meera can explore the feeling safely. Option d is right because UPR is one of Rogers's core conditions and the heart of client-centred (person-centred) therapy. Exam trap: humanistic and existential therapies are often lumped together, so logotherapy and Gestalt therapy tempt; both value the person and the present, but UPR as a named condition of change belongs to Rogers alone.", "traps": {"a": "Logotherapy, Viktor Frankl's (1946) existential therapy, helps clients find meaning through techniques such as paradoxical intention and dereflection; warm acceptance is not its defining method.", "b": "Psychoanalysis, Sigmund Freud's (1896) method, works through free association and the interpretation of transference and resistance by a neutral analyst, not through unconditional warmth.", "c": "Gestalt therapy, from Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman (1951), is active and often confrontational, using here-and-now awareness and the empty chair; it does not rest on UPR."}}, {"q": "In operant conditioning, the reinforcer occurs ______ the response, and in classical conditioning, it occurs ______.", "concept": "The answer is a: in operant conditioning the reinforcer occurs after the response, and in classical conditioning it occurs before. In classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus such as a bell is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS) such as food, which automatically elicits an unconditioned response, salivation. Pavlov called the food the reinforcement, because it strengthens the bell and food link. The sequence runs bell, food, salivation: the UCS (the reinforcer) is presented first and draws the response out, so it comes before the response. The learner is passive, and the behaviour is elicited. In operant conditioning, set out by Edward Thorndike (1898) with his puzzle-box cats and by B. F. Skinner (1938), the organism first emits a behaviour, and a consequence then follows it. A rat presses a lever, and then a food pellet drops. The reinforcer is contingent on the response, so it must come after it; a reward given before the act could not tell the animal which behaviour paid off. Skinner called the two kinds of behaviour respondent (elicited) and operant (emitted). Example: each time the pressure cooker whistles, lunch is served, and the food in Kabir's mouth makes him salivate; the food, the reinforcer, comes first and pulls out the response, until the whistle alone makes his mouth water. In an operant case, Kabir gets praise only after he finishes his homework, so finishing homework becomes more frequent. Option a is right because the reinforcer follows the emitted response in operant learning but precedes, and elicits, the response in classical learning. Exam trap: in classical conditioning the UCS comes after the conditioned stimulus, so students write 'after' twice (option b); the stem asks about the timing relative to the response, not the stimulus.", "traps": {"b": "'After; after' comes from mixing up stimulus order: the UCS does follow the conditioned stimulus in classical conditioning, but it precedes and elicits the response.", "c": "'Before; before' wrongly puts the operant reinforcer ahead of the response; in B. F. Skinner's (1938) operant learning a consequence can only strengthen a behaviour already emitted.", "d": "'Before; after' reverses both halves of the rule, putting the operant reinforcer first and the classical one last."}}, {"q": "Othello Syndrome is a syndrome characterized by", "concept": "The answer is b: Othello syndrome is pathological (morbid) jealousy. It is a delusion, or sometimes an overvalued idea, that one's sexual partner is being unfaithful, held without adequate evidence and not corrected by reassurance. John Todd and Kenneth Dewhurst (1955) named it after Shakespeare's Othello, who kills his innocent wife Desdemona in a fit of jealousy fed by Iago's lies. The person checks phones and clothes for 'proof', questions the partner for hours, follows them, and reads tiny events (a late bus, a new hairstyle) as signs of betrayal. It is more common in men and is classically linked to alcohol dependence, where sexual dysfunction and marital conflict may feed it; it also occurs in delusional disorder (jealous type), schizophrenia, dementia and stimulant misuse. It matters clinically because it carries a real risk of violence, even homicide, against the partner or the imagined rival, so assessment includes asking about threats and sometimes advising separation. Example: Rohan, a heavy drinker, insists his wife Kavya is meeting a colleague; he smells her saree for perfume and demands to see her phone every evening, and no denial convinces him. Option b is right because the eponym refers to the jealousy theme alone. Exam trap: because Othello syndrome is so often seen in people who drink heavily, examiners offer 'pathological drinking' (option a); drinking is a common setting, not the syndrome. Also keep the named delusional syndromes apart: Capgras is the impostor delusion, Fregoli the belief that strangers are a familiar person in disguise, and de Clerambault the erotomanic belief that someone of higher status is secretly in love with one.", "traps": {"a": "Pathological drinking is alcohol dependence; it is the commonest setting in which Othello syndrome appears, not the syndrome itself.", "c": "Over-concern with a defect in bodily appearance is dysmorphophobia, named by Enrico Morselli (1891) and now called body dysmorphic disorder.", "d": "Believing a family member has been replaced by an impostor is Capgras syndrome, described by Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923)."}}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 16 per cent of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of 1. A z score tells you how many standard deviations a score lies from the mean: z = (X minus the mean) divided by the SD. The normal curve, derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) and later tied to measurement error by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809), is symmetrical, with mean, median and mode at the centre, and its areas are fixed. Working step by step: 1. Half of all scores lie above the mean (z = 0), so the area above z = 0 is 50 per cent. 2. The area between the mean and z = +1 is 34.13 per cent (the familiar 68 per cent within one SD either side is 34.13 on each side). 3. So the area above z = +1 is 50 minus 34.13 = 15.87 per cent, which rounds to 16 per cent. 4. Put another way, z = +1 sits at about the 84th percentile, and 100 minus 84 = 16. Example: IQ tests have a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. Ananya scores 115, so z = (115 minus 100) divided by 15 = +1. About 84 per cent of people score below her and about 16 per cent above. By symmetry, another 16 per cent score below 85 (z = minus 1). Option b is right because 50 minus 34.13 leaves 15.87, roughly 16 per cent, in the upper tail beyond one SD. Exam trap: option a (50 per cent) is the area above the mean, not above z = 1; students who forget to subtract the middle band choose it. Also, the tail beyond two SDs is only about 2.3 per cent, so split the 68, 95 and 99.7 rule into halves before reading off a tail.", "traps": {"a": "50 per cent lies above the mean (z = 0), not above z = +1; this ignores the 34.13 per cent between the mean and one SD.", "c": "25 per cent lies above the upper quartile, which falls at a z of about +0.67, not +1.", "d": "10 per cent lies above a z of about +1.28 (the 90th percentile), further out than one SD."}}, {"q": "The so-called critical period in prenatal development is the", "concept": "The answer is b: the embryonic stage is the critical period of prenatal development. Prenatal life, about 38 weeks from conception, has three periods. The germinal period (also called the period of the zygote) covers about the first two weeks: the fertilised egg divides into a morula and then a hollow blastocyst, which implants in the uterine wall. The embryonic period runs from about week 3 to week 8. It opens with gastrulation, when the inner cell mass forms three germ layers (ectoderm for skin and nervous system, mesoderm for muscle, bone and heart, endoderm for gut and lungs), and by about week 4 the neural tube, the future brain and spinal cord, has formed. Almost every major organ takes shape in these six weeks (organogenesis). The fetal period, week 9 to birth, is mostly growth and refinement. A critical period is a window when a structure is forming and is most open to harm. Because the organs form in the embryonic period, teratogens (harmful agents such as alcohol, rubella or certain drugs) do the most structural damage then. The classic evidence is thalidomide, a sedative given to pregnant women in the late 1950s: babies exposed during the embryonic weeks were born with missing or shortened limbs, while later exposure caused far less harm. Example: Priya learns at 6 weeks that she is pregnant after taking an anti-epileptic drug; her doctor is concerned because the embryo's heart, limbs and other organs are still forming. Option b is right because organogenesis, and so maximum vulnerability, falls in weeks 3 to 8. Exam trap: the fetal period is longer and the fetus bigger, so students think it is more vulnerable; and the germinal period tends to follow an all-or-none rule, in which harm usually ends the pregnancy or leaves no defect.", "traps": {"a": "The germinal stage (about the first two weeks: zygote, blastocyst, implantation) comes before organs form; harm then follows an all-or-none pattern, so it is not called the critical period.", "c": "'Zygotic stage' is only another name for the germinal period, the period of the zygote, so it fails for the same reason as option a.", "d": "'Mucotic stage' is not a stage of prenatal development at all; it is an invented distractor."}}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of cortex is responsible for our sense of", "concept": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "traps": {"a": "Taste is processed in the gustatory cortex of the insula and frontal operculum, reached through cranial nerves VII, IX and X and the thalamus, not in the postcentral gyrus.", "b": "Sight is processed in the primary visual (striate) cortex around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe.", "c": "Hearing is processed in the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus of the temporal lobe."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important characteristic of research procedure?", "concept": "The answer is c: replicability is the most important characteristic of a research procedure. Science differs from opinion and authority because its findings are public and can be checked. A procedure is replicable when it is described so fully (sample, materials, measures, steps and analysis) that another researcher can repeat it and see whether the same result appears. Replication is how science corrects itself: a chance finding, an artefact or a fraud will not keep turning up when others repeat the work, while a real effect will. That is why journals demand a detailed method section, written so that any trained reader could redo the study. The importance of replication became painfully clear in the replication crisis. The Open Science Collaboration (2015) repeated 100 studies published in leading psychology journals: about 97 per cent of the originals had significant results, but only about 36 per cent of the repetitions did. Example: Riya reports that a five-minute breathing exercise lowers test anxiety in Delhi students. Unless she states exactly how she sampled, which anxiety scale she used and how long the exercise lasted, Arjun in Chennai cannot repeat her study, and her claim can be neither confirmed nor refuted. Option c is right because a procedure that cannot be repeated cannot be verified, so replicability is what makes the other qualities testable. Exam trap: objectivity (option d) is also a hallmark of science and tempts many; but objectivity means freedom from personal bias in observation, and it is itself shown by replication, when different researchers repeating the procedure get the same result. The stem asks about the procedure, and the defining property of a procedure is that it can be repeated.", "traps": {"a": "Communicability means the findings can be clearly reported to others; it serves replication, but it is a feature of the report rather than of the procedure.", "b": "Generalisability is external validity, how far results extend to other people and settings; it concerns the findings, not the procedure itself.", "d": "Objectivity, freedom from the researcher's personal bias, is a key feature of science, but it is demonstrated through replication, which makes replicability the more basic property of a procedure."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important dimension in the reference to the qualitative research?", "concept": "The answer is d: contextual relevance is the most important dimension in qualitative research. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words, images and observed action, in natural settings, analysed by interpretation rather than statistics. Its central claim is that behaviour makes sense only in its context: the culture, relationships, history and situation in which it occurs. The anthropologist Clifford Geertz (1973) called the goal thick description, recording behaviour with the context that gives it meaning, which alone tells a wink from a twitch of the eyelid. So a qualitative study is judged by how well its account fits and illuminates the setting it studied, and how far it captures the participants' own (emic) meanings. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) replaced the quantitative criteria of validity, reliability and objectivity with trustworthiness: credibility, transferability, dependability and confirmability. Transferability makes no claim of statistical generalisation; it relies on rich contextual description so that a reader can judge whether the findings apply to another setting. Example: Meera interviews women who care for relatives with schizophrenia in rural Bengal. Their talk of 'fate' and 'family duty' is meaningful only against local beliefs about karma and joint-family obligations; strip that context away and the findings become empty. Option d is right because qualitative findings take their value from fidelity to context, not from numbers or controlled conditions. Exam trap: objectivity and technical precision are virtues of quantitative, positivist research, so 'clarity on the basis of objectivity' sounds scientific but belongs to the other paradigm; qualitative researchers instead practise reflexivity, openly accounting for their own influence on the data.", "traps": {"a": "Newness and originality are expected of any research problem, quantitative or qualitative; they are not what is distinctive about qualitative inquiry.", "b": "Clarity grounded in objectivity is a positivist, quantitative criterion; qualitative research accepts the researcher as the instrument and uses reflexivity instead.", "c": "Technical practicability (whether time, money and tools allow the study) is a general criterion for choosing any research problem, not the defining dimension of qualitative research."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is NOT a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder (BPD). Adolph Stern (1938) used 'borderline' for patients thought to sit on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation. DSM-5 (2013) defines BPD as a pervasive pattern of instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood; five of nine criteria are needed. They include frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships that swing between idealisation and devaluation, identity disturbance, self-damaging impulsivity, recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm, affective instability, chronic emptiness, intense anger, and brief stress-related paranoia or dissociation. So options b, c and d are true cardinal features: b is relationship instability, c is identity disturbance, and d is affective instability, with mood shifting within hours. Option a is the odd one out. Odd thinking, odd speech, magical beliefs and eccentric dress define schizotypal personality disorder, introduced in DSM-III (1980) and placed in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, with paranoid and schizoid. BPD sits in Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD around this core of emotional dysregulation. Example: Tanya idealises a new friend in week one, accuses her of betrayal in week three, cuts her wrist after a fight and says she does not know who she really is; that is BPD. Kabir, who dresses strangely, believes he can read minds and speaks vaguely, fits schizotypal instead. Option a is right because eccentricity is the Cluster A hallmark, not a BPD feature. Exam trap: BPD can show brief paranoid ideas under stress, so 'odd thinking' tempts; persistent eccentricity, though, is schizotypal.", "traps": {"b": "True of BPD: unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation is a DSM-5 criterion, so it cannot be the answer.", "c": "True of BPD: identity disturbance, a markedly and persistently unstable self-image, is a DSM-5 criterion.", "d": "True of BPD: affective instability, marked reactivity of mood with episodes usually lasting hours, is a DSM-5 criterion."}}, {"q": "Match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer from the code given below. List-I (Interviews): A. Structured interviews, B. Unstructured interviews, C. Focused interviews, D. Clinical interviews. List-II (Meaning): 1. Greater flexibility approach, 2. Attention on the questions to be answered, 3. Individual life experience, 4. Pre-determined question, 5. Non-directive", "concept": "The answer is a: A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3. This matching follows C. R. Kothari (2004), Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, a standard Indian research methods text. Interviews differ in how much is fixed in advance. A structured interview uses a set of predetermined questions with fixed wording and order, often read from a schedule; answers are easy to compare and score, but the method is rigid (A-4). An unstructured interview fixes only the aim and lets the interviewer change the order, wording and follow-up of questions, so its mark is greater flexibility, gaining depth at the cost of comparability (B-1). The focused interview, described by Robert Merton and Patricia Kendall (1946), focuses attention on a given experience of the respondent, such as watching a film or living through a disaster, and on its effects; the interviewer decides the manner and sequence of questions while keeping attention on the issues to be answered (C-2). The clinical interview is concerned with broad underlying feelings or motives, or with the course of the individual's life experience, as when a psychologist takes a developmental history (D-3). Kothari also lists the non-directive interview, in which the interviewer simply encourages the respondent to talk with a bare minimum of direct questioning, a style drawn from the counselling of Carl Rogers (1942); it is the extra item 5 that matches nothing. Example: Sneha studies a cyclone's impact on fishermen. She asks all 200 the same 20 questions (structured), chats freely with village elders (unstructured), asks survivors specifically about the night of the storm and how it changed them (focused), and traces one man's whole life story (clinical). Option a is right because it pairs each interview type with its defining feature. Exam trap: 'non-directive' sounds like unstructured, but here it is the spare distractor; flexibility is what defines the unstructured interview.", "traps": {"b": "This gives structured interviews the focused interview's feature and unstructured interviews predetermined questions, reversing the basic fixed versus flexible contrast.", "c": "This pairs structured interviews with non-directive, the opposite of a fixed schedule, and gives focused interviews predetermined questions; item 5 matches none of the four types.", "d": "This gives structured interviews flexibility and clinical interviews predetermined questions, again reversing the fixed versus flexible contrast, and uses the non-directive distractor for focused interviews."}}, {"q": "What hormone, secreted by the pineal gland, increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes feelings of drowsiness?", "concept": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "traps": {"a": "Testosterone is an androgen made mainly by the testes (with small amounts from the adrenal glands and ovaries); it governs male sexual development and libido, not sleep onset.", "c": "Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex under the HPA axis, peaks soon after waking (the cortisol awakening response) and promotes alertness and coping with stress.", "d": "Epinephrine (adrenaline), from the adrenal medulla, drives the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915), raising heart rate and arousal, the opposite of drowsiness."}}, {"q": "Out of 7 consonants and 4 vowels, how many words of 3 consonants and 2 vowels can be formed?", "concept": "The answer is c: 25200 words can be formed. The task has two parts: first choose which letters to use, then arrange them. Choosing is a combination problem, written nCr = n! / (r! (n minus r)!), because the order of picking does not matter. Arranging is a permutation problem, because in a word the order of letters does matter. Step 1: choose 3 consonants from 7: 7C3 = (7 x 6 x 5) / (3 x 2 x 1) = 210 / 6 = 35. Step 2: choose 2 vowels from 4: 4C2 = (4 x 3) / (2 x 1) = 6. Step 3: number of different sets of 5 letters = 35 x 6 = 210 (multiply, because each consonant set can go with each vowel set). Step 4: each set of 5 different letters can be arranged in 5! = 5 x 4 x 3 x 2 x 1 = 120 orders. Step 5: total words = 210 x 120 = 25200. Here a 'word' means any arrangement of the letters, whether or not it means anything, and no letter is repeated. Option c is right because it multiplies the number of letter selections (210) by the number of arrangements of each selection (120). Exam trap: option a (210) catches students who stop after choosing the letters and forget that words must also be arranged; option b (1050) comes from multiplying by 5 instead of by 5 factorial.", "traps": {"a": "210 is only the number of ways to select 3 consonants and 2 vowels (35 x 6); it leaves out the 120 ways of arranging each set of five letters.", "b": "1050 is 210 x 5, which multiplies by 5 instead of by 5! = 120 arrangements.", "d": "21400 has no valid working behind it; it is a near-miss figure, since 210 x 120 gives 25200."}}, {"q": "During which stage of sleep are EEG recordings most similar to those during alert, awake periods, giving this stage the informal name \"paradoxical sleep\"?", "concept": "The answer is d: REM sleep is the stage whose EEG most resembles alert waking, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) noticed bursts of rapid eye movements in sleepers, and William Dement (1957), working with Kleitman, linked these periods to vivid dreaming. Michel Jouvet (1959), working with cats, coined the term paradoxical sleep, because the brain looks awake while the body is deeply asleep. Sleep is scored from the EEG, eye movements and muscle tone, following Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968). Stage 1 is light, drowsy sleep with low-voltage theta waves. Stage 2 adds sleep spindles and K-complexes. Stages 3 and 4 (now merged as N3) show large, slow delta waves: deep, slow-wave sleep. In REM sleep the EEG becomes fast, low-voltage and desynchronised, with sawtooth waves, much like that of an awake, alert person; yet the sleeper is hard to wake, the core muscles are almost paralysed (REM atonia), the eyes dart, and breathing and heart rate become irregular. About 80 per cent of awakenings from REM yield a dream report. REM makes up about 20 to 25 per cent of adult sleep, and REM periods lengthen in the later cycles of the night, each cycle lasting about 90 minutes. Example: at 5 a.m. Neha watches her sleeping brother Rahul: his eyes flick under his lids and his body is limp, yet an EEG would show his brain as busy as when he solves maths; woken now, he will probably report a dream. Option d is right because only REM combines a waking-like EEG with deep behavioural sleep. Exam trap: stage 1 also has a low-voltage, mixed EEG close to drowsy waking, so it tempts; but 'paradoxical' refers to the clash between an active brain and a paralysed body, which belongs to REM.", "traps": {"a": "Stage 1 is the light transition into sleep, with theta waves and hypnic jerks; its EEG is close to drowsiness, but muscle tone remains and it is not called paradoxical.", "b": "Stage 2 is marked by sleep spindles and K-complexes, EEG features never seen in alert waking.", "c": "Stage 3 is deep slow-wave sleep with high-amplitude delta waves, the least waking-like EEG of all."}}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of", "concept": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both examples of punishment. B. F. Skinner (1938) classified consequences by two questions. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. This gives four cells. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet); both increase behaviour. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding), and negative punishment removes something pleasant, as in time-out from reinforcement or response cost (a fine, losing points); both decrease behaviour. The stem describes exactly the two punishment cells. Edward Thorndike (1911), in his law of effect, had already held that responses followed by discomfort are weakened. Punishment can suppress behaviour quickly, but Skinner argued that the suppression is often temporary and can teach fear, aggression and avoidance of the punisher, so it works best when it is immediate, consistent and paired with reinforcement of a better behaviour. Example: when ten-year-old Kabir hits his sister, his mother scolds him (positive punishment) and takes away his cricket bat for the weekend (negative punishment). Both aim to make hitting less frequent. Option b is right because what defines punishment is its effect, a decrease in the behaviour, whether a stimulus is added or removed. Exam trap: the word 'removal' makes many students choose negative reinforcement (option a); but negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant and increases behaviour, whereas removing something pleasant to reduce behaviour is negative punishment.", "traps": {"a": "Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus (switching off a shock, ending nagging) and so increases behaviour; it is not punishment.", "c": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus such as praise or food to increase behaviour, the reverse of both procedures in the stem.", "d": "Secondary (conditioned) reinforcement uses a learned reinforcer such as money or tokens, valued through pairing with primary reinforcers; it strengthens behaviour rather than weakening it."}}, {"q": "Group therapy can be advantageous when an individual:", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe when group therapy is advantageous. Group therapy treats several clients together with one or two therapists. Joseph Pratt (1905) began it in Boston with classes for poor tuberculosis patients and found that their mutual support lifted morale. Irvin Yalom (1970), in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy, named eleven therapeutic factors that explain why groups help, and each option matches some of them. Option a, working out problems in the presence of others, suits problems that are interpersonal in nature. The group becomes a social microcosm in which members soon behave as they do in life, so relationship difficulties, isolation, loneliness and fear of rejection show up in the room and can be worked on through interpersonal learning, the development of socialising techniques and universality, the relief of finding that others share the same struggle. Option b, needing comfort and support, matches group cohesiveness (a sense of belonging), instillation of hope (seeing others improve) and altruism (the boost to self-worth from helping others). Option c, benefiting from watching others, matches imitative behaviour: seeing how another member faces a fear or handles conflict teaches without one having to go first, much as Albert Bandura (1977) described observational learning. Groups also save therapist time and cost, which matters in busy hospital clinics. Example: Sameer, a lonely college student who feels no one understands him, joins a group; he finds others feel the same, receives warm support, and learns to speak up by watching a quieter member practise assertiveness. Option d is right because a, b and c are each recognised advantages that groups offer. Exam trap: each option alone looks like a complete answer, especially a, which reads like a full definition; when every option names a real Yalom factor, choose 'all of the above'.", "traps": {"a": "True: interpersonal problems such as loneliness and rejection are best tackled where they arise, in the group as a social microcosm (Irvin Yalom, 1970); but b and c are also advantages, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "True: cohesiveness, hope and support are core Yalom factors, but this is only one of the three advantages listed.", "c": "True: imitative behaviour, learning by watching other members, is a Yalom factor, but a and b are equally valid, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Any obstacle in communication process is called?", "concept": "The answer is c: any obstacle in the communication process is called noise. Communication moves a message from a sender to a receiver. The sender encodes an idea into symbols and sends it through a channel (speech, print, a phone); the receiver decodes it and gives feedback. Claude Shannon and Warren Weaver (1949) added noise to this model: anything that interferes with the message between encoding and decoding, so that what is received differs from what was sent. Noise is not only sound. Physical noise is outside interference, such as traffic, a weak network or a smudged page. Physiological noise comes from the body: hunger, poor hearing, fatigue. Psychological noise is inner distraction or bias: worry, prejudice, daydreaming. Semantic noise arises when words mean different things to sender and receiver, as with jargon or a word with two senses. Example: Professor Iyer explains 'reliability' while a drill runs next door (physical noise), Rahul is anxious about his rent (psychological noise), and Pooja takes 'reliability' in its everyday sense rather than the statistical one (semantic noise): three kinds of noise in one lecture. Option c is right because noise is the technical term for every barrier that distorts or blocks a message. Exam trap: 'inner process' sounds like psychological noise, but it simply names thinking, or intrapersonal communication, not an obstacle; noise is the umbrella term for all barriers.", "traps": {"a": "Encroachment is intrusion on another person's territory or personal space, a term from territoriality research (Stanford Lyman and Marvin Scott, 1967), not the name for barriers to communication.", "b": "Plumpness has no meaning in communication theory; it is a filler distractor.", "d": "Inner process refers to the thinking that goes on within a person (intrapersonal communication), not to a barrier between sender and receiver."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "concept": "The answer is d: information on treatment options is not provided in DSM-IV-TR. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, it has used explicit diagnostic criteria and a descriptive approach that is neutral about causes. DSM-IV (1994) was updated as DSM-IV-TR (2000), the text revision, which mainly refreshed the descriptive text for each disorder with newer research while leaving the criteria almost unchanged. For each disorder the manual uses standard headings: diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory and physical findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern, differential diagnosis, and finally the boxed criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present. So options a, b and c are all in the manual: a is the essential features, b the associated features, and c the criteria set. What it leaves out is treatment. The DSM is a tool for classification and diagnosis, not a treatment guide; choosing therapy is left to clinical judgement, outcome research and separate practice guidelines. Example: Dr Kavita looks up obsessive-compulsive disorder in DSM-IV-TR. She finds the essential features, the usual age of onset, the course, the prevalence and the criteria she must check; to choose between exposure and response prevention and an SSRI, she must turn to the treatment literature. Option d is right because DSM-IV-TR describes and defines disorders but deliberately says nothing about how to treat them. Exam trap: the word 'manual' makes students assume it tells clinicians what to do; it tells them what to call a problem, not how to treat it, and the same holds for DSM-5 and ICD-11.", "traps": {"a": "True: each disorder's text opens with its diagnostic (essential) features, so this information is provided.", "b": "True: DSM-IV-TR has an 'associated features and disorders' section for each disorder, including laboratory findings, so this is provided.", "c": "True: the boxed diagnostic criteria list the symptoms that must be present, the core of the manual."}}, {"q": "Mommy shows her boy five black and three white marbles and asks him \"Are there more marbles or more black marbles?\" He replied \"More black ones!\". According to Piaget, the child is in", "concept": "The answer is a: the child who says there are 'more black ones' is in Piaget's pre-operational stage. Jean Piaget (1952) described a pre-operational stage, from about 2 to 7 years, when children can use symbols and language but cannot yet perform operations, mental actions that are reversible and logically organised. The marble question tests class inclusion: understanding that a subclass (black marbles) is part of a larger class (marbles), so the whole must be at least as large as any of its parts. Here there are 8 marbles, 5 black and 3 white, so there are more marbles than black marbles. The pre-operational child fails because of centration: he fixes on the most striking contrast, black versus white, and compares the two subclasses instead of comparing a subclass with the whole. Holding the part and the whole in mind at once needs reversibility, which he lacks. Piaget's own version used wooden beads, mostly brown and a few white: asked 'Are there more brown beads or more wooden beads?', young children said 'more brown'. Class inclusion, like conservation and seriation, is mastered in the concrete operational stage, from about 7 to 11. Example: shown 6 roses and 2 lilies, five-year-old Ishaan says there are more roses than flowers; at 8, his sister Diya laughs, 'Roses are flowers too!' Option a is right because failure of class inclusion is a hallmark of pre-operational thought. Exam trap: James McGarrigle, Robert Grieve and Martin Hughes (1978) showed that rewording the question raises young children's success, so the failure partly reflects language; but in Piaget's scheme this answer still places the child in the pre-operational stage, not 'none of the above'.", "traps": {"b": "Concrete operational children (about 7 to 11) succeed on class inclusion, seeing that the black marbles are part of all the marbles, so they would answer 'more marbles'.", "c": "Formal operational thinkers (about 11 onwards) reason abstractly and hypothetically; class inclusion is easy for them.", "d": "Piaget's stages do cover this response: failing class inclusion is a textbook sign of the pre-operational stage."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a mature defense mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is a: altruism is a mature defence mechanism. Defence mechanisms are mostly unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety by distorting or rechannelling threatening feelings; Sigmund Freud (1894) introduced the idea, and Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, used decades of life-history data on Harvard men in the Grant Study to rank defences in four levels of maturity. Level I, psychotic: delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion. Level II, immature: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out. Level III, neurotic: intellectualisation, isolation, repression, displacement, reaction formation, dissociation. Level IV, mature: altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation and suppression. Men who relied on mature defences had better marriages, careers, health and happiness decades later. Altruism as a defence means gaining satisfaction from constructive service to others, turning one's own pain into help for people in the same position instead of being swamped by it. It is mature because it faces reality and feelings while also strengthening relationships. Example: after losing her brother to suicide, Nisha volunteers on a suicide prevention helpline; her grief is real, but channelling it into helping callers protects her from despair and helps others too. Option a is right because altruism sits at Vaillant's mature level with humour, anticipation, sublimation and suppression. Exam trap: rationalisation and intellectualisation sound grown-up and thoughtful, so they tempt as mature defences; but both distort reality through thinking, and they sit lower, at the neurotic level or below.", "traps": {"b": "Intellectualisation, thinking about a painful event in abstract terms to avoid its feeling, is a neurotic (Level III) defence in George Vaillant's (1977) hierarchy.", "c": "Repression, Sigmund Freud's primary defence of keeping threatening material out of awareness, is neurotic (Level III) for Vaillant, not mature.", "d": "Rationalisation, described by Ernest Jones (1908), invents acceptable reasons for behaviour driven by other motives; the DSM-IV Defensive Functioning Scale (1994) places it at the disavowal level with denial and projection, well below mature."}}, {"q": "Mediated intra-personal communication can be seen in writing:", "concept": "The answer is b: writing a personal diary is mediated intrapersonal communication. Communication is classified by who takes part. Intrapersonal communication is communication with oneself: thinking, self-talk, planning and reflecting. Interpersonal communication is between two or a few people; group communication happens within a small group; organisational communication runs through the formal channels of an organisation; and mass communication reaches large, scattered audiences through the media. Communication is mediated when a medium, such as pen and paper, a phone or a computer, carries the message instead of direct face-to-face speech. Mediated intrapersonal communication therefore needs both features: the sender and the receiver are the same person, and the message passes through a medium. A personal diary is the classic case. The writer records thoughts and feelings in writing, and the only intended reader is the writer, now or later. Example: every night Arjun writes in his diary about his fear of the entrance exam; reading it a month later, he notices how his worry has eased. He is both sender and receiver, and the diary is the medium. Option b is right because a diary is a message to oneself carried through a medium. Exam trap: all four options involve writing, which makes them all mediated, so the real test is who the receiver is; only in the diary is the receiver the writer.", "traps": {"a": "A letter to friends is mediated interpersonal communication: the medium is writing, but the receiver is another person.", "c": "A memo to subordinates is mediated organisational (downward) communication from a superior to staff, not communication with oneself.", "d": "Reports for internal circulation are organisational communication to many readers in the organisation, not intrapersonal communication."}}, {"q": "Which of the following has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "concept": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of behaviour that violates the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate norms: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule-breaking such as truancy. The strongest link is to antisocial personality disorder (ASPD). DSM-5 (2013) diagnoses ASPD only in people aged at least 18 with evidence of conduct disorder beginning before age 15, so adult ASPD is by definition a continuation of childhood CD. The classic evidence on adult crime is Lee Robins (1966), Deviant Children Grown Up, which followed children seen at a St Louis child guidance clinic some 30 years later: the antisocial children became adults with high rates of arrest, imprisonment and sociopathy, and adult sociopathy almost never appeared without antisocial behaviour in childhood. Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated adolescence-limited offending from a life-course-persistent pattern, whose early-onset CD predicts the worst adult outcomes. The link to psychosis comes from the Dunedin cohort in New Zealand, followed from birth: Julia Kim-Cohen and colleagues (2003) found that many adults with schizophreniform disorder had a juvenile history of conduct disorder. Example: Vikram, at 13, bullies classmates, steals and sets fire to a shed; if the pattern persists, he is at high risk of imprisonment and an ASPD diagnosis as an adult, and at some raised risk of later psychotic illness. Option d is right because each of a, b and c has research support. Exam trap: ASPD is the textbook link, so many tick c alone; the stem asks what has been associated with CD, and all three have. Key note: psychosis is the least direct of the three links, resting on cohort data rather than on diagnostic definition, but d stands.", "traps": {"a": "True: Lee Robins (1966) showed that antisocial children often became adults with arrests and prison sentences, but a alone leaves out b and c.", "b": "True, though the least direct link: in the Dunedin cohort, Julia Kim-Cohen and colleagues (2003) found juvenile conduct disorder in many adults with schizophreniform disorder.", "c": "True and the strongest link, since DSM-5 requires conduct disorder before age 15 for antisocial personality disorder; but a and b are also associated, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is false? Social anxiety:", "concept": "The answer is c: it is false that social anxiety is more likely to manifest in adulthood. Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is a marked fear of situations in which one may be scrutinised by others, such as speaking, eating or writing in public, because one fears acting in a way that will be humiliating or embarrassing; the situations are avoided or endured with intense anxiety, typically for 6 months or more. Its onset is early. DSM-5 (2013) gives a median age of onset in the United States of 13 years, with about 75 per cent of cases beginning between 8 and 15; first onset in adulthood is relatively rare and often follows a stressful or humiliating event or a new social role. So c is the false statement and the answer. The other three are true. Option a: social anxiety runs in families, and twin data (Kenneth Kendler and colleagues, 1992) suggest a moderate heritability of about 30 per cent, partly through the behavioural inhibition that Jerome Kagan (1988) found in shy, high-reactive infants. Option b: overprotective, controlling parenting is linked to child anxiety (reviewed by Ronald Rapee, 1997), because it signals that the world is dangerous and denies the child practice at coping. Option d: early experiences of shame, humiliation, teasing or bullying are common; Ann Hackmann, David Clark and Freda McManus (2000) found that the negative self-images in social phobia often trace back to such memories. Example: Ritu was mocked when she stammered during a Class 6 recitation; by 14 she avoids answering in class and eats lunch alone, a typical adolescent onset. Option c is right because social anxiety usually begins in childhood or early adolescence, not adulthood. Exam trap: many adults first seek help in their twenties or later, so students mistake late help-seeking for late onset.", "traps": {"a": "True: twin studies (Kenneth Kendler and colleagues, 1992) show moderate heritability, partly through the inhibited temperament described by Jerome Kagan (1988), so this cannot be the false statement.", "b": "True: overprotective, controlling parenting is a recognised risk factor (Ronald Rapee, 1997), because it limits the child's practice at social coping.", "d": "True: early shaming, teasing or humiliation often seeds the negative self-images found in social anxiety (Ann Hackmann, David Clark and Freda McManus, 2000)."}}, {"q": "Of the following individuals, the one who is credited as being one of the founders of social psychology is", "concept": "The answer is a: Norman Triplett is credited as one of the founders of social psychology. Norman Triplett (1898), working at Indiana University, studied cycling records and noticed that riders were faster when paced by another cyclist or racing rivals than when riding alone against the clock. He then had about 40 children wind fishing reels as fast as possible, alone or beside another child doing the same task. Many wound faster in company, though some did worse. Published in the American Journal of Psychology as 'The dynamogenic factors in pacemaking and competition', it is often called the first experiment in social psychology, and it began research on social facilitation, later explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, which helps on easy or well-learnt tasks and hurts on hard ones. Other founding figures are William McDougall and Edward Ross, who both published textbooks titled Social Psychology in 1908, and Floyd Allport (1924), who made the field experimental. Example: Aarav runs his best 400-metre time at the inter-school meet with rivals beside him, not in solo practice; that is the effect Triplett measured. Option a is right because Triplett's 1898 study is the landmark taken to mark the start of experimental social psychology. Exam trap: the other three are real, well-known social psychologists, so their names feel familiar; but they did their main work in the 1970s to 1990s, long after the field was founded. Some historians, such as Wolfgang Stroebe (2012), argue that Triplett's data gave weaker support than textbooks suggest, but he is still the standard answer to this question.", "traps": {"b": "Kenneth Gergen (1973) wrote 'Social psychology as history' and led social constructionism; he is an influential critic, not a founder.", "c": "David Sears (1986) is known for political psychology and for his critique of social psychology's reliance on college students, decades after the field began.", "d": "Harry Triandis (1995) is a pioneer of cross-cultural psychology, known for individualism and collectivism, not a founder of social psychology."}}, {"q": "The auditory nerve, which transmits information from the ear to the brain, is an example of an", "concept": "The answer is a: the auditory nerve is an afferent nerve, because it carries sensory messages from the ear inward to the brain. The peripheral nervous system has two directions of traffic. Afferent (sensory) fibres carry signals from receptors toward the central nervous system; efferent (motor) fibres carry commands away from it to muscles and glands. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent; afferent fibres arrive, efferent fibres exit. The split was first shown at the spinal cord by Charles Bell (1811) and Francois Magendie (1822): the dorsal roots carry sensory input in, and the ventral roots carry motor output out (the Bell-Magendie law). Interneurons inside the brain and spinal cord link the two, as in the reflex arc. The auditory nerve is the cochlear branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve, cranial nerve VIII. Hair cells in the cochlea transduce sound vibrations into neural impulses, and the nerve carries these to the cochlear nuclei of the brainstem, then on through the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. Because its job is to bring information in, cranial nerve VIII is classed as a sensory nerve. Example: when Meera hears her name called in a crowded market, the message travels along her afferent auditory nerve to her brain; the turn of her head that follows uses efferent motor nerves. Option a is right because afferent means conducting toward the central nervous system, which is exactly what a nerve that transmits information from the ear to the brain does. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, offering efferent for a sensory nerve, and they add labels such as autonomic that name a division of the nervous system rather than the direction in which a nerve conducts.", "traps": {"b": "Efferent nerves carry motor commands away from the brain and spinal cord to muscles and glands, such as the facial nerve moving the face; the auditory nerve carries information the other way, into the brain.", "c": "Autonomic refers to the autonomic nervous system (sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions) that runs involuntary organs such as the heart and gut; it names a division, not a direction of conduction, and hearing is a special sense, not an autonomic function.", "d": "An analgesic is a pain-relieving drug such as paracetamol or morphine; it is not a kind of nerve at all."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements are correct, since research objectives may be phrased as questions or as statements and are presented in the first chapter of a thesis. An objective states what a study sets out to find or do. It grows out of the research problem: the researcher narrows a broad area to a specific problem, raises questions about it, and turns them into objectives, which then guide the hypotheses, design, sample and analysis. Objectives can take two grammatical forms. In statement form they usually begin with an action verb: 'To study the relationship between screen time and sleep quality among college students.' In question form the same aim reads: 'Is there a relationship between screen time and sleep quality among college students?' Both are accepted; the question form is often called a research question. Good objectives are specific, clear, measurable and achievable within the limits of the study. In a standard thesis, Chapter I (Introduction) presents the background, the statement of the problem, its significance, the objectives, the hypotheses and the operational definitions of the main terms. Chapter II reviews the literature, Chapter III describes the method, and the results, discussion and summary follow. Example: Kabir, an M.Phil scholar, writes in Chapter I: 'Objective 1: To assess the level of exam anxiety in Class 12 students. Objective 2: Do boys and girls differ in exam anxiety?' Both lines are legitimate objectives, and both sit in the introductory chapter. Option d is right because a, b and c are each true, and only d includes all of them. Exam trap: students pick one form, thinking objectives must be statements and that questions belong only to hypotheses; the examiner wants you to see that both forms are allowed and that objectives belong in the introduction, not in the method or results chapter.", "traps": {"a": "True: objectives can be framed as questions ('Is there a relationship...?'), often called research questions; but b and c are also true, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "True: the commonest form is a statement beginning 'To study' or 'To assess'; but the question form is allowed too and c also holds, so b alone is incomplete.", "c": "True: in a standard thesis Chapter I (Introduction) states the problem, objectives and hypotheses; but a and b are also correct, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "According to Gould, a \"crisis of questions,\" where an individual ponders the meaning of life, occurs during", "concept": "The answer is c: Roger Gould placed the questioning of life's meaning in middle adulthood, in what he called the mid-life decade, roughly 35 to 45. Roger Gould (1978), an American psychiatrist, set out his theory in Transformations: Growth and Change in Adult Life. He argued that adults grow by shedding false assumptions carried over from childhood, beliefs that once gave a feeling of safety but later block maturity. Each phase of adulthood challenges one of them. From about 16 to 22 people question 'I will always belong to my parents and believe in their world.' From about 22 to 28 they test 'Doing things my parents' way, with will power and perseverance, will bring results, and they will rescue me if I cannot cope.' From about 28 to 34 they face 'Life is simple and controllable.' In the mid-life decade, roughly 35 to 45, comes the deepest challenge: 'There is no evil or death in the world.' Awareness of mortality, the sense that time is running out, children growing up and limits at work push people to ask what their life means and what they truly want. After about 45, those who work through this reach a more settled, self-accepting adult consciousness. Gould's account sits beside Daniel Levinson's (1978) midlife transition at 40 to 45 and Elliott Jaques's (1965) coinage of the term midlife crisis. Example: Arjun, 42, a successful bank manager, suddenly asks whether his work means anything, thinks about his father's recent death and wonders what he wants from the years left to him. That is Gould's mid-life questioning. Option c is right because Gould's questioning of meaning, driven by a new sense of mortality, falls in the mid-life decade, which belongs to middle adulthood. Exam trap: the word crisis makes students jump to adolescence and Erik Erikson's (1950) identity crisis; Gould's crisis is about meaning and mortality, and it peaks at midlife, not in the teens.", "traps": {"a": "Adolescence is where Erik Erikson (1950) placed the identity crisis; in Gould's scheme the late teens only begin the break from the parents' world, not the questioning of life's meaning.", "b": "Early adulthood in Gould's scheme (about 22 to 34) is about building one's own life and learning that life is not simple and controllable; the deeper questioning of meaning and mortality comes later.", "d": "Old age is the time of Erik Erikson's integrity versus despair and Robert Peck's (1956) ego transcendence; Gould's theory centres on false assumptions shed before about 45."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following options are the main tasks of research in modern society? i. To keep pace with the advancement in knowledge, ii. To discover new things, iii. To write a critique on the earlier writings, iv. To systematically examine and critically analyze the investigations/sources with objectivity", "concept": "The answer is a: statements i, ii and iv (keeping pace with knowledge, discovering new things, and objective critical examination of sources) are the main tasks of research in modern society, while writing a critique of earlier writings is not. Research is a careful, systematic search for new knowledge. In a modern society that runs on science, technology and evidence-based policy, it serves three linked tasks. First, it keeps pace with the advancement of knowledge: findings age quickly, so fields such as medicine, psychology and education must keep updating what they know. Second, it discovers new things: new facts, relations, theories, tools and solutions to practical problems, from a vaccine to a better school curriculum. Third, it examines investigations and sources systematically and critically, with objectivity, so that conclusions rest on evidence rather than on opinion, authority or tradition. Charles Sanders Peirce (1877) called science the only way of fixing belief that corrects its own mistakes, and these tasks are what keep that self-correction going. Writing a critique of earlier writings is a scholarly or literary exercise. It happens inside research as part of the literature review, but on its own it creates no new knowledge and is not one of research's main tasks in society. Example: Meera, a clinical psychologist in Pune, wants to know whether an online CBT app reduces anxiety in college students. She reads recent trials to stay current (i), runs her own trial to find out something new (ii), and analyses her data and her sources objectively (iv). A book review she writes for a journal is a separate activity. Option a is right because it is the only choice that contains i, ii and iv and leaves out iii. Exam trap: option a lists the numbers in an odd order (iv, ii, i), which makes students distrust it; the order does not matter, only which statements are included.", "traps": {"b": "It includes iii, writing a critique of earlier writings, which is a literary or review activity rather than a main task of research, and it leaves out iv, objective critical analysis, which is central.", "c": "It keeps iii and drops ii and iv; discovering new things and objective analysis are the heart of research, so this combination misses its main tasks.", "d": "It contains ii and iv but adds iii and drops i; keeping pace with the advancement of knowledge is a main task, while critique writing is not."}}, {"q": "A person who has an extreme lack of self-confidence and who allows others to run his or her life is said to have a(n) ______ personality.", "concept": "The answer is d: an extreme lack of self-confidence, together with letting others run one's life, is the core picture of dependent personality disorder. Dependent personality disorder belongs to DSM-5 Cluster C, the anxious-fearful cluster, along with avoidant and obsessive-compulsive personality disorders. Its essence is a pervasive and excessive need to be taken care of, which leads to submissive, clinging behaviour and fear of separation, beginning by early adulthood. DSM-5 requires five of eight features: difficulty making everyday decisions without excessive advice and reassurance; needing others to take responsibility for major areas of life; difficulty disagreeing for fear of losing support; difficulty starting projects alone because of a lack of self-confidence rather than of motivation; going to great lengths to obtain care, even volunteering for unpleasant tasks; feeling helpless when alone; urgently seeking a new relationship when one ends; and unrealistic fear of being left to care for oneself. Theodore Millon (1981) described this as the passive-dependent pattern: the person sees himself as weak and others as strong and protective, and trades autonomy for security. Example: Riya, 30, lets her mother choose her clothes, her course and her husband, cannot decide what to cook without phoning someone, and agrees with everyone so that no one will leave her. Option d is right because both parts of the stem, extreme lack of self-confidence and allowing others to run one's life, are defining criteria of dependent personality disorder and of no other option. Exam trap: avoidant personality disorder is the near miss. The avoidant person also feels inadequate, but withdraws from relationships for fear of rejection, whereas the dependent person clings to relationships and hands over control. Both are Cluster C, so tell them apart by direction: avoidant moves away from people, dependent moves toward them.", "traps": {"a": "Antisocial personality disorder (Cluster B) is a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights, with deceit, impulsivity and lack of remorse; such people run over others rather than letting others run their lives.", "b": "Narcissistic personality disorder (Cluster B) is grandiosity, a need for admiration and lack of empathy; it shows inflated, not deficient, self-confidence.", "c": "Paranoid personality disorder (Cluster A) is pervasive distrust and suspicion of others' motives; a paranoid person would not hand control of his life to others."}}, {"q": "Delusions shared between two closely connected persons is also known as", "concept": "The answer is d: a delusion shared by two closely connected people is called folie à deux, French for 'madness of two'. Charles Lasegue and Jules Falret (1877) described it. Typically a dominant primary person has a genuine psychotic disorder with a fixed delusion, and a more passive secondary partner, closely bound to the primary and often socially isolated, comes to share the same belief. The pair are usually relatives, such as spouses, siblings or parent and child, living together and cut off from outside contact. The main clinical point is that separation often lets the secondary partner's belief fade, while the primary's persists. Variants include folie imposée (the delusion is imposed on a healthy partner), folie simultanée (two predisposed people fall ill together) and folie à trois or folie à famille when more people share it. ICD-10 coded it as induced delusional disorder (F24) and DSM-IV as shared psychotic disorder. DSM-5 dropped the separate category: the partner is diagnosed with delusional disorder if the criteria are met, otherwise with other specified schizophrenia spectrum disorder (delusional symptoms in the partner of an individual with delusional disorder). Example: Meera's elderly mother believes the neighbours are poisoning their water. Meera, who lives alone with her and rarely goes out, begins boiling every drop and accusing the neighbours too. When Meera moves to a hostel for her course, her belief slowly weakens; her mother's does not. Option d is right because folie à deux is the specific term for a delusion shared by two people in a close relationship. Exam trap: the distractors are other French terms from descriptive psychopathology. Déjà vu and déjà entendu are disturbances of recognition memory, and derealisation is a change in how the world feels; none of them is a delusion, and none is shared.", "traps": {"a": "Derealisation is the feeling that the outside world is unreal, dreamlike or distant, with reality testing intact; it belongs to depersonalisation-derealisation disorder, not to shared beliefs.", "b": "Déjà vu is the feeling that a new situation has been seen or lived before, a disturbance of recognition memory found in healthy people and in temporal lobe epilepsy; it is not a delusion.", "c": "Déjà entendu is the feeling of having already heard something that is new, the auditory cousin of déjà vu; it is a memory illusion, not a shared delusion."}}, {"q": "Effectiveness of teaching depends on", "concept": "The answer is d: the effectiveness of teaching depends above all on the teacher's understanding of the subject. A teacher can only help students understand what he himself understands. Deep command of the subject lets a teacher explain an idea in more than one way, choose good examples, foresee the usual mistakes, answer unexpected questions and connect the topic to students' lives. Lee Shulman (1986) called the most useful form of this pedagogical content knowledge: knowing a subject in the special way needed to teach it, such as which analogy makes fractions clear to a ten-year-old. Personality matters too (warmth, enthusiasm, fairness), but a pleasant teacher who does not understand the subject cannot teach it well, while a clear, knowledgeable teacher can be effective with many kinds of temperament. Formal qualification shows that a course was passed, not that the subject was understood. Example: Kabir and Ananya both hold an M.Sc. in physics. Ananya truly understands why objects float, so she turns a bucket of water into a lively lesson; Kabir recites a memorised definition of buoyancy and freezes when a student asks how a steel ship floats. Same qualification, very different teaching. Option d is right because understanding of the subject is the foundation on which explanation, questioning and every other teaching skill rests. Exam trap: qualification sounds official and personality sounds modern, but teaching aptitude questions reward the factor that most directly produces learning, which is the teacher's grasp of the subject.", "traps": {"a": "Qualification is a paper credential showing that courses were passed; two equally qualified teachers can differ greatly in how well they teach, so effectiveness does not rest on it.", "b": "Personality (warmth, enthusiasm) helps classroom climate, but without understanding of the subject a pleasant teacher cannot explain content well; it supports teaching rather than founding it.", "c": "Neat handwriting helps blackboard work but is a minor mechanical skill; it has little to do with whether students learn."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following measures is to make research more ethical?", "concept": "The answer is a: informed consent is the measure that makes research more ethical. Informed consent means that a person agrees to take part in research freely, after being told what the study involves, and while able to understand that information. It has four parts: information (the purpose, procedures, duration, risks, benefits and the use of data), comprehension (explained in language the person understands), voluntariness (no coercion or undue inducement) and competence (the capacity to decide; for children, their assent plus a parent's permission). Participants must also know that they can withdraw at any time without penalty. The principle grew out of abuse. After Nazi doctors experimented on prisoners, the Nuremberg Code (1947) made the voluntary consent of the human subject its first and absolutely essential rule. The Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), with its principle of respect for persons, built on it. In India the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) require informed consent, usually in writing, and the APA Ethics Code (2002, Standard 8.02) requires it for psychological research. Example: before Arjun interviews flood survivors in Assam about trauma, he explains the study in Assamese, warns that some questions may be upsetting, gives a counsellor's number, promises confidentiality and makes clear they can stop at any point. Only then does each person sign. Option a is right because informed consent directly protects participants' autonomy and is the central ethical safeguard in research with people. Exam trap: 'all the above' tempts students because the other options sound responsible, but a pilot study is a methodological trial run and government regulation is an outside control, not a measure the researcher takes to make a study ethical.", "traps": {"b": "A pilot study is a small trial run to test instruments, procedures and feasibility before the main study; it may reveal problems, but its purpose is methodological, not ethical.", "c": "Government regulation (laws and agencies) is an external control on research, not a measure researchers build into a study; ethics in practice rests on consent, ethics committees and professional codes.", "d": "Because a pilot study and government regulation are not ethical measures in themselves, 'all the above' fails; informed consent is the direct ethical safeguard."}}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "concept": "The answer is c: functional analysis rests on operant conditioning, the principle that behaviour is maintained by its consequences. Operant conditioning was developed by B. F. Skinner (1938): behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and behaviour that is not reinforced, or is punished, becomes less likely. Functional analysis applies this to a problem behaviour by asking what the behaviour does for the person. The clinician defines the target behaviour precisely and records its ABC: the Antecedent (what happens just before), the Behaviour, and the Consequence (what follows). Problem behaviours usually serve one of four functions: gaining attention, escaping demands, getting tangible items, or automatic (sensory) reinforcement. Brian Iwata and colleagues (1982) turned this into an experimental method for self-injury, deliberately varying conditions (attention, academic demand, alone, play) to see which one kept the behaviour going. Strictly, functional analysis is an assessment rather than a full therapy, but it drives treatment in behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis: remove the reinforcer that maintains the problem and reinforce an alternative behaviour that serves the same function. Example: Kabir, 7, throws his workbook whenever he is given maths sums, and the teacher sends him out of class. The ABC record shows that the consequence is escape from maths, so the behaviour is negatively reinforced. The plan keeps him in class, breaks tasks into small steps and teaches him to ask for a short break instead. Option c is right because the whole logic of functional analysis, consequences maintaining behaviour, comes from operant conditioning. Exam trap: because functional analysis is used in behaviour therapy, students choose classical conditioning, which underlies systematic desensitisation and exposure; but classical conditioning is about pairings of stimuli, not about consequences.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) explains how stimuli become linked to responses and underlies systematic desensitisation and flooding; functional analysis examines consequences, which is operant.", "b": "Humanistic principles (Carl Rogers, 1951; Abraham Maslow, 1954) stress subjective experience and growth; humanistic therapy does not use an ABC analysis of observable behaviour.", "d": "Psychodynamic principles (Sigmund Freud, 1900) explain behaviour by unconscious conflict; their analysis is interpretation of hidden meaning, not recording of antecedents and consequences."}}, {"q": "Which of the following changes is not caused by activity of the sympathetic nervous system?", "concept": "The answer is d: pupil dilation, decreased digestive secretions and a faster heart are all caused by sympathetic activity, so none of a, b or c is the exception. The autonomic nervous system controls involuntary organs and has two main divisions that usually act in opposition. The sympathetic division prepares the body for emergency action, what Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, most of its effects use noradrenaline, and adrenaline from the adrenal medulla backs them up. The parasympathetic division, from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the opposite, 'rest and digest', using acetylcholine. Check each option. (a) Sympathetic fibres contract the radial dilator muscle of the iris, so the pupil widens and lets in more light; parasympathetic fibres in cranial nerve III constrict it. (b) Sympathetic activity reduces salivation and digestive secretions and slows the gut, since digestion can wait in an emergency; this is why the mouth goes dry before an interview. (c) It speeds and strengthens the heartbeat, sending more blood to the muscles. It also widens the airways, releases glucose from the liver and causes sweating. Example: Arjun is walking home at night when a dog leaps at him. His pupils widen, his mouth goes dry, his heart pounds and his breathing quickens, all within seconds and all sympathetic. Once he is safe at home and eating dinner, parasympathetic activity slows his heart and restarts digestion. Option d is right because all three listed changes are classic sympathetic effects, so there is no change among a to c that the sympathetic system fails to cause. Exam trap: students mix up the divisions on digestion and the pupil; remember that sympathetic means dilated pupils, a dry mouth and halted digestion, and parasympathetic reverses each one.", "traps": {"a": "Dilation of the pupil is caused by the sympathetic system through the iris dilator muscle; the parasympathetic system constricts the pupil, so a is not the exception.", "b": "A decrease in digestive secretions is sympathetic: in fight or flight, saliva and gut activity are suppressed, while parasympathetic activity increases them, so b is not the exception.", "c": "Acceleration of heart action is sympathetic, through noradrenaline and adrenaline acting on the heart; the vagus nerve (parasympathetic) slows it, so c is not the exception."}}, {"q": "In south and east Asia, a man may experience Koro, which is", "concept": "The answer is a: koro is an acute anxiety that the penis is shrinking or retracting into the body, often with the fear that full retraction will cause death. Koro is a classic culture-bound syndrome, now called a cultural concept of distress: a pattern of suffering shaped by, and mainly found in, particular cultures. It is reported in southern China, where it is called suo yang, in Southeast Asia, and in north-east India. The person, usually a young man, suddenly becomes convinced that his genitals are retracting. He panics, may tie or clamp the penis or have relatives hold it, and seeks urgent help. In women the fear may concern the breasts or vulva. Koro can spread in epidemics carried by rumour, as in Singapore in 1967 and in outbreaks in north-east India and West Bengal in the 1980s. Local beliefs about sexual energy, semen loss and spirit attack give the symptom its meaning. Pow Meng Yap (1965) described koro in detail and helped build the idea of culture-bound syndromes. DSM-5 lists koro as an example of other specified obsessive-compulsive and related disorder, beside other body-focused worries. Example: during a koro scare in his village, Arjun, 19, wakes at night sure that his penis is shrinking, shouts for his brothers to hold it and is rushed to the health centre. Examination is normal, and reassurance with an explanation of anxiety brings relief. Option a is right because koro is defined by anxiety about the penis receding into the body. Exam trap: koro is often confused with dhat syndrome, the South Asian anxiety about losing semen in the urine (named by N. N. Wig, 1960); both involve genital worries, but dhat is about semen loss and weakness, while koro is about retraction.", "traps": {"b": "Insanity is an old legal term for mental incapacity, as in the M'Naghten rules (1843); it is not a syndrome and does not describe koro's specific genital fear.", "c": "Depression is a mood disorder of persistent low mood and loss of interest; koro is acute, panic-like anxiety about the genitals, not low mood.", "d": "Schizophrenia is a chronic psychotic disorder with delusions, hallucinations and negative symptoms; koro is an acute, culturally shaped anxiety state that usually settles with reassurance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is TRUE?", "concept": "The answer is c: siblings of people with schizophrenia have a higher lifetime risk of mental health problems, including schizophrenia itself. Schizophrenia runs in families. Pooling European family and twin studies, Irving Gottesman (1991) estimated a lifetime risk of about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for the child of one affected parent, 17% for a fraternal twin and about 48% for an identical twin. Risk rises with the share of genes, but environment matters too, since even an identical co-twin falls ill only about half the time. Siblings also show raised rates of related problems such as schizotypal traits, depression and substance misuse, through shared genes and shared stress. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent, and they are more often victims than perpetrators of violence. Hearing voices or holding unusual beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: such experiences occur in bereavement, sleep deprivation, drug use, severe depression, bipolar disorder and PTSD, and in a minority of healthy people. Example: Riya's elder brother was diagnosed with schizophrenia at 22. Riya is not doomed, but her risk is higher than her classmates', so her doctor advises her to avoid cannabis and to seek help early if problems appear. Option c is right because it is the only statement firmly supported by family studies; a, b and d overstate, overgeneralise or stigmatise. Exam trap: d sounds factual because the media link schizophrenia with murder, but the great majority of homicides are committed by people without schizophrenia. Key note: large registry studies (Seena Fazel and colleagues, 2009) do find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, much of it linked to substance misuse, although the absolute risk stays very small; d is written as the popular myth and c is the clearly true statement, so the key used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is a myth: most people with schizophrenia are never violent and are more often victims of violence; any raised risk is concentrated in untreated illness with substance misuse.", "b": "False: voices and delusions also occur in mood disorders, PTSD, drug states, dementia, bereavement and some healthy people; schizophrenia needs a fuller pattern lasting at least 6 months (DSM-5).", "d": "Put this way it is a stigmatising myth: homicide by a person with schizophrenia is very rare, and almost all homicides are committed by people without the disorder."}}, {"q": "Identify the TRUE statement:", "concept": "The answer is a: the main purpose of training is to equip a candidate with a specific skill. Teaching aptitude papers separate several related ideas. Training develops particular skills or habits through practice, usually for a definite job: typing, driving, giving an IQ test. Its aim is competent performance of a defined task. Teaching is broader: it aims at understanding, reasoning and the growth of the whole learner, and it respects the learner's right to ask why. Instruction is the organised presentation of knowledge or directions so that someone learns, and it is interactive: the teacher explains, the learner responds, the teacher gives feedback. Indoctrination is getting someone to accept beliefs without regard to evidence, so that they are not questioned. Thomas F. Green (1971), in The Activities of Teaching, placed training and instructing close to the centre of teaching and indoctrinating at its edge, near propaganda. Now test the statements. Statement a is true by definition. Statement b fails in its second half: indoctrination does not need teaching, since propaganda, constant repetition, ritual, peer pressure and the media can indoctrinate without any lesson. Statement c is false because instruction always involves interaction between teacher and learner. So d, 'all the above', fails too. Example: Arjun's driving school trains him to parallel-park, which is training for a specific skill. His physics teacher explaining why a car skids on a wet road is teaching for understanding. Option a is right because equipping a person with a specific skill is exactly what training is for, and it is the only fully true statement. Exam trap: b sounds balanced and philosophical, so students accept it; read both halves, and notice that indoctrination can happen with no teaching at all.", "traps": {"b": "Its first half is true (good teaching avoids indoctrination), but the second half is false: indoctrination can occur through propaganda, repetition or social pressure without any teaching.", "c": "False: instruction is an interactive process in which the teacher presents material and the learner responds and receives feedback; without interaction it is mere broadcasting.", "d": "Because b and c are false, 'all the above' cannot be true; only a is fully correct."}}, {"q": "The MMPI measures which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: the MMPI measures mood, physical concerns and social attitudes, along with much else. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota. It is a true-false self-report inventory (567 items in the MMPI-2 of James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) made by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if patients in a diagnostic group answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. Multiphasic means that it samples many areas of personality and psychopathology at once. Its original item pool was sorted into some two dozen content areas, including general health, bodily symptoms, family, work, sexual, religious, political and social attitudes, depressed and manic mood, phobias and morale. The ten clinical scales reflect this breadth. Mood: scale 2 (Depression) and scale 9 (Hypomania), with scale 7 (Psychasthenia) for anxiety. Physical concerns: scale 1 (Hypochondriasis) and scale 3 (Hysteria), the somatic side of the neurotic triad. Social attitudes: scale 4 (Psychopathic Deviate) taps conflict with authority and social rules, and scale 0 (Social Introversion) taps comfort with people; content scales such as Cynicism and Antisocial Practices add more. Validity scales (L, F, K) check how the person approached the test. Example: Arjun, 35, complains of headaches and stomach pain with no medical cause. His MMPI-2 shows high scores on scales 1, 2 and 3, a pattern that points the psychologist toward somatic and depressive concerns rather than physical illness. Option d is right because mood, physical concerns and social attitudes are each measured by MMPI items and scales, so all three are correct. Exam trap: students think the MMPI measures only clinical syndromes; it is a broad multiphasic inventory, and social attitudes were one of its original item categories.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: mood is tapped by scale 2 (Depression) and scale 9 (Hypomania), but the MMPI also covers bodily and social content, so a alone is not the best answer.", "b": "True but incomplete: physical concerns are tapped by scales 1 (Hypochondriasis) and 3 (Hysteria), yet mood and social attitudes are measured too.", "c": "True but incomplete: social attitudes appear in scale 4 (Psychopathic Deviate), scale 0 (Social Introversion) and the content scales, but they are only one part of what the MMPI covers."}}, {"q": "In which of the following ways a theory is not different from a belief?", "concept": "The answer is b: acceptability is the one respect in which a theory is not different from a belief, because both are things people accept as true. A belief is a proposition a person accepts, often on the strength of tradition, authority or feeling, without asking for evidence. A scientific theory is a set of interrelated concepts, definitions and propositions that explains and predicts phenomena by specifying relations among variables; Fred Kerlinger (1964) gave this classic definition in Foundations of Behavioral Research. Both a belief and a theory are accepted by those who hold them, so acceptability does not separate them. What separates them is how each stands to evidence. A theory states antecedent-consequent relations: if certain conditions are present, certain outcomes follow, so it explains causes and predicts effects. A theory is verifiable: its predictions can be tested and could turn out false, which Karl Popper (1934) made the mark of science. And a theory is demonstrable: its support can be shown publicly to others, who can repeat the observations. A belief needs none of these. Example: Meera's grandmother believes that exams go well if you eat curd and sugar before leaving home. Meera, studying psychology, learns the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908), which predicts that performance rises with arousal up to a point and then falls, and which can be tested in experiments. Both ideas are accepted by the people who hold them; only the second states testable cause-and-effect relations that can be shown to others. Option b is right because acceptance is common to both, while antecedent-consequent structure, verifiability and demonstrability belong to theory alone. Exam trap: the word not in the stem reverses the usual question; you are looking for the shared feature, not the differences.", "traps": {"a": "A theory specifies antecedent-consequent (cause and effect) relations among variables, which lets it explain and predict; a belief need not, so this is a real difference and not the answer.", "c": "A theory is verifiable: its predictions can be tested and could be shown false (Karl Popper's falsifiability); a belief is held whatever the evidence, so this is a difference.", "d": "A theory is demonstrable: its evidence can be shown publicly and repeated by others; a belief rests on private conviction, so this is a difference."}}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her", "concept": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe, which houses the primary visual cortex. Visual information leaves the retina along the optic nerves. At the optic chiasm the fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross, so each optic tract carries the opposite half of the visual field. The tracts reach the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and the optic radiations carry the signal to the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, Korbinian Brodmann's (1909) area 17) around the calcarine sulcus at the back of the occipital lobe. V1 is mapped point by point onto the retina, so damage to part of it removes part of the visual field. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which supplies the occipital lobe, typically causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia: loss of the same half of the visual field in both eyes, on the side opposite the lesion. Central vision is often spared (macular sparing), because the occipital pole also receives blood from the middle cerebral artery. Damage to both occipital lobes causes cortical blindness. Example: after a stroke in her right occipital lobe, Kamala, 70, cannot see cars coming from her left unless she turns her head, although her eyes themselves are healthy. She knows the left half of her view is missing and learns to scan toward it: a left homonymous hemianopia. Option a is right because the occipital lobe contains the primary visual cortex, and losing part of it produces partial blindness. Exam trap: the parietal lobe is the near miss, since a right parietal stroke causes hemispatial neglect, in which the patient ignores the left side; but neglect is a failure of attention, and the person is not blind in that field.", "traps": {"b": "The parietal lobe holds the somatosensory cortex and spatial attention; a right parietal stroke causes hemispatial neglect, an attention deficit, not true blindness.", "c": "The temporal lobes handle hearing, memory and object recognition; a temporal lesion can clip Meyer's loop and cut an upper quarter of the field, but blindness after stroke classically points to the occipital lobe.", "d": "The reticular formation in the brainstem regulates arousal and the sleep-wake cycle (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949); damage causes drowsiness or coma, not partial blindness."}}, {"q": "Some proverbs/idioms are given below together with their meanings. Choose the correct meaning of proverb/idiom. To keep one's temper", "concept": "The answer is b: 'to keep one's temper' means to stay calm and not become angry, and of the four options 'to be in good mood' is the closest. Temper here means a person's state of mind, especially control over anger. To keep your temper is to stay calm and in control even when provoked; to lose your temper is to become suddenly angry. Example: when the bus conductor shouted at her for not having change, Riya kept her temper, smiled and paid by phone; her friend Kabir lost his temper and argued for ten minutes. Option b is right because a person who keeps his temper stays even-tempered and pleasant, which is nearer to being in a good mood than to hunger, saving energy or aloofness. Exam trap: keep tempts students toward 'to preserve one's energy', and temper sounds like temperament, but the idiom is specifically about not getting angry. Key note: none of the options says 'stay calm' exactly, and being in a good mood is not quite the same as controlling anger; b stands as the closest meaning.", "traps": {"a": "'To become hungry' has nothing to do with temper; English idioms for hunger include 'to feel peckish'.", "c": "'To preserve one's energy' is 'to save one's strength' or 'to pace oneself'; keep in this idiom refers to keeping control of anger, not saving energy.", "d": "'To be aloof from' means to keep one's distance or keep oneself to oneself; keeping one's temper is about staying calm, not withdrawing."}}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from", "concept": "The answer is a: projective tests grew out of Sigmund Freud's idea that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives that show themselves indirectly. Sigmund Freud (1896) described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his own unacceptable impulses to someone else. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious wishes, fears and conflicts shape everything we do, from dreams to slips of the tongue, and that direct questions cannot reach them because the person does not know them. Testing borrowed this logic. Give someone an ambiguous stimulus, such as an inkblot, a vague picture or an unfinished sentence, and he must impose his own meaning on it; in doing so he reveals his inner needs and conflicts. This is the projective hypothesis. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods, comparing them to an X-ray of personality. The best-known examples are Hermann Rorschach's inkblot test (1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935). Projective tests share an ambiguous stimulus, open responses, a disguised purpose and global interpretation. Example: shown a TAT card of a boy staring at a violin, Kabir tells a story about a son forced to practise by a strict father who later runs away from home; the psychologist notes a possible theme of conflict with authority. Option a is right because the core assumption of projective testing, that hidden unconscious motives leak into how we interpret ambiguity, comes directly from Freud's psychoanalytic view of unconscious motivation. Exam trap: Carl Jung did contribute a projective forerunner, the word association test (1910), so students pick b; but his theory of psychological types became the basis of an objective inventory, the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung's (1921) theory of psychological types (introversion, extraversion and four functions) inspired the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, an objective self-report inventory; his word association test was a forerunner of projective work, but not its origin.", "c": "Tests that do not rely on language are non-verbal or performance tests, such as the Army Beta (1917) and John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938); they aim to reduce cultural and educational bias, not to reveal unconscious motives.", "d": "This is a popular myth: Hermann Rorschach (1921) deliberately made and selected his inkblots, drawing on the popular inkblot game Klecksographie; projective tests did not begin with an accident."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of research?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'research is not a process' is false, because research is precisely a systematic process, so b is the statement that is not a characteristic of research. Research is a careful, organised search for new facts or for answers to a problem. Its characteristics are usually listed as follows. It is systematic: it follows a planned sequence of steps, from identifying a problem, reviewing the literature and framing hypotheses to designing the study, collecting and analysing data and reporting conclusions. It is a process: each step leads to the next, and the findings raise new questions, so research is cyclical rather than a one-off act. It is problem-oriented: it starts from a question or a gap in knowledge and aims to answer it. It is active and dynamic: the researcher goes out and gathers evidence rather than waiting for it. It is also empirical, objective, controlled, replicable and critical. Fred Kerlinger (1964) summed this up as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation. Example: Arjun notices that many hostel students sleep badly before exams (problem), reads earlier studies, predicts that night-time phone use worsens sleep (hypothesis), surveys 200 students, analyses the data and reports. His results prompt a new question about screen light, which starts the cycle again. Option b is right because it denies a defining feature: research is always a process, so 'research is not a process' cannot describe it. Exam trap: option d is a double negative. 'Research is not inactive' means research is active, which is true, so d is a characteristic; students who skim see the word not and wrongly pick it.", "traps": {"a": "True: research is systematic, following planned, orderly steps from problem to conclusion, so a is a genuine characteristic and not the answer.", "c": "True: research is problem-oriented, beginning with a question or a gap in knowledge that it tries to answer, so c is a characteristic.", "d": "True: 'not inactive' is a double negative meaning research is active and dynamic, with the researcher seeking out evidence, so d is a characteristic."}}, {"q": "Pia sees Arsi make a strong speech against legalized gambling. She learns that Arsi had free choice to make a speech on any issue she chose, and Pia knows that most of the students in Arsi's class are in favor of legalized gambling. Pia infers that Arsi truly is opposed to legalized gambling. This is most consistent with", "concept": "The answer is b: Pia's confident inference that Arsi truly opposes legalised gambling fits correspondent inference theory. Attribution theory asks how we explain other people's behaviour. Correspondent inference theory, from Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965), asks when we can infer that an act corresponds to a stable inner disposition, that is, that the person really is the way she acted. They proposed three main cues. Choice: freely chosen behaviour tells us more than forced behaviour. Non-common effects: if the chosen action has a distinct consequence that other options lacked, that consequence points to the motive. Low social desirability: behaviour that goes against what is expected or popular reveals more than behaviour anyone would show. Apply the cues. Arsi chose her topic freely, and she argued against the view held by most of her classmates, so her speech was unpopular in that setting. Both cues point to a correspondent inference: she really is against gambling. Harold Kelley's (1972) augmenting principle says the same: behaviour that occurs despite pressure against it is attributed more strongly to the person. Example: if Kabir praises the canteen food while the owner is listening, you learn little; if he criticises it to the owner's face, you infer that he truly dislikes it. Option b is right because free choice plus unpopular behaviour are the exact conditions under which the theory predicts a confident dispositional inference. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is the near miss. It means inferring a disposition even when the situation forced the behaviour, as in the assigned essays of Edward Jones and Victor Harris (1967); Arsi had free choice, so Pia's inference is justified, not an error.", "traps": {"a": "The self-fulfilling prophecy (Robert K. Merton, 1948) is an expectation that makes itself come true by changing behaviour; Pia's inference does not change Arsi's behaviour, it only explains it.", "c": "The fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977) is over-attributing behaviour to disposition while ignoring situational pressure; Arsi chose freely and spoke against the majority, so the dispositional inference is justified, not an error.", "d": "The false-consensus effect (Lee Ross, David Greene and Pamela House, 1977) is overestimating how many people share our own views; Pia is judging Arsi's attitude, not projecting her own onto others."}}, {"q": "Counterconditioning is an exposure therapy technique which involves:", "concept": "The answer is c: counterconditioning teaches the client that a feared event or situation is no longer threatening, by linking it with a new response that is incompatible with fear. Counterconditioning comes from classical conditioning. A fear is a conditioned response: a once-neutral stimulus, such as a dog or a lift, has been paired with something frightening and now triggers anxiety. In counterconditioning the feared stimulus is paired instead with a pleasant or calming response that cannot coexist with fear, such as relaxation or eating. As the new association grows, the stimulus stops signalling danger and comes to evoke calm. Mary Cover Jones (1924) gave the classic demonstration: she brought a rabbit gradually closer to Peter, a boy of nearly three, while he ate food he liked, until he could play with it. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built the idea into systematic desensitisation and called the principle reciprocal inhibition: deep muscle relaxation is paired with each step of a graded fear hierarchy, from least to most frightening. Exposure is part of the method, but the extra ingredient is the competing response. Example: Riya panics in lifts. Her therapist teaches her progressive muscle relaxation and then, while she stays relaxed, has her imagine looking at a lift, then standing in one, then riding one floor, before practising in real lifts. Over sessions the lift becomes linked with calm, and Riya learns that it is no longer a threat. Option c is right because the end point of counterconditioning is new learning that the previously feared event is safe. Exam trap: b, exposing the client to the feared stimulus, is true of every exposure therapy, including flooding, so it does not capture what is special about counterconditioning, which is the replacement of fear by an incompatible response.", "traps": {"a": "Learning that one negative event is linked to another describes how fears are acquired through classical conditioning, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's (1920) Little Albert; counterconditioning reverses such learning.", "b": "Exposure to the feared stimulus is the shared method of all exposure therapies, including flooding and implosion (Thomas Stampfl, 1967); it names the procedure, not what counterconditioning teaches.", "d": "Helping clients see that their behaviour is counterproductive is a cognitive or insight technique, as in Aaron Beck's (1976) cognitive therapy or motivational interviewing; it is not a conditioning procedure."}}, {"q": "One of the essential characteristics of research is", "concept": "The answer is a: replicability, the ability of a study to be repeated with the same results, is taken here as the essential characteristic of research. Replicability means that another researcher, following the same method with a similar sample, can repeat the study and should obtain similar findings. It requires that procedures, measures and analyses are described clearly enough to be copied. It matters because a single result can be a fluke of chance, of a particular sample or of the researcher's own expectations; only a finding that survives repetition becomes accepted knowledge. Replication is science's main tool of self-correction and the practical check on objectivity: if a result depends on who ran the study, it will not replicate. Fred Kerlinger (1964) defined research as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation, and replicability follows from all four. The Open Science Collaboration (2015), led by Brian Nosek, repeated 100 published psychology studies: about 97% of the originals had significant results, but only about 36% of the repetitions did, which showed how seriously the field must take replication. Example: Meera reports that a brief gratitude diary lifted mood in 60 college students in Chennai. Her finding becomes credible only when Kabir, following her published procedure with students in Kolkata, finds a similar effect. Option a is right because replicability turns one investigator's result into public, verifiable knowledge, and it separates research from one-off observation. Exam trap: generalisability is the near miss; it concerns whether findings extend to other people and settings (external validity), which is desirable but not essential, since a deliberately limited study can still be good research. Key note: objectivity (d) is also listed as a basic characteristic of research in most methods texts, so d has support; replicability is preferred because it is the public test of objectivity itself, and the key used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Generalisability is how far findings extend to other people, places and times (external validity); it is valued, but many sound studies are deliberately limited in scope.", "c": "Usability, whether findings can be applied in practice, matters in applied research, but basic research can be valuable without immediate use, so it is not essential.", "d": "Objectivity, freedom from personal bias, is also a feature of good research; in practice it is checked by replication, which is why replicability is preferred here."}}, {"q": "Life time prevalence rate of OCD is", "concept": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is about 2.5%, the figure given in DSM-IV-TR (2000). Prevalence is the proportion of a population that has a disorder: point prevalence on a given day, period prevalence (such as 12-month) over a stated period, and lifetime prevalence at any time in life. Incidence, by contrast, counts only new cases in a period. Lifetime figures are always higher than 12-month figures, because they include people who have recovered. OCD is marked by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety, such as fear of contamination) and compulsions (repeated acts or mental rituals, such as washing or checking, done to reduce that anxiety), which take more than an hour a day or cause distress or impairment. DSM-IV-TR gave a lifetime prevalence of 2.5% and a 1-year prevalence of 0.5% to 2.1% in adults. The US National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) found 2.3% lifetime and 1.2% over 12 months. DSM-5 (2013) reports a 12-month prevalence of 1.2% in the United States and 1.1% to 1.8% internationally. Onset is usually in adolescence or early adulthood (mean about 19.5 years), earlier in boys. Example: in a town of 10,000 adults, about 250 people would have had OCD at some point in their lives, but only about 120 in the past year; Meera, a psychologist planning a clinic there, should use the 12-month figure for expected caseload. Option a is right because 2.5% is the standard lifetime estimate, and it agrees closely with the 2.3% found in the largest US survey. Exam trap: students confuse lifetime with 12-month prevalence and pick 1% or less; the roughly 1% figure is the 12-month rate. Key note: the marked copy of this paper highlights d (less than 1%), which is wrong for lifetime prevalence, so a is used here.", "traps": {"b": "About 1% (1.2% in DSM-5) is the 12-month prevalence of OCD, not its lifetime prevalence; lifetime figures are higher because they include past cases.", "c": "4.5% overstates OCD's lifetime prevalence; it is closer to the lifetime rate of panic disorder (about 4.7% in the National Comorbidity Survey Replication).", "d": "Less than 1% sits at the very bottom of old 1-year estimates and far below the 2.3% to 2.5% lifetime figures, so it cannot be the lifetime rate."}}, {"q": "Faulty learning involves:", "concept": "The answer is d: faulty learning, the behavioural account of how disorders are acquired, is read broadly here, so all three routes count. The behavioural model holds that most abnormal behaviour is learned in the same way as normal behaviour, through classical conditioning, operant conditioning and observation; disorder reflects faulty (maladaptive) learning rather than disease or unconscious conflict. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) showed the classical route: Little Albert learned to fear a white rat after it was paired with a frightening noise, an exposure to an aversive stimulus. Operant learning adds the reinforcement of unhelpful behaviour, such as avoidance that relieves anxiety, and Albert Bandura (1977) added learning by watching others. Faulty learning also covers failing to learn. A child who misses school misses chances to acquire academic and social skills and may learn that avoidance pays, as in school refusal. A child who is repeatedly criticised learns a negative view of himself, low self-esteem, which then becomes a vulnerability to depression and anxiety. Example: Arjun was bitten by a dog at six and has avoided dogs ever since (aversive conditioning); he skipped school for months after being bullied, so he never learned to handle his peers; and constant comparison with his cousin taught him that he was worthless. Each is part of his learning history. Option d is right because, on the broad behavioural reading, all three are ways in which disorders can be acquired through experience and learning. Exam trap: students treat faulty learning as classical conditioning alone and choose c, overlooking avoidance learning and missed learning. Key note: c is the most direct example of faulty learning, since a and b name circumstances or traits rather than learning processes, so c has real support; the key used here takes the broad reading, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Poor school attendance is one route the broad view includes (missed skill learning and rewarded avoidance), but it is only one example, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "Low self-esteem is usually a product of learning, such as repeated criticism, that then raises vulnerability; it is one example, so b alone is incomplete.", "c": "Exposure to aversive stimuli is the classic conditioning route (Watson and Rayner's Little Albert) and the most direct example, but on the broad reading it is still only one of the three, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which of the following bodily patterns does not typically accompany dreaming activity?", "concept": "The answer is d: higher general muscle activity does not accompany dreaming; during REM sleep, when most vivid dreams occur, the major muscles are almost paralysed. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and found that people woken from it usually reported vivid dreams. REM is called paradoxical sleep because the brain looks awake while the body is limp. Its features are: (a) a distinctive EEG of fast, low-amplitude, desynchronised waves like waking, with sawtooth waves; (b) rapid, jerky eye movements under closed lids; (c) high brain activity, with cerebral blood flow and metabolism at or above waking levels in limbic and visual areas; plus irregular heart rate and breathing and genital arousal. The remaining feature is atonia: circuits in the pons inhibit the spinal motor neurons, so muscle tone (EMG) in the postural muscles is at its lowest of the night, apart from small twitches. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with small lesions in the pons entered REM without atonia and acted out apparent dreams, stalking and pouncing. In humans this failure is REM sleep behaviour disorder, described by Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986). Example: in a sleep lab, Kabir's recording at 4 a.m. shows waking-like EEG, darting eyes and a flat chin EMG; woken, he describes a vivid dream of running for a train, yet his legs never moved. Option d is right because dreaming in REM is accompanied by low, not high, muscle activity. Exam trap: option c is garbled ('cerebral blood level' for cerebral blood flow), which makes students pick it as the odd one out; but blood flow to the brain really is high in REM, so c does accompany dreaming.", "traps": {"a": "A distinctive EEG does accompany dreaming: REM shows fast, low-voltage, desynchronised waves with sawtooth waves, so a is true and cannot be the answer.", "b": "Rapid eye movements are the defining sign of REM sleep (Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman, 1953), when most vivid dreams occur, so b is true.", "c": "Cerebral blood flow and brain metabolism are high during REM, close to or above waking levels, so c (despite its garbled wording) does accompany dreaming."}}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "concept": "The answer is a: Aaron Beck's cognitive triad is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Aaron Temkin Beck (1967), a psychiatrist working with depressed patients, noticed that their thinking was coloured by a steady stream of automatic thoughts with the same three themes. The self is seen as defective or worthless (\"I am a failure\"). The world, or current experience, is seen as unfair, demanding and full of obstacles (\"nothing goes right for me; people let me down\"). The future is seen as hopeless (\"it will always be like this\"). Beck argued that these views come from deeper schemas formed in childhood, and that cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising and selective abstraction keep them going. He built cognitive therapy around testing these thoughts against evidence, set out in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (Beck and colleagues, 1979). Hopelessness, the future corner, is the part most linked to suicide risk, which is why Beck also built the Beck Hopelessness Scale (1974). Example: after failing one exam, Meera thinks \"I am stupid\" (self), \"the teachers have always been against me\" (world) and \"I will never get into any college\" (future); her therapist would ask her to list the evidence for and against each thought. Option a is right because it lists exactly Beck's three domains: self, world and future. Exam trap: the distractors swap one corner for a plausible word. \"Others\" is not a separate corner, because other people fall inside \"the world\", and \"past, present, future\" is a time line, not the triad. Do not confuse the triad with the three dimensions of attributional style (internal, stable, global) from Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978), or with the CBT triangle of thoughts, feelings and behaviours.", "traps": {"b": "Self, world and others counts the social world twice (other people are part of the world) and drops the future, the hopelessness corner that Aaron Beck tied most closely to depression and suicide risk.", "c": "Self, others and future replaces the world with others; Aaron Beck's second corner is the world or ongoing experience as a whole, not relationships alone.", "d": "Past, present and future is a time line, not Aaron Beck's triad; the triad is about content (self, world, future), and the past is not one of its corners."}}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language", "concept": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be studied at several levels. Phonology deals with sounds, the smallest unit being the phoneme (/p/ versus /b/ in pat and bat). Morphology deals with the morpheme, the smallest unit of meaning (\"cats\" has two: cat and -s). Syntax deals with the rules for combining words into sentences. Semantics deals with meaning: what words refer to, how word meanings relate (synonyms, opposites, categories) and how the meaning of a sentence is built from the meanings of its parts. Pragmatics, the last level, deals with how language is used in social context (\"Can you pass the salt?\" is a request, not a question about ability). The French linguist Michel Breal (1897) set out the field in his Essai de sémantique. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed how separate syntax and semantics are with his sentence \"Colourless green ideas sleep furiously\": the grammar is perfect, but the meaning is nonsense. In memory research, semantic memory, named by Endel Tulving (1972), is our store of general knowledge and word meanings, which is why the same root appears there. Example: when Kabir hears \"Riya sat on the bank\" and has to decide whether she sat by a river or at a building where money is kept, he is solving a semantic problem, since the words and grammar are clear and only the meaning is in doubt. Option c is right because semantics is, by definition, the branch of linguistics concerned with meaning. Exam trap: linguistics is the whole science of language, so it is the parent field, not the specific study of meaning; and syntax is about word order and grammar, not meaning. Anchor each term to its level: phonology is sound, syntax is form, semantics is meaning, pragmatics is use.", "traps": {"a": "Linguistics is the scientific study of language as a whole (sounds, words, grammar, meaning and use); semantics is only one branch of it, so linguistics is too broad for this stem.", "b": "Encoding is a memory process, turning incoming information into a form that can be stored, as in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968); it is not a branch of language study.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into phrases and sentences (word order and grammar), studied famously by Noam Chomsky (1957); a sentence can be syntactically perfect and still meaningless."}}, {"q": "Type A and Type B Personality are associated with which personality inventory?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Jenkins Activity Survey is the inventory built to classify people as Type A or Type B. The story starts with two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were impatient, competitive and always racing the clock. They called this the Type A behaviour pattern: hard-driving competitiveness, time urgency and hostility. The relaxed, easy-going opposite was Type B. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 employed men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later work found hostility to be the most harmful part. Friedman and Rosenman first measured the pattern with a Structured Interview, in which the interviewer deliberately provokes impatience and watches speech and gestures. Because interviews are slow and need trained raters, C. David Jenkins, working with Rosenman and Friedman (1967), developed a self-report questionnaire, the Jenkins Activity Survey (JAS). Its standard form (Jenkins, Stephen Zyzanski and Rosenman, 1979) has about 50 multiple-choice items and gives a Type A score plus three factors: speed and impatience, job involvement, and hard-driving competitiveness. Example: Arjun, a bank manager who eats lunch at his desk, finishes other people's sentences and hates queues, would score high on speed and impatience and be classed as Type A. Option b is right because the JAS was designed specifically to measure the Type A and Type B pattern. Exam trap: the word \"type\" pulls students toward the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, but its types come from Carl Jung's (1921) psychological types, not coronary-prone behaviour. Also do not confuse Type A with Type D (distressed: negative affectivity plus social inhibition) from Johan Denollet (1996).", "traps": {"a": "The Self-Directed Search of John L. Holland (1971) is a self-scored career interest inventory that gives a three-letter RIASEC code (Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising, Conventional); it measures vocational interests, not coronary-prone behaviour.", "c": "The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator of Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers (1943) sorts people into 16 types from Carl Jung's theory (extraversion-introversion, sensing-intuition, thinking-feeling, judging-perceiving); the shared word \"type\" is its only link to Type A.", "d": "The MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) is a broad clinical inventory of psychopathology; it later gained a Type A content scale among its 15 content scales, but it was not built to classify Type A and Type B, which is the purpose of the JAS."}}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by", "concept": "The answer is a: Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon developed the first practical intelligence test, the Binet-Simon Scale of 1905. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify schoolchildren who would not benefit from ordinary teaching and needed special classes. Earlier \"mental tests\" by Francis Galton (1884) in London and James McKeen Cattell (1890) in America had measured reaction time, grip strength and sensory sharpness, and these turned out to have little to do with school success. Alfred Binet (1905) and his collaborator Theodore Simon took a different route: they tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most normal children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911. William Stern (1912) then proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University multiplied this by 100 to give the ratio IQ in the Stanford-Binet. Example: if eight-year-old Riya passes the items that typical ten-year-olds pass, her mental age is 10 and her ratio IQ is 10 / 8 × 100 = 125. Option a is right because the Binet-Simon Scale (1905) is universally credited as the first intelligence test in the modern sense. Exam trap: \"Stanford and Binet\" turns a university into a person; the Stanford-Binet is Terman's later American revision, not the first test. Also separate the first mental test (Cattell's term, 1890, for sensory and reaction-time measures) from the first intelligence test (Binet and Simon, 1905).", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman (1904) is a factor theorist: he found that school marks correlate positively and proposed the general factor g (two-factor theory); he built a theory of intelligence, not the first test.", "c": "There was no person called Stanford; the Stanford-Binet (1916) is Lewis Terman's revision of the Binet-Simon scale at Stanford University, which made the ratio IQ famous eleven years after the first test.", "d": "Lewis Terman (1916) revised Binet's scale and David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue with the deviation IQ; both came after Binet and Simon and never worked together on a first test."}}, {"q": "Find the odd man out: 3, 5, 11, 14, 17, 21", "concept": "The answer is c: 14 is the odd man out because it is the only even number in the series. Test each possible rule and count how many numbers it leaves out. Step 1, odd or even: 3, 5, 11, 17 and 21 are all odd; 14 is even (14 = 2 × 7). This rule leaves out exactly one number. Step 2, primes: 3, 5, 11 and 17 are prime, but 14 (2 × 7) and 21 (3 × 7) are both not prime, so this rule leaves out two numbers and cannot give a single answer. Step 3, differences: 2, 6, 3, 3 and 4 show no regular pattern, so no series rule applies. Option c is right because 14 is the single number that breaks the rule all the others follow. Exam trap: noticing that 21 is not prime tempts you towards option a, but a rule that excludes two numbers cannot define a unique odd one out; always check that your rule picks out exactly one term.", "traps": {"a": "21 is odd like 3, 5, 11 and 17; it is not prime (3 × 7), but neither is 14, so the prime rule would leave out two numbers and cannot make 21 the unique odd one.", "b": "17 is odd and also prime, so it fits both the odd-number rule and the prime rule followed by most of the series.", "d": "3 is odd and prime, so it fits the pattern of the series rather than breaking it."}}, {"q": "Pick out the most effective word(s) from the given words to fill in the blank to make the sentence meaningfully complete. Fate smiles ______ those who untiringly grapple with stark realities of life.", "concept": "The answer is c: the correct sentence is \"Fate smiles on those who untiringly grapple with stark realities of life\", because \"smile on\" (or \"smile upon\") is the fixed English expression meaning to look on someone with favour or to bring them good luck. \"Smile at\" someone is the literal act of smiling towards a person. \"Smile on\" or \"smile upon\" someone is figurative and is usually used with luck, fate, fortune or the gods as the subject: \"Fortune smiled on Arjun when his last-minute application was accepted.\" The stem says that luck favours people who keep struggling with hard facts, so this sense of favour is needed. Option c is right because \"smile on\" is the only one of the four that expresses being favoured by fate. Exam trap: \"smile with\" sounds natural because people smile with joy or with their eyes, but it describes the manner of smiling, not favour shown to someone; \"over\" and \"round\" do not form this idiom at all.", "traps": {"a": "\"Smile with\" describes how or in whose company one smiles (smile with relief, smile with a friend); it does not carry the sense of fate favouring someone.", "b": "\"Smile over\" is not the idiom; at most it means smiling about something (smiling over an old joke), and it never means that fate favours a person.", "d": "\"Smile round\" is not idiomatic English; \"round\" signals movement or position (look round, turn round) and has no link to favour or luck."}}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is used mainly as a test of attention, in particular selective attention and the inhibition of an automatic response. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people to name the colour of the ink in which colour words were printed. When the word and the ink conflict (the word RED printed in green), naming the ink colour is much slower and more error-prone than naming plain colour patches. The reason is that reading is automatic and well practised, while naming ink colours is controlled and effortful; the automatic reading response pops up and must be suppressed. This slowing is the Stroop interference effect. The clinical version by Charles Golden (1978) has three pages (reading colour words printed in black, naming colour patches, and naming the ink of conflicting colour words), each timed for 45 seconds; the interference score compares the third page with the first two. Neuropsychologists use it to measure selective attention, response inhibition and executive control, linked to the anterior cingulate cortex and the frontal lobes, and it is sensitive to ADHD, frontal injury and schizophrenia. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads colour words quickly but stumbles and corrects himself again and again on the conflicting page, a sign that he cannot hold back the automatic reading response. Option d is right because the task demands attending to one feature (ink colour) while ignoring another (the word), which is the core of selective attention. Exam trap: the test uses words and colours, so verbal fluency or visual perception look plausible, but a person with perfect reading and colour vision can still fail it. It is often called a test of executive function; in a list without that term, choose attention.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal fluency is the ability to produce many words quickly under a rule (for example, words beginning with F, A or S in one minute each); the Stroop task asks for one colour name per item and measures interference, not word generation.", "b": "Visual perception covers recognising and organising what is seen; the Stroop task assumes normal colour vision and reading and measures how well a person can ignore the word, so it is not a perception test.", "c": "Memory tests such as the Wechsler Memory Scale of David Wechsler (1945) ask a person to learn and later recall material; the Stroop task has no learning or delayed recall phase, since every stimulus stays in view."}}, {"q": "The correct sequence of stages of research is", "concept": "The answer is c: research moves from selecting a problem to stating a hypothesis, then collecting data and finally analysing them. This is the logic of the scientific (hypothetico-deductive) method. You cannot form a hypothesis until you know what problem you are studying, and you cannot collect meaningful data until the hypothesis tells you what to measure and in whom. Analysis comes last, because there is nothing to analyse until data exist. C. R. Kothari (1985), in Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, lays out the full flow: formulating the research problem, an extensive literature survey, developing the hypotheses, preparing the research design, deciding the sample design, collecting data, executing the project, analysing data, testing hypotheses, generalisation and interpretation, and preparing the report. Example: Arjun notices that his hostel mates seem anxious before exams (problem selection); after reading the literature he predicts that students who sleep less than six hours will report higher test anxiety (hypothesis); he gives a sleep diary and an anxiety scale to 200 students (data collection); then he runs a t-test comparing short and normal sleepers (data analysis). Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps all four steps in their logical order. Exam trap: the distractors use the same words but shuffle their order, so read every position; option a looks short and tidy but puts the hypothesis after the data and leaves out analysis altogether. In qualitative, theory-building work such as grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967), ideas may emerge from the data, but the standard quantitative sequence is the one being tested here.", "traps": {"a": "This puts the hypothesis after data collection, which is backwards for hypothesis-testing research, and it also leaves out data analysis, so it is neither complete nor in order.", "b": "Data cannot be collected before a problem is selected, because the problem decides what data are needed; starting with data collection is aimless fact gathering.", "d": "A hypothesis is a tentative answer to a research problem, so it cannot come before the problem is selected; this option swaps the first two steps."}}, {"q": "Newborns", "concept": "The answer is a: newborns prefer to look at face-like images over images that contain the same facial features in a scrambled arrangement. Robert Fantz (1961) introduced the preferential looking method: two pictures are shown side by side and an observer times how long the baby looks at each. Infants from a few days to 6 months old looked longest at a face, less at patterns such as newsprint and a bull's-eye, and least at plain discs. Carolyn Goren, Merrill Sarty and Paul Wu (1975) went further with babies only minutes old: when a schematic face was moved slowly across their view, newborns turned their eyes and heads further to follow it than to follow a scrambled face made of the same eyes, nose and mouth, or a blank head shape. Mark Johnson and John Morton (1991) replicated this and proposed an inborn mechanism (CONSPEC) that orients newborns to the basic layout of two eyes above a mouth, later joined by learned face expertise (CONLERN). So it is the arrangement, not just the parts, that attracts the newborn. Example: when baby Meera, two days old, is shown a card with a simple face and another with the same features jumbled, she follows the face card further with her eyes. Option a is right because the preference for organised faces over scrambled facial elements is present from birth. Exam trap: the other options sound like early abilities but each is overstated. Newborn acuity is about 20/200 to 20/600, not 20/40; newborns do habituate, but slowly; and depth perception is demonstrated on the visual cliff only by crawling age.", "traps": {"b": "Newborns can habituate (looking falls as a picture repeats and recovers to a new one, the method Robert Fantz (1964) used), but it takes many trials and long looks and speeds up over the first months, so \"almost instantly\" is wrong.", "c": "Newborn visual acuity is about 20/200 to 20/600; 20/40 is far sharper than a newborn can see, and acuity approaches adult levels only over the first year or later.", "d": "Depth perception was shown by the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) in infants of crawling age, about 6 months; newborns lack binocular depth cues such as stereopsis, which appears at around 4 months."}}, {"q": "Separation anxiety occurs when the newborn is", "concept": "The answer is c: separation anxiety, distress when the main caregiver leaves, appears at about 8 months and peaks at about 13 to 15 months, so 8 to 15 months is the best answer. It needs two developments. First, the infant must have formed a specific attachment to one or a few people; in the Glasgow study of H. Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), specific attachments appeared at about 7 to 9 months. Second, the infant's memory must be good enough to notice that someone is absent and to expect them, which links to object permanence in the theory of Jean Piaget (1954). John Bowlby (1969) placed this in his phase of clear-cut attachment, from about 6 to 8 months to 18 to 24 months, when the baby protests the departure of the attachment figure and uses her as a secure base. Jerome Kagan and colleagues (1978) found the same curve in several very different cultures: protest rises from about 8 months, peaks around 13 to 15 months, then declines as toddlers learn that the caregiver comes back. Stranger anxiety, which Rene Spitz (1965) called eight-month anxiety, emerges a little earlier, at about 6 to 8 months. Example: at 11 months, Kabir plays happily while his mother is in the room, but cries the moment she walks out to the kitchen and calms once she returns. Option c is right because 8 to 15 months covers both the onset and the peak of separation protest. Exam trap: 1 to 2 months is the age of the social smile and 3 to 4 months of laughter, when babies still accept any caring adult; distress that stays excessive well beyond the toddler years is separation anxiety disorder, a DSM-5 diagnosis, not the normal milestone.", "traps": {"a": "1 to 2 months is the age of the social smile (about 6 to 10 weeks), in John Bowlby's preattachment phase; the baby has no specific attachment yet, so a departing caregiver causes no protest.", "b": "3 to 4 months is when laughter appears and the baby prefers familiar people but still accepts care from anyone (Bowlby's attachment-in-the-making phase); separation protest has not yet begun.", "d": "By 24 to 30 months separation protest is declining; Bowlby's goal-corrected partnership begins as the toddler understands the caregiver's plans and trusts that she will return."}}, {"q": "To teach a child to eat spaghetti, you would reinforce initial responses such as holding the fork and then increasingly closer approximations to the final response. The procedure is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: reinforcing first rough attempts and then responses that come closer and closer to the final behaviour is called shaping. Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner (1953) used shaping to build behaviours an animal would never emit on its own. To teach a rat to press a lever, the trainer first reinforces facing the lever, then approaching it, then touching it, and finally only pressing it. At each step the requirement for reinforcement is raised, so responses that were reinforced before are no longer enough: this is differential reinforcement. Shaping is an operant technique because it works through consequences, not through pairing stimuli. In therapy it is used to build speech in children with autism (first eye contact, then any sound, then a syllable, then the word), and it is part of everyday teaching. Example: Riya teaches her four-year-old nephew Kabir to eat spaghetti. At first she praises him just for holding the fork; once he does that easily, she praises him only for putting the fork into the spaghetti; later only for twirling it; and finally only for getting a mouthful in without help. Option c is right because shaping is the name of the whole procedure described in the stem. Exam trap: \"successive approximations\" (option b) describes how shaping works, the steps that get reinforced, so it looks correct; but the stem asks for the name of the procedure, and that name is shaping. Do not confuse shaping with chaining, which links several different, already learned responses into a sequence.", "traps": {"a": "Counterconditioning, used by Mary Cover Jones (1924) with Peter and the rabbit and later by Joseph Wolpe (1958), replaces a conditioned fear with an incompatible response such as eating or relaxation; it is classical, not the building of a new skill.", "b": "Successive approximations are the steps that get reinforced during shaping; the phrase describes the method's ingredients, while the procedure itself is called shaping.", "d": "Chaining links a series of separate responses so that each completed step becomes the cue for the next, often taught backwards from the last step; the stem describes one final response approached gradually, which is shaping."}}, {"q": "Which of the following expresses a correct sequence in cognitive development?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, mental representation appears first, at the end of infancy, then symbolic thought, then intuitive thought. Jean Piaget (1952) described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 or 12 onwards), passed through in an invariant order. The sensorimotor stage has six substages. In the last one, at about 18 to 24 months, the child can for the first time represent objects and actions in the mind instead of only acting on them; Piaget called this the beginning of thought, and it makes deferred imitation and full object permanence possible. The preoperational stage is then divided into two substages. In the symbolic function substage (about 2 to 4 years) the child lets one thing stand for another: words, pretend play and drawings. In the intuitive thought substage (about 4 to 7 years) the child asks endless \"why\" questions and reasons by intuition rather than logic, still unable to conserve or to explain answers. Example: at 20 months Arjun copies his father's shaving the next day (mental representation); at 3 he uses a banana as a phone (symbolic thought); at 5 he insists the taller glass holds more juice but cannot say why (intuitive thought). Option c is right because it is the only option that lists achievements in the order in which Piaget's child reaches them. Exam trap: each distractor mixes real Piagetian terms in the wrong positions. Voluntary movement and object permanence belong to infancy, so object permanence cannot follow intuitive thought; conservation is a concrete operational gain that cannot precede object permanence; and information processing is a separate theory, not a Piagetian stage.", "traps": {"a": "Voluntary (intentional) movement and object permanence are sensorimotor achievements of the first two years, while intuitive thought belongs to ages 4 to 7, so putting object permanence last reverses the order.", "b": "Concrete operations (7 to 11) come long after mental representation (about 18 to 24 months), and information processing is a separate approach modelled on the computer, not a step in Piaget's sequence.", "d": "Conservation is the hallmark of the concrete operational stage (7 to 11), so it cannot come before object permanence (infancy) or intuitive thought (4 to 7)."}}, {"q": "If two presynaptic neurons coincidentally stimulate a postsynaptic neuron, their combined effects on that neuron are described by a process called", "concept": "The answer is d: when inputs from two different presynaptic neurons reach a postsynaptic neuron at the same time and add together, the process is spatial summation. A neuron receives thousands of synapses on its dendrites and cell body. Each input produces a small, graded change called a postsynaptic potential: an excitatory one (EPSP) slightly depolarises the membrane and an inhibitory one (IPSP) slightly hyperpolarises it. One EPSP alone is usually too small to fire the neuron; an action potential follows only if the combined potentials at the axon hillock reach threshold. The adding up happens in two ways. In spatial summation, potentials from different locations (different synapses, usually from different neurons) arrive together and combine; an EPSP and an IPSP arriving together can cancel out. In temporal summation, potentials from the same synapse arrive in quick succession and pile up before the first has faded. Charles Sherrington (1906) inferred both from dog reflexes in The Integrative Action of the Nervous System: one weak pinch on the foot produced no withdrawal, but two weak pinches at nearby points at the same moment did (spatial), and so did repeated weak pinches at one point in quick succession (temporal). John Eccles (1952) later recorded these potentials directly with electrodes inside motor neurons. Example: neither neuron A nor neuron B alone can make Meera's motor neuron fire, but when both fire together their EPSPs add up and cross threshold. Option d is right because the inputs come from two neurons at once, which is summation across space. Exam trap: \"coincidentally\" means \"at the same time\", and the idea of time pulls students towards temporal summation; but temporal summation needs repeated input from one source.", "traps": {"a": "Soma summation is not a recognised term; the soma and axon hillock are where potentials are integrated, but the process is named by whether inputs differ in place (spatial) or in time (temporal).", "b": "Temporal summation is the adding up of potentials that arrive in rapid succession at the same synapse from one neuron; the stem describes two different neurons acting together.", "c": "Cholinergic describes neurons and synapses that use acetylcholine, as at the neuromuscular junction; it names a transmitter, not a way of combining postsynaptic potentials."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the highest level of cognitive ability?", "concept": "The answer is a: of the four options, evaluating is the highest level of cognitive ability in Bloom's taxonomy. Benjamin Bloom (1956) and his colleagues wrote the Taxonomy of Educational Objectives to give teachers a shared ladder of thinking skills. Its cognitive domain has six levels, from simplest to most complex: knowledge (recalling facts), comprehension (understanding and explaining), application (using knowledge in a new situation), analysis (breaking material into parts and seeing how they relate), synthesis (combining parts into a new whole) and evaluation (judging the value of ideas or work against criteria). Each level assumes the ones below it: you cannot judge an argument you cannot understand. Teachers use these levels to write learning objectives and to build a test blueprint, a grid of content against levels, so that a paper does not test memory alone. Lorin Anderson and David Krathwohl (2001) revised the taxonomy using verbs: remembering, understanding, applying, analysing, evaluating and creating. Example: Kabir reading a research article first recalls what a p-value is (remembering), explains it in his own words (understanding), computes one for his own data (applying), separates the study's design from its conclusions (analysing), and finally judges whether the conclusions are justified by the evidence (evaluating). Option a is right because evaluating sits above knowing, understanding and analysing in both versions of the taxonomy. Exam trap: in the 2001 revision creating sits above evaluating, so evaluating is not the very top there; but creating is not offered here, and in Bloom's original list evaluation is the summit. Knowing is the lowest level, so do not be misled by its sounding fundamental.", "traps": {"b": "Knowing (knowledge, renamed remembering in the 2001 revision) is the lowest level of Bloom's taxonomy: recalling facts, terms and definitions without needing to understand them.", "c": "Understanding (comprehension) is the second level: explaining, summarising or paraphrasing ideas; it sits well below evaluating.", "d": "Analysing is the fourth level: breaking material into parts and seeing how they connect; it is a higher-order skill but still sits below evaluating in both the 1956 and 2001 versions."}}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called", "concept": "The answer is d: memories that influence behaviour without conscious awareness are implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) introduced the pair of terms. Explicit memory is conscious, intentional recollection, tested by recall and recognition (\"What did you have for breakfast?\"). Implicit memory shows itself only in performance: past experience changes how a person acts, feels or thinks, even though the person does not remember the experience or is unaware that it is having an effect. Its main forms are procedural memory (skills such as riding a bicycle or typing), priming (a word seen earlier is completed or recognised faster later) and simple classical conditioning. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) drew a similar line between declarative knowing that and procedural knowing how. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Elizabeth Warrington and Lawrence Weiskrantz (1970) found that amnesic patients who could not recall a list of words still completed word fragments with those words about as well as healthy people. Henry Molaison (H.M.), studied by Brenda Milner (1962), improved at mirror drawing over three days while denying he had ever done the task. Example: Riya cannot tell you the order of the keys on her phone keypad, yet her thumbs type her friend's number in a second; the knowledge is there, but outside awareness. Option d is right because implicit memory is defined by the absence of conscious recollection. Exam trap: explicit memory is the exact opposite; \"proactive\" is borrowed from proactive interference (old learning disrupting new) or confused with prospective memory (remembering to do something in future); and \"reactive memory\" is not a standard memory system at all.", "traps": {"a": "Proactive is not a type of memory; it names proactive interference, in which older learning disrupts recall of newer material, and it is easily confused with prospective memory, remembering to carry out a future action.", "b": "Reactive memory is not a recognised memory system in cognitive psychology; it is a made-up distractor built to sound like proactive.", "c": "Explicit memory is the opposite: conscious, deliberate recollection of facts and events, tested by recall and recognition (Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter, 1985)."}}, {"q": "White matter is primarily composed of", "concept": "The answer is a: white matter is made up mainly of myelinated axons. When fresh brain or spinal cord is sliced, two tissues are visible. Grey matter consists mostly of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, synapses and glial cells; it is where information is processed. White matter consists mostly of axons wrapped in myelin, a fatty insulating sheath made by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system. The fat in myelin is what makes the tissue look white. Myelin allows saltatory conduction, in which the impulse jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next, so myelinated axons carry signals far faster than bare ones. White matter is therefore the brain's wiring: bundles of axons called tracts connect one area to another, the largest being the corpus callosum that joins the two hemispheres. The arrangement differs by region: in the cerebrum grey matter forms the outer cortex with white matter beneath, while in the spinal cord the butterfly-shaped grey matter lies inside and white matter surrounds it. Disease shows why white matter matters: in multiple sclerosis the immune system attacks myelin in the brain and spinal cord, slowing or blocking conduction. Example: Meera's MRI shows bright patches in the white matter near her ventricles; her neurologist explains that these are areas of damaged myelin, which is why signals to her legs are slowed. Option a is right because myelinated axons are the main component of white matter. Exam trap: dendrites are never myelinated, so any option with \"myelinated dendrites\" is impossible; and unmyelinated axons belong to grey matter, so options a and c differ by one word, which decides the answer.", "traps": {"b": "Dendrites receive input over short distances and are not myelinated, so myelinated dendrites do not exist; this option pairs a real sheath with the wrong part of the neuron.", "c": "Unmyelinated axons lack the fatty sheath that makes tissue look white; they belong to grey matter along with cell bodies and dendrites.", "d": "Dendrites, which are always unmyelinated, are part of grey matter; white matter is defined by its myelinated axons, not by dendrites."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements about the typical pattern of sleep cycle is false?", "concept": "The answer is c: the false statement is that we most often wake naturally from Stage 4 sleep; Stage 4 is the deepest sleep, the hardest to wake from, and it is mostly over by the second half of the night. The modern study of sleep began when Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, and William Dement and Kleitman (1957) described the cycle. Sleep begins with Stage 1, a light, drowsy transition with theta waves, then Stage 2 with sleep spindles and K-complexes, then Stages 3 and 4, slow-wave sleep with large delta waves (merged as N3 in the 2007 scoring rules). The sleeper then climbs back up and enters REM, the stage of vivid dreams and muscle atonia. Each cycle lasts about 90 minutes, giving four to six cycles a night. Slow-wave sleep dominates the early cycles and fades later, while REM periods lengthen towards morning. So the true statements are: a, sleep starts in Stage 1; b, infants spend more time in REM than adults (about half of a newborn's sleep against 20 to 25% in adults); and d, near the end of the night most time is spent in Stages 1, 2 and REM. We usually wake from REM or light sleep. Example: if Arjun's alarm rings an hour after he falls asleep, it drags him out of deep Stage 4 and he feels groggy and confused (sleep inertia); when he wakes by himself at dawn, it is usually from REM or Stage 2, and he often remembers a dream. Option c is right because it is the only false statement. Exam trap: in a \"false\" question the true statements look like plain facts, and the false one hides a single wrong stage name; always check where in the night each stage dominates.", "traps": {"a": "This is true: sleep normally begins with Stage 1 (N1), the brief drowsy transition with theta waves, before moving down to Stage 2 and deeper, so it cannot be the false statement.", "b": "This is true: about half of a newborn's sleep is REM, against 20 to 25% in adults, as Howard Roffwarg and colleagues (1966) showed, so it is not the false one.", "d": "This is true: slow-wave sleep is concentrated early in the night, so the last cycles consist mainly of Stages 1, 2 and lengthening REM periods."}}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to", "concept": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "traps": {"a": "Reducing inappropriate behaviour is the goal of behaviour-modification techniques such as extinction or response cost; ethical principles may have that effect, but their purpose is to guide all conduct.", "b": "Punishing inappropriate behaviour is the job of law or of disciplinary bodies that enforce a code after a breach; ethics itself sets out the standards before anyone acts.", "c": "Reinforcing appropriate behaviour is the operant language of B. F. Skinner (1938); ethics states what appropriate behaviour is but does not work by rewarding it."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following belongs to the category of homogenous data?", "concept": "The answer is d: a student population in a class is the best example of homogeneous data, because its members are alike on the main characteristics that matter. Data or a population are homogeneous when the units are similar to one another on the characteristics being studied, and heterogeneous when the units vary widely. Students in one class are usually of nearly the same age, have reached the same educational level, follow the same syllabus and are taught by the same teachers, so they form a fairly uniform group. By contrast, the houses in a colony differ in number of storeys, size, design and age even if all are multi-storeyed; the trees in a garden belong to different species and differ in height, age and shape; and highway traffic mixes cycles, two-wheelers, cars, buses and trucks moving at different speeds. Homogeneity matters for sampling. The more homogeneous a population, the smaller the sample needed to represent it, since any member is much like any other. With a heterogeneous population a larger sample is needed, or the population is divided into homogeneous subgroups (strata) and each is sampled separately: stratified random sampling, whose most precise allocation was worked out by Jerzy Neyman (1934). Example: if Kabir wants the average reading speed of the Class 8 students in one school, a small random sample of that class will do; to study reading speed across all ages in Kolkata, he would need a much larger, stratified sample. Option d is right because, of the four, only the students in a class share age, level and setting. Exam trap: \"multi-storeyed\" makes option a sound like a group defined by a common feature, but sharing one label does not make units homogeneous when they still differ widely on everything else.", "traps": {"a": "Multi-storeyed houses share one label but differ in number of storeys, size, design, age and value, so they form a heterogeneous collection.", "b": "Trees in a garden are usually of many species and differ in height, age and form, a typical heterogeneous population.", "c": "Highway traffic mixes cycles, two-wheelers, cars, buses and trucks of different sizes and speeds, the opposite of homogeneous data."}}, {"q": "A failure to develop a consistent identity results in", "concept": "The answer is c: in Erik Erikson's theory, failing to build a consistent identity in adolescence results in role confusion. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole life span. Each stage is a crisis, a turning point between a healthy and an unhealthy outcome, set by the demands society makes at that age. Stage 5, at about 12 to 18 years, is identity versus role confusion (also called identity diffusion). Faced with a changing body and new demands, the adolescent must pull childhood identifications, abilities, values and goals together into one continuous sense of who he or she is and where life is going. Success brings the virtue of fidelity, the ability to stay loyal to chosen values and people. Failure leaves a person unsure of who they are, drifting between roles and unable to commit to an occupation, beliefs or relationships. Erikson (1968) developed the idea in Identity: Youth and Crisis, describing a psychosocial moratorium, a socially allowed delay for exploring. James Marcia (1966) later turned it into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Example: at 17, Kabir switches from wanting to be an engineer to a musician to a cricketer within a month, and changes his views to match whichever friends he is with; he has no stable sense of self yet, the picture of role confusion. Option c is right because role confusion is Erikson's named negative pole of the identity stage. Exam trap: the other options are the negative outcomes of neighbouring stages, so all are real Erikson terms. Isolation is especially tempting because Erikson held that true intimacy needs a firm identity first, but isolation is the failure of the next stage, not of identity itself.", "traps": {"a": "Isolation is the negative pole of stage 6, intimacy versus isolation, in young adulthood (about 18 to 40); a weak identity makes intimacy harder, but isolation names that later failure.", "b": "Inferiority is the negative pole of stage 4, industry versus inferiority (about 6 to 12 years), when school-age children judge their competence against others.", "d": "Stagnation is the negative pole of stage 7, generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood (about 40 to 65), a failure to care for the next generation."}}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "concept": "The answer is c: the token economy is based on operant conditioning, in which behaviour is changed by its consequences. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely. A token economy applies this to a whole ward, classroom or home. Clearly defined target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework) earn tokens such as points, stars or plastic chips. The tokens are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers: they have no value in themselves but can be exchanged later for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because tokens can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken away for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) built the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois: long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work nearly stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Gordon Paul and Robert Lentz (1977) later found that a social learning programme built on tokens helped long-stay patients with schizophrenia more than milieu therapy did. Example: in a children's hostel, Arjun earns a star for each morning he is ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option c is right because the method rests on reinforcement contingencies, the core of operant conditioning. Exam trap: Skinner supplied the principles, but the token economy as a ward programme belongs to Ayllon and Azrin; and although tokens gain value by being paired with backup rewards, the technique as a whole is operant, not classical.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned one and underlies systematic desensitisation, flooding and aversion therapy; the token economy works through the consequences of voluntary behaviour, not stimulus pairing.", "b": "Implicit learning, studied by Arthur Reber (1967) with artificial grammars, is learning complex patterns without awareness; it is a cognitive phenomenon, not the basis of a reinforcement programme.", "d": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) offers unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness in a non-directive relationship; contingent rewards are the opposite of unconditional regard."}}, {"q": "Incidence of dementia in 65 years old is approx.", "concept": "The answer is a: the yearly incidence of dementia at about age 65 is roughly 0.7% to 1%, so 0.7% is the best answer. Two measures are easily mixed up. Incidence counts new cases arising during a period, usually one year, among people who did not have the disorder at the start. Prevalence counts all existing cases, old and new, at a point or over a period. For a long-lasting condition like dementia, prevalence is always much larger than yearly incidence, because cases build up over the years (prevalence is roughly incidence multiplied by average duration). Dementia is strongly tied to age. Pooled studies such as the meta-analysis by Anthony Jorm and Daniel Jolley (1998) show that incidence is below 1% a year at 65 to 69 and roughly doubles with every 5 to 6 years of age, reaching several per cent a year in the late 80s. Prevalence follows the same steep curve: about 5% of all people aged 65 and over have dementia, with Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), the commonest cause. Example: in a town with 10,000 people aged 65 to 69 who are free of dementia this year, about 70 to 100 will develop it over the next 12 months; that is incidence. If you counted everyone over 65 who already has dementia today, you would find a much larger share, about one in twenty; that is prevalence. Option a is right because 0.7% matches the yearly rate of new cases at around 65. Exam trap: the large figures (17%, 27%, 37%) are prevalence-sized numbers seen only in the very old, people in their late 80s and 90s, and even then they count existing cases, not new ones; check whether the stem says incidence or prevalence before choosing.", "traps": {"b": "17% is far too high for yearly new cases at 65; a figure of that size is close to the prevalence (existing cases) of dementia among people in their late 80s.", "c": "37% is a prevalence-sized figure found only in the oldest groups, around age 90 and above, not the yearly incidence at 65.", "d": "27% resembles the share of very old people who already have dementia (prevalence), not the proportion of 65-year-olds who develop it in a year."}}, {"q": "Type I error occurs when", "concept": "The answer is b: a Type I error is a false positive, concluding that an effect exists when in reality it does not. Hypothesis testing starts from the null hypothesis (H0), the claim of no effect or no difference. After collecting data, the researcher either rejects H0 or fails to reject it. Since the truth is unknown, two kinds of wrong decision are possible, as Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) set out. A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis: declaring a difference that is not really there, a false positive. Its probability is alpha, the significance level fixed before the study, usually .05, a 5% risk of a false alarm when H0 is true. A Type II error is failing to reject a false null hypothesis: missing a real effect, a false negative. Its probability is beta, and power (1 minus beta, conventionally at least .80) is the chance of detecting a real effect. The two errors trade off: a stricter alpha (say .01) lowers Type I errors but raises Type II errors unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Meera tests whether a yoga programme reduces anxiety. Her t-test gives p = .03, so she rejects H0 and reports that yoga works. If in truth the programme has no effect and her result came from chance, she has made a Type I error. If instead yoga really works but her small sample gives p = .20 and she concludes it does not, that is a Type II error. Option b is right because a false positive is the definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: think of the boy who cried wolf: the villagers who first believed him made a Type I error (a false alarm), and those who later ignored him when the wolf really came made a Type II error (a miss).", "traps": {"a": "A false negative, missing an effect that really exists, is a Type II error (failing to reject a false null hypothesis); its probability is beta.", "c": "Type I and Type II errors cannot both happen in one decision: a Type I error needs a true null that is rejected and a Type II error a false null that is retained, so they are mutually exclusive.", "d": "Option b correctly defines a Type I error, so \"none of the above\" cannot be right."}}, {"q": "In the early work of Hartshorne and May, designed to assess children's moral behavior, it was found that", "concept": "The answer is b: in the Character Education Inquiry, Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928 to 1930) found that children's honesty was largely situation-specific: the same child might cheat in one setting and be honest in another, even though both involved the same moral principle. Working at Teachers College, Columbia University, they asked whether children have a general trait of honesty. They gave more than 10,000 schoolchildren cleverly disguised chances to lie, cheat and steal in many settings: in class, at home, at parties and in athletic contests. For instance, children could secretly change answers on a test they marked themselves, or keep coins from a box. Almost every child cheated in some situations and not in others, and cheating in one situation predicted cheating in another only weakly. Knowing moral rules, or attending character education and Sunday school, barely predicted honest conduct. Hartshorne and May called this the doctrine of specificity: behaviour depends heavily on the particular situation, such as the risk of being caught and the effort needed. The study became a cornerstone of the critique of trait consistency by Walter Mischel (1968), which started the person-situation debate. Later, Roger Burton (1963) reanalysed the data and found a small general factor of honesty, and Seymour Epstein (1979) showed that behaviour averaged over many occasions is far more consistent. Example: Arjun copies answers in a class test when the teacher steps out, yet returns the extra fifty rupees a shopkeeper gives him by mistake. Option b is right because it states the inquiry's central finding of situational morality. Exam trap: \"always\" and \"never\" in a and c state the opposite of what the study found; moral behaviour was inconsistent across situations, not fixed.", "traps": {"a": "This is the opposite of the finding: no group of children kept to moral standards in every situation, and honesty in one setting only weakly predicted honesty in another.", "c": "Almost every child cheated somewhere when given a safe chance, so \"moral children never cheat\" contradicts the doctrine of specificity.", "d": "The inquiry's conclusion concerned situations, not sex, and it did not establish that boys have stronger consciences; the idea that one sex has a weaker conscience echoes Sigmund Freud (1925), not Hartshorne and May."}}, {"q": "The Whitehall study indicated an association between coronary heart disease and which of the following:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Whitehall studies of British civil servants linked coronary heart disease to low job control. The first Whitehall study, begun in 1967 with about 17,500 male civil servants in London, found a steep social gradient: men in the lowest employment grades, such as messengers and doorkeepers, were several times more likely to die of coronary heart disease than men in the top administrative grades, even though all had secure office jobs and free medical care. Smoking, blood pressure, cholesterol and obesity explained only part of this gap. Michael Marmot and colleagues then began Whitehall II in 1985 with about 10,000 men and women. In their 1997 paper in The Lancet, low job control (little say over how and when one's work is done, also called decision latitude) predicted new coronary heart disease independently of grade and the usual risk factors, and it accounted for a large part of the grade difference. High job demands did not show the same link. The idea draws on the job demand-control model of Robert Karasek (1979), which holds that the most stressful jobs combine high demands with low control. Example: Kabir, a junior clerk who must process files in the order and at the pace set by others, has lower job control than his section officer, who decides her own priorities; the study suggests that Kabir carries the higher heart risk. Option b is right because low job control was the named psychosocial predictor of heart disease in Whitehall. Exam trap: hostility and anger (the toxic part of the Type A pattern of Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, 1959) and depression are real heart-disease risk factors in other research, which makes c and d tempting; but the Whitehall finding concerns control at work.", "traps": {"a": "Lack of training for the job was not a Whitehall predictor; the study measured psychosocial work conditions such as control, demands and support, and control was the factor linked to heart disease.", "c": "Anger and hostility are linked to heart disease in Type A research (Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, 1959, and later hostility studies), but they were not the Whitehall finding, which concerned job control.", "d": "Depression does raise coronary risk in other studies, but the classic Whitehall result tied heart disease to low control at work, which explained much of the social gradient."}}]});
